        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>juice of the fruit is very strong in tannin, and so it is said a highly satisfactory ink, and shoe blacking can be obtained from it." As an article of diet, it is a whole larder in itself. Marmalade can be made from it, also a pleasant drink,something after the style oftider. Its admirers appear to be agreed that the banana is twenty-five times as nutritive as the ordinary loaf, and forty-four times as nutritive as the potato, while 9, plot of ground that would give 331 h. of wheat, or 991 b. of potatoes, would, so far as space is concerned give 4,0001 b. of bananas, with a fractional amount of the same trouble.</p>
        <p>About Holidays.—lf one would keep one's -.friends, there are two things one must never dot viz : never recommend one's dressmaker, or a place in which to take a holiday. Ideas as to the best manner of spending the yearly vacation are as varied as faces,no two people holding exactly the same views on the subject. And there is nothing a man or woman resents so much, as having been persurieed.to spend his or her one holiday in the year, in a fashion that has proved uncongenial. Fashionable Ailments.—Cures of all sorts and des- criptions have been the one topic of conversation lately and the great majority of society women have departed in search of one or other of them. The " rest " cure ris "Guests hot and dishes cold "is now the rule. There the most simple,but because this can almost be followed is no more delightful dish for a hot summer's day than anywhere, it is not so popular as those which take one a Brown and Poison Blanc Mange. It is made with " lI to some particular spot, far afield. The craze for the Brown and Poison's "Patent corn flour and sweet "mud "bath has somewhat subsided, and is now only milk. There is quality in this Blanc Mange, there is taken by some who really, and unfortunately, need to flavour,klaintiness, food value. Served with stewed undergo the treatment. For the comfort of intending fresh fruit it is s. cooling, appetizing and refreshing bathers, it may be mentioned that the mixture—coin- dish for a hot summer day. Recipes for serving corn posed of peaty, boggy turf, which contains stimulating flour and fruit in twenty-one different ways are given chemical properties and which often being carefully in Brown and Poison's booklet," Summer sweets,, be prepared,is mixed with the mineral waters of the locality, had for a Id. stamp from Brown and Poison, Paisley where it is used—is not adhesive, but leaves the skin • Send for it now, else you may forget. easily, under ,the warm douche, which proceeds the . cleansing bath. RUNCORN AND