could truthfully and her utter joy, Jim Farquhar had moved in and say that she had "other plans " ? It was a mean taken a room under the roof of Galbraith Barclay. thing in Gwynne, and when he saw Nell's growing "Parson Barclay," a God-fearing, God-serving pallor and the dark circles under her eyes, and the soldier and jentleman,the whole regiment honoured, lassitude and listlessness, and heard no more her even if they could not imitate. It meant that merry laughter and happy, birdlike song about the Jimmy meant to cut loose from "the boys," to save house, he could have kicked himself; for, in takina money and study. He had so told her the day the counsel with his wife on the subject before speak: squadron unsaddled from the winter's rest, and ing to Farquhar, he had discreetly concealed all now—and now. with hardly a month gone by, had thought of what was the most vital part of the he not gone, if her mother and others were to be statement he intended to make; and now he dare believed, from what might, have been bad to what not tell her, and strove to work himself into a must be infinitely wot se. had he not turned front compensatory passion of virtuous wrath over "that her to that—to tl at—she could not say the word. young scoundrel's" having ever presumed to make She could not turn back with out one lenging look love to his child when here he was now ‘_2_7‘aalivant- at the dim lisht in the window she well -knew to ing with that little hussy next door." UP to ths be his, the window of the r min where he lay in day of the Major's blunt warning, Jimmy Farquhar, fevered torment, where she—that othur woman— apparently, hadn't cared a rap for pretty Mrs. thst very day had sat by him, nursed him, called Welland, though he had known her well ats-Frayne. him Jimmy, as those townspeople said she did that The week after the black Friday of the Major's cruel day. Across the parade the chapel lights ultimatum, Jimmy was at wolfs/Id's quarters hours gleamed through the night. She could see the at a time, while that obese warrior played his black gionps of soldiers hovering about it, shewing solemn game at the club. And on this gloomy, that the little building must be packed within. The wintry day, they had actually gone sleighing walk em- ded abruptly at the fence along the icy together Mrs. Welland and hindsome Jim river, al d it was time to turn back. One more Farquhar—snugly tucked in with Welland's costly lingering look she gave at that window on the home- robes. Well—well—Welland! ward way, then horror! What was that. afar off Oh, of course Nell heard of it, though she was along the row? CI inching feet! Jovial voices! spared the sight of the start. People came in and Beyond doubt Dana, Frost, and some others of the conferred with Mrs. Gwynne in tones expressive of boys comina up to inquire for Jimmy. And they Much moral shock, and with bated breath and much would meet her, and know whither she had gone! significant glancing towards Nell ; and the child Not for worlds ! With the quickness of thought she knew what it all meant even before the visitors slipped through the gateway of Captain Greggs's departed, and, like a long, slim, big little fool, as quarters, scurried around in the shadows to the she called herself, six times had she gone to the back of the house, out at, the rear gste, and then, window and looked out towards town and seen czui chin through the icy-furrowed snow, hastened nothing. Now, just after gun-fire and the scolding s'i ng the line of the fence intending to regain the she had given herself, there came a sight that well front walk as soon as well past the intruders. But nigh drove her frantic. gate after gate was closed .. and barred. She Afar out over the snowy waste a black dot was remembered her father having given that order. spreading into a blotch--crawling swiftly towards Then she would have to go clear round the post her, -too, growing bigger and bigger every minute, and enter from the main gate at the