und a half-fainting girl at the spot dole with Welland and Mrs. Major with madam, three minutes later, and far out on the road rAn Both looked white, said the men, and black, said down a panting, staggerina man. the women. Many of these last-named ministering "It is the robber, and my daughter winged him," angels of the garrison had hastened, of course, to said the Major, as with ashen face the big Captain see what they could do for her, and found that burst his way through an excited group. very pretty little woman pallid, but unaccountably a Hush! for God's sake, Gwynne, " moaned composed, and even unevasive. To each woman Welland, t,lrn sank his. voice into a whisper. "It who entered, in turn she patiently told the story, is my son. They had gone to get certain packages at the express office. These were safely stowed in the sleigh. Mr. Farquhar, reins in hand, was just stepping in beside her when n shot was fired near them. The horses started violently, jerking Mr. Farquhar off his feet. Then away they tore, the reins over the side, and Jimmy Farquhar, hanging -on to them like grim death, was dragged head Fore- most over the icy road until knocled senseless against a rock. She was pitched into a drift, and —that was all. -i But it wasn't all. Farquhar's anti was broken, and there was, fear of concussion of the brain. Not until late at'night did the post surgeon return to Foster. "The boy's a mass of bruises," said he. "But wasn't it plucky ? He held On nearly half a mile." Not until two days later did they move Farquhar out to the post, and, meantime, there had been no end of—talk.</p>
        <p>. • • . . . . . And yet they had a merry Christmas, an excep- tionally merry Christmas at Foster that winter. A story, only half • suspected hitherto, came fully to light. At the bedside of a wounded prodigal, humbled and repentant, a father's heart had been poured forth in mingled gratitude and forgiveness. Frr years he had borne with the wayward Ind, his only son, first for love of the boy himself, then for the sake of the fond woman who had shielded him so long, and died praying for that boy's rYeimption. But the last pound camt... when Walland married a second time, and found that OA money his pretty young wife so frequently lymed of him "for kindred in distress,' really wet to his unworthy son. The boy's threats of fr+ide or of shooting his father had terrified her if*, acquiescence. Then Welland drove him frotn W 3 doors, and the boy followed them to Fravne, It'd there :Jim Farquhar shared purse and larder him o und .strove to make a man of him. and</p>
        <p>Interposed to shield her when the son repeated his demands. The father placed money for him in the hands of an agent, to be paid at stated intervals, and again sent him forth. But this December, violating his solemn word, here he was again at Foster dunning her, even while, declaring that if eke betrayed his presence he would kill her or hisa</p>
        <p>or both, and then make away with himself. She dared not tell Welland: she did tell Farquhar, as luck would have it, just at the time Major Gwynne had practically ordered the boy to keep aloof from his daughter. Then she saw that her young knight of the Frayne days was utterly unhappy, and she extracted his story. "Other plans, indeed!" said the little woman, in wrath. "I'll bring the Gwynnes to their senses." And just what mischief she might have done to her own affairs in mending Jimmy's, goodness only knows. Just in the nick of time that drink- maddened stepson of hers had dared to let drive a shot the day she and Farquhar went for the Christ- mas things she had ordered from the East, and so led to one blessing in disguise. Just in the nick of time, young Hopeful, despairing of getting money otherwise, bethought him of a scheme to rob his father's desk the night of the Bishop's visit, and ran foul of the dead shot among the Foster girls, to the end that he was brought to his knees and a realising sense of his iniquity. All Fostet_ without knowing all Welland's sins, knew that reconciliation, and even joy had come, that pretty Mrs. Welland, and Farquhar, and Nellie Gwynno were severally instrumental; end that: "Well, of course, we could only judge from what we saw, you know, and it did look very suspicious." But the night of the big post Christmas-tree and the children's party and the "grown-up" German, the doctor wouldn't let Jimmy dance, but did let him down to the ballroom, his arm in its splints, his heart in a flutter (and both under his blouse), for the Major greeted him as he never had before, and Nell looked up shyly when he begged her to "sit out" three dances with him, and austere Mrs. Gwynne looked away. r "I'll give you three—and a—square dance, if you like, Mr. .Farquhar," said the dead shot, with eyes that brimmed suspiciously and lips that couldn't keep from twitching, for had not Kitty Gregg told her only an hour before there wa,sn't hope of his being at the ball? "Give me—this—right now," murmured Jimmy, seizing with his left hand and twisting her soft, white arm within his own. She pulled her fin,re!s free from his, but not too suddenly, as he led her to a seat across the thronging room. "No, not here—there, just beyond the evergreen. Don't you remember? There's where you were sitting the night I came from Frayne a year Remember it? She could never forget ! It was her first "grown-up" party, and Jimmy Farquhar, the best waltzer in the regiment, liad danced with her a dozen times; and here he was now—and wounded, and a hero, and that wicked story, all —trash! _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Just beyond the big evergreen &amp;flag-draped door- way led to a little side room where tables were set for cards ; but it was too early in the evening yet. The managers were still receiving, the band had just crashed into the opening bars of the Girona Lancers, and busy subalterns in full-dress uniform were scurrying :Jaunt placing the couples for the dance. No time is wasted at an army ball; and one of those brisk, breezy young directors, darting out of the card-roow, collided with the splint side of Jimmy Farquhar, who, gazing down into the flushed, happy face so close to his shoulder, saw nothing else. "Good L--and, Jimmy! Have I hurt you? Oh, what can Ido?" wailed the lad, as he saw Farquhar's face turning ashen. "Nothing. Get out!" gasps Jimmy. "Yes, but don't run, Mr. Blunt," chirrups Mrs. Welland, appearine suddenly around the evergreen and dropping her escort's arm. "Walk demurely, and fetch a glass of water. Now, Nell, you take care of Jimmy. mount guard." No time for words—or explanations. A tearful, terrified little army girl, just within that portal, is clinging imploringly to the uninjured arm, and looking up into a very white, but brave young face. "It hurt you dreadfully, I know, Mr. Farquhar," she begins. "No," protests Jimmy, "only—the room's kind o' swimming round." "Oh-h!" shudders Nellie, nestling closer. "Oh, what can I do?" "Don't let him fall, Nell. Here comes the water," whispers, over her shoulder, the good angel at the doorway. "He'll be all right in a minute." There are chairs in abundance at the tables, but these are six feet away, and what might not happen if she were to let go, even a minute? Jim has closed his eyes a second. What can it mean but that he is faint and—and falling The long, white, slender, girlish arms are about him in an instant, and then, with astonishing ease, the hero revives, and the uninjured member in the blue sleeve twines rapturously about the slim waist, and the curly head is boved,.. and the lips that were pallid but a moment aoone, are buried in the soft, shimmering waves of her hair. The blushing, burning, bonny face is hiding on his shoulder, but their hearts are beating together like mad. "The water, Nell," says Mrs. Welland at th• doorway, passing a brimming glass, but not so much as a glance, behind her, and stoutly engaging all inquirers in brilliant conversation. Then 'Here comes your father, Nell! What idiots people are 1 Somebody's told him! I suppose I'll have to let Mm in." • Neil's head is uplifted only in the nick of time. Brimming eyes and flusl el cheeks tell their story as Farquhar faces the anxious incomer. The post commander steps under the flag and surveys the pretty tableau. One morue-nt of silence, and then: "Merry Christmas, Major!" stammers Jimmy. And so, after all, it was the doing of "that little buil" next door</p>
        <p>[TILE END, 1</p>
        <p>THE Hfff WE A.rlIEl AN TIIE DEATH RATE.</p>
        <p>The-Wce'h(s:s:-e-ather has had a tremendous effect on the St. Helens death-rate. For the past two weeks the rate is 30. (corrected, 29.8), which is very much higher than has been recorded for a long time. The rate is more than double that of last year, when the rate was 13.0 (corrected, 14.5). Of the 103 deaths registered, 41 are of children under one year, against 20 last year ; and the total deaths of children under five years is 71 of the 103. Diarrhea has been responsible for 26 deaths, measles (;, whooping cough 3, and scarlet fever 2 ; while there has only been one death and one new case of typhoid. The birth-rate is exceptionally low—namely, 28.9, against 36.8. Dr. Buchan reported to the Health Committee yesterday that 40 deaths of children under three years of age had resulted from zymotic diarrhcea and !Aber ,-!iarhmal diseases, and these deaths were due to improper feeding of infants.</p>
        <p>MUSTARD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Tussore silk has been much in vogue this summer and makes up into excellent and use- ful costumes relieved by silk embroidery or open work. It is a material which will stand any amount of hard wear and which does not readily soil nor crush. There is also a new kind of cotton material which bears a strong resemblance to the silk and makes up into pretty dresses which are also very inexpensive. A costume of this style for morning wear I recently admired was completed by a brown hat of coarse straw trimmed with softly shaded pansies, while a stock collar of violet silk finished the throat softened by. a little turn-over lace collar. The effect was charm- ing, for the same tones of colour were repeated in the accompanying sunshade. There are any number of dainty sunshades this season, but (Te lady recently startled all beholders by appearing with one in which broad stripes of red, white, and blue silk were repeated. One</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RMING BODICE,</head>
        <p>RMING BODICE,</p>
        <p>would easily recognise the bearer of such a noticeable item of dress, however far off they might be, and this is a doubtful advantage. Anything which is very pronounced in colour or style is apt to prove wearisome. Some of the prettiest morning sunshades are of grass lawn interspersed with broad bands of satin ribbon. Of course, the fussy chiffon parasoles are used for important occasions by those who can afford such luxuries. There are also some pretty lace covers by whose aid a last season's sunshade can be smartened up. The charming bodice shown in my first sketch is of fine ecru spotted muslin trimmed with a bordering of insertion. It is made in fichu fashion with double frills crossed in front and passed round to the back of the waist where the ends are tied into a smart little bow. The full sleeves have the upper part to match the frills on the bodice, while the under sleeve is of clear plain muslin finished at the wrists by a deep fall of lace. A little chemisette of lace fills up the space between the folds of the fichu. It is a very becoming mode for a slender figure, and any light, thin material would make up prettily in this fashion. Figured gauze and muslin are much used in the creation of fete dresses just now, but re- quire a very dainty lining to ensure success. Indeed, the more elaborate of these toilettes</p>
        <p>'RET'</p>
        <p>HAT,</p>
        <p>are 'rather costly, for they boast an interlining of chiffon which comes between the material and the silk and gives a very soft effect. Very charming also are the striped taffetas mousse- line made up with dainty ruchings of silk and appliques of silk or lace. The pretty hat sketched is of pale pink chip trimmed with one huge creamy rose and its buds, while a soft pale, green silk band of ribbon is drawn round the crown. It is a most becoming piece of headgear and could have narrow black velvet strings added if desired as these soften some faces very much, while others look nicer without any strings at, all. A flowing lace veil can always be added to a hot of this style gathered loosely round the brim so that it can easily be thrown back- wards from the face if desired. Net does not look well as a substitute for 'lace as it is not by any means suitable. Nearly every hat and toque nowadays boasts some sort of fall at the back whether it consists merely of a ribbon bow or some trails of flowers and foliage as in the long sweeping veil of lace. I saw a very charming hat the other day of a very deep cone of burnt straw trimmed with trails of laburnum, the effect being charming, while another of a deeper tone of brown boasted a trimming of wistaria. There is something</p>
        <p>A THREE-Q UARTER COAT.</p>
        <p>very artistic and picturesque in these flowers, and they can be arranged in so many different ways and are sometimes intermingled or veiled by a little old soft toned lace. A mushroom hat of the real old-fashioned style trimmed with a little ruche of plum-coloured silk which matched the gown it was worn with, was rendered more becoming by a bandeau of velvet which lifted it a little off the face of the wearer. My last sketch shows one of the most useful of this season's outdoor garments, namely, a three-quarter coat. Everywhere one sees these worn as everyone has recognised their value. The one depicted is made of cream serge to match the skirt it accompanies, and is trimmed with strappings of cream silk spotted with pale blue and piped with a narrow line of black. It is very - plain in design, and the sleeves are loose and roomy, allowing the coat to be easily and comfortably slipped over any kind of bodice. It is lined with thin pale blue silk. White and ecru linen coats are also greatly worn, the former with any kind of skirt, and the latter usually with a skirt to match. Bands of embroidery and rows of stitching together with lace generally of a coarse make ornament the coats and render them more dressy looking. Soft dull putty or pale grey cloth make an afternoon coat, and OR these .the embroidery is generally a good deal handsomer than that (seen on the linen coats.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE•</p>
