      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEKd</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEKd</p>
        <p>THE WAR IN THE FAR EAST.</p>
        <p>At the time of writing it seems as though 'the great crisis of the war in the Far East has been reached. For the moment even Port Arthur has been dwarfed by the magnitude and importance of the proceedings round Liaoyang, though there is little doubt that the Japanese, who have evolved all the strategy upon one concerted plan, will make n simultaneous attack on the great fortress. But the campaign has centred itself round liaoyang, and the eyes of the whole civilised -world are now focussed upon the little :\lanohurian town, which has assumed such undue importance under the circumstances. Despite the great heat the Japanese have followed up their success at Haicheng with -great dash and now practically surround .General Kuropatkin on three sides, and his Only line of retreat appears to be into Chinese territory, where an immense army Of 150,000 Chinese are waiting to enforce • disarmament, which is the strategic penalty of invading neutral territory. As far as can be gathered. General Kuropatkin seems to have</p>
        <p>'under him about 150,000 men, many of them more or less jaded by the heat and the hasty retreat. Against this army General Kuroki has a main body of ioo,ooo men, flushed with victory, with two wings of 50,000 each under Generals Oku and Nodzu. The Russian Commander-in-Chief is constantly being harrassed by orders and telegrams from St. Petersburg, and the Tsar appears to be doing his best to confuse and hamper his movements by mismanaging the campaign from the security of his palace. In conse- quence, General Kuropatkin, reproached with constantly retreating, has decided against his better judgment to make a great stand. He will personally take command of the Southern Portion of his army and attack General Nodzu, leaving General Kutchensky, of the loth Army Corps, and General Bilderling. of the 17th Army Corps, to resist General Kuroki's attack. If he suffers a great disaster at Liaoyang, it may mean that the end of the war is in sight, especially if Port Arthur should fall simultaneously.</p>
