      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>READING ELECTION RESULT.</head>
        <p>READING ELECTION RESULT.</p>
        <p>The polling on Saturday last at Reading has resulted in the return of Mr. Rufus Isaacs, the Liberal nominee, the figures read- ing at the declaration of the poll as follows: —Mr. Rufus Isaacs (Liberal), 4,770; Mr. C. E. Keyser (Conservative), 4,540; Liberal majority, 230. There was a heavy poll, go per cent, of the actual electors voting. Party colours were freely worn, motor-cars and carriages were plentiful on both sides, and the utmost good humour prevailed. It was the usual half-day at the biscuit factory and other works, and Messrs. Sutton and Sons closed their offices at one o'clock in Order that their employees might record their vote at their leisure for which candidate they thought the best fitted to represent the constituency. v. Both candidates addressed their supporters after the result had been declared, and whilst Mr. Isaacs said his return was a victory for Free Trade, Mr. Keyser complained that the election had been Sprung upon the constituency at a time likely to be most favoured to the Liberals. 11r. Rufus Daniel Isaacs is the second son 01</p>
        <p>. RUFUS ISAACS. 11r. josepn Michael Isaacs, a merchant in the city of London, and a nephew of Sir Henry Aaron Isaacs, who was Lord Mayor of London in 1889-90. The new member was iporn in London on October ioth, 1860, and !ducated at University College School, and in Brussels and Hanover, with the intention Of his following a commercial career. He "was, indeed, for some years on the Stock Exchange, but quitted that profession in order to study for the Bar. A pupil of Mr. Lawson Walton, K.C., he was called to the tar at the Middle Temple in November, 1887, joining the Norther Circuit. After Practising less than eleven years, .and obtain- ing one of the largest junior practices, he took silk in 1898, and since that period has been counsel in a large number of celebrated eases. Mr. Isaacs married, in 1887, Alice Edith, the third daughter of the late Mr. Albert Cohen, a merchant of the City of London. He is a member of the Reform, :National Liberal, Savage, and Ranelagh Clubs. Ile unsuccessfully contested North Kensing- ton at the last General Election against Mr. Thompson Sharpe.</p>
