      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LIVELY SCENES IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SCENES IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS.</p>
        <p>The scene in the Hole of Commons Vhich took place last Thursday over the befaulting Authorities (Education) Bill has only twice been paralleled—once when the Liberals followed Mr. Gladstone and once when the Con- ervatives fol- Vwed Mr. W. H. x•riith out of the House with- out vo tin g— all(' then only „Partially. The 'Welsh mem- I3ers in this in- st. -ance consid- 'eted theGovem- -1!? en t Bill a "Auld of Coercion et for Wales, :ncl were deter- rifled to resist et to the bitter ,tl,d. and con- iklerable feel- Was aroused '4l both sides. 41,,t last after CHAIRMAN tuur hours dis- -r:ssion over the opening lines Mr. Balfour °Posed to closure the four additional pages aznendments, and the division bell rang.</p>
        <p>........'</p>
        <p>COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The Unionists filed out into their lobby, as did the majority of the Liberals, but a small body determinedly kept their seats and refused to move in accordance with Parlia- mentary procedure. An angry scene occurred between Mr. J. W. Lowther, the Chairman of Committees, and the recalCitrant members, during which the House filled again. At length Mr. Lowther named Mr. Guest, Mr. Lloyd-George, Mr. McKenna, Mr. Alfred Davies, and Mr. Harewood. Then a Gil- bertian situation arose. Mr. Lowther had then in accordance with procedure to report them to the Speaker, but, the Speaker being away ill, it devolved upon him as Deputy- Speaker to fill the dual role and report them to himself! This he did with as much dignity as possible amidst considerable laughter, and then he ," named" to the House the fo!lowing members :—Mr. Guest, Mr. McKenna, Mr. Harwood, Mr. A. Davies, Mr. Lloyd-George, Sir A. Thomas. Mr. Moss, Mr. H. Lewis, Mr. Ainsworth, Mr. Brynmor Jones, Dr. Shipman, Mr. Levy, Mr. H. J. Wilson, Mr. Whitley. Mr. E. Griffith, Mr. Abraham, Mr. G. White, Mr. Philippe, Mr. W. Jones, and Mr. Herbert Roberts. Not wishing to proceed to extremes, after a consultation with the Premier Mr. Lowther resumed his seat as Chairman of Committees and requested the members who would not vote to leave the House. After a brief consultation, Mr. Asquith and Mr. Lloyd- George walked out followed by the rest of the Liberal party. Thus ended an amazing and unusual scene!</p>
