      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DEATH OF LADY TWEEDMOUTH.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF LADY TWEEDMOUTH.</p>
        <p>By the death of Lady Tweedmouth society has lost one of its leading hostesses and the Liberal party its chief entertainer. For many years at Brooke House she has held the near- est modern approach to a political salon in tows. and has gathered round her, in addi- tion to the poli- ticians of her</p>
        <p>own party, all the most cul- tured and intel- lectual people of the last quarter of a century. Her death, after a comparatively short illness, will leave a gap which it will be hard to fill. Last May Lady Tueedmouth was operated</p>
        <p>- ). • 4 .90 9 . rite, • ' ....c A --- dibi I -</p>
        <p>upon for. a can-</p>
        <p>ceroys growth,</p>
        <p>which -showed</p>
        <p>kr-s-rt)'' g</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LAD'</head>
        <p>LAD'</p>
        <p>TWEEDMOUTE</p>
        <p>signs of recru- descence the week before Ascot, when she learnt that she could not have long to live. However, she went on pluckily fulfilling her social engagements rather than disappoint others, and then she retired to her wild High- land home, Guisachan House, in Inverness- shire, in the knowledge that she could never hope to return: Lady Tweedmouth, who was only just over fifty years of age, was the third of six handsome and clever daughters of the seventh Duke of, Marlborough, who are all so well known in society. The others are Lady Wimborne, the Dowager Duchess of Rox- burghe, Lady de Ramsey, the Countess Howe, whose health has recently been giving great cause for anxiety, and Lady Sarah Wilson, of Mafeking fame. In 1872, when quite a girl, Lady Fanny ' Churchill married Mr. Edward Marjoribanks, and step by step she assisted him in his political career. Mr. Gladstone had a great opinion of her juagment, and she shone especially when her husband was Libe- ral Whip. He succeeded to the peerage in 1894, and since then Brooke House has been the scene of many brilliant entertainments, both political and social. Lady Tweedmouth was, amongst other things, a noted shot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DEATH OF A NOTED IRISH JUDGE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A NOTED IRISH JUDGE.</p>
        <p>One of the best-known figures in the legal and social life of Ireland has just passed away in the person of Judge O'Connor Morris. The learned judge who was in his eightieth year was prominent as a writer on Irish land questions, and frequently his re- marks from the bench have been brought under the notice of the House of Commons by the Irish party. He held strong views regarding the De Freyne tenants' struggle, and constantly impeached the conduct of the Congested Districts Board. From the start he ridiculed Mr. Wyndham's Land Act. He was one of the most versatile and the most independent judges on the Irish bench. He had been over thirty years a county court judge, and this year reached his jubilee in the law, having been called as a barrister in Ireland in 1854; and he has been known throughout his career as a man who said</p>
        <p>exactly what he thought. He was a keen observer of the social changes .in Ireland for half a century and more, and nothing struck him so strongly as the battle of the creeds. He often told of how he had been a guest at legal dinners where not another Protestant was to be seen at the long rows of tables ; and he said not long ago that he doubted if ever an Anglican bishop of Ireland had sat down with a bishop of the Roman Catholic Church in the drawing-room of a Dublin citizen. During his • career he managed to write at least a dozen books on Ireland, as</p>
        <p>well as countless articles in reviews and</p>
        <p>magazines. His . books include a survey 'of history for the last mo years and licgraphies• of Hannibal, . Napoleon, and Moltke.</p>
        <p>COWES REGATTA. The Cowes Regatta, which mas held last week, was hardly such a success as usual in many ways; and the racing was not very exciting, considering the standing of the re- gatta and the great and fashionable crowds it attracts, to say nothing of the cream of the yachts in *he country,and the prizes are not very valuable ournpared with those offered in other bran- ches of sport. However, it is .bne of the great soc.i alga herings of the year, and the King and Queen are most . loyal in their _patron-</p>
