        <p>fresh Council commissioners to be appointed. The Non- Traders wanted to see a certain tender. The Aa.roit : Don't you want anyone else there to watch you? Councillor PE.VIBMITJN : No. You can appoint three if you like. You can appoint thirty-three. Tne NIA.YOR said tea months ago there was a dead block in the proceedings. The commissioners could not agree. o.3tlrl3iliOr PENIMMT)N said the rel3oll forthat was the expense. Tne Non rraders kicked against it, and always had. Tnere had been an abominable waste of money on the inquiry, and in his opinion it was coun- selled by certain people in order to put an early end to the inquiry. The Non-traders would not go on onaccount of the expense. There had been more money spent on the inquiry than wis necessary. Surely they were en- titled to examine documents to make their report out. The evidence had been taken—they could not get away from the printed shorthand notes. The .\l3. von : I have never seen them. Councillor PEMBERTON : You have them in your possession, or the Town Jlerk has. . Tne : He has never shown them to me. I know nothing about them. CounciIIorPEMBERTON said he did not know until the morning of the last sittin,, that the inquiry was about to be closed. The procedure, we contended, was un- English. • Councillor EVANS said he was by no means satisfied with the short, abridged report of the Council's com- missioners, and he thought the ratepayers of the town were entitled to know all the findings of the commission —and know them in detail. (Hoar, hear.) The com- mission was not one of those things that could be easily passed over. Several men's reputations were at stake. Some wore in bondage waiting anxiously to se what the final decision should be. Therefore it was not a thing they could put aside lightly. Now they would be in a fresh difficulty ifj the Council's commissioners refused to serve further, but he thought that to conclude the inquiry now would not be right in the best interests of eeryboly Con-3ernel, and especially on the rate- payers' acceont, as they woald want a satisfactory report for their money. He thought the Council would be very ramis ;in their luty f they/did:not see the inquiry carried out to a proper end now they had gone so far. Tne Tee Cooncil's commissioners say emphatically they have finished altogether. Councillor EVANS said he hid spoken to one of the co rimissioners and told him th it the Council could not refuse to grant any evidence the Non-Traders might want. If they did they would only have a one-sided report, and that would not be fair to either the Non- Traders themselves or the people concerned. Tne NlA.vort : Oar con nissioners say they have made all the investigations they possibly can, and the Non-Traders have only sprung this last desire upon them. That is where the rub comes in.</p>
        <p>The MAYort: I did not know he had seen the private minutes.</p>
        <p>C)unaillor EVINS : Well, before this inquiry can be brought to a satisfactory issue we rnult have the investigations complete. That is certain, and it will ha fair to no one to have a one-sided finish.</p>
        <p>The NI ueram : 'Caere weall Seefn to be plenty of investigation, seeing that there are these 4,003 para- graphs of a. report. I cannot say what they are about, because I don't know. Councillor EvaNs : Exactly. I have no doubt they " will be the property of the ratepayers and of the mem- bers of the Council in time. . The 11tY0Ft : I don't sea it. Councillor EVINS : I do. In fact I shall on some . future occasion prepose that they he made public. (Hear, hear.) lam entitled to a.`copy, and every person in the borough who jaws rates is entitled to know just what they contain. (Haar, hear.) Councillor IVIAGINN : Charge 2s. Gd. for them. The NIAXOit : don't see the wisdom of publishing them at all. It might be the means of doing some one an injustice where it is not intended. Councillor EVANS : That is a question. T move that, in CASS theCcuncil's commissioners refuse to serve, the Mayor ask three other gentlemen to o:fieiate on our behalf. The NI kYOR : Wiere can I get the three wise men? Councillor EvAxs : Oh, you can get anything if you ;ask for it. • As this mltion did netaff ect th tt o f Conneillor D Ads, Councillor Evans agreed that the two should be put together as one. Alderman SAtEriILTR,S7 said he was opposed entirely, to the extension of time. The inquiry had been on quite sufficient for them to have found out all the faults, defects, and dishonesty in the borough of Warrington. The rep)rt of the Council's commissioners did not appear to give s utisfaction, but to show theui that the mutter had been gone into carefully he would give them one or two items. For instance, they have discovered : that the Arpley Che nicul Clme unv huh had trade with the Clrperation to the extent of ,C 7 is. lid. (Laughter.) A Vows: We hAVe read all that. • Alderm in SNIETEIU,RST I want to show you that ! this matter has been gone into thoroughly. Then Fair- dough's have traded to the extent of Hs. Bd.. during the last seven years. (Laughter.) _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PENIBERTON: I could tell from his speech that he had seen them, and that was why I did not want him to go on speaking. However, Warrington will hear our report, and hear it very effectually—(hear, hear)— and don't forget that. I am-- The MAYOR here interrupted, and Councillor PEMBERTON said he thought he had a right to speak, seeing that Alderman Smethurst had been allowed to go into details. The MAYOR: If he had no sense in going on with his statements it does not say you have not. (Laughter.) Councillor PF,MBERToN: That may oe flattering to me, Mr. Mayor, but it is not the point, and I wish to make the matter clear. Continuing, he said that he wanted the Council to understand that up to the last meeting but one of the commissioners it was understood that they were to go through the evidence, yet at the last meeting, to his entire surprise, there was a proposition that the inquiry be closed. He was thunderstruck, and was surprised that such a thing should have been put forward, seeing that the other side knew that Mr. Williams could not possibly attend, and that that being so they were in a majority. They therefore took the Non-Traders at a disadvantage, and closed the inquiry, instead of going through the evidence and trying, as far as possible, to agree on a joint report,.</p>
        <p>The l'ilA.voEt : Well, there is now a resolution allow- ing you to bring the matter to a proper finish. Councillor PENIBER,TON, continuing, said that Alder- man Smethurst had very happily taken a few figures,but he did not mention Buckley's with £5,430 ; and Rich- mond's, with £2,615. The Slate Company's £16,000 was direct trading with the Corporation alone, and had nothing to do with indirect trading. The inquiry was to go into " direct and indirect trading," and what they wanted now was time to go into the indirect trading. They were about to go into it when the inquiry was suddenly closed. What about the indirect trading in connection with the Police Station and Hill Warren reservoir?</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : That is legitimate business and he cannot get away from it. Norbury Williams acknowledges the same fact. The MAYOR: I will let him go on. I shall under- stand just as much when he has finished. Councillor PENIBERTON 'said he objected to Alderman Sm3thurst patting it in a wrong light. The indirect trading was the supply of materials to jobs, but they had no means of ascertaining its extent. Councillor MA.GIICN : All this is not merely argument but chargss against firms in the town. The MAYOR : This is an ordinary committee meet- ing now, and it will just give the public an idea of what committee meetings are like, and what I have to put up with.</p>
        <p>CoUncillor PEMBERTON: This is town's business. and T say Alderman Smethurst should not hold up the Slate Company and say they have only done £16,000 worth. What about the materials they have supplied for build- ings that have cost £180,000? Councillor ROGERS asked whether,if the extension of time wac granted, the proceedings would be reported. The MAYOR : That is a matter to be decided after- wards.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS : We 7aave no right to allow it to go on without being reported in a proper manner. Councillor BROUGH said as one of the fortunate or unfortunate men whose name appeared on the" black list "in the early stages of the agitation he did not think anyone was more pleased than he when it was decided to hold an inquiry. He happened to be a mem- ber of a firm which had done trading with the Corpora- tion to the extent of about 303. during the last six years. It would be a great mistake to let the matter stop in its present position. (Hear,hearl. He understood that it was to bea full complete and exhaustive inquiry It would be a great mistake to put any hindrance an the way of the Non-Trading commissioners. Conncillor JoHN ROBERTS pointed out that Waring and Company, who had traded to the extent of £7 with the Corporation, had not made a profit but a loss out of it.</p>
        <p>-The MA.YOR sail to end the matter at this stage would be unfortunate. If the commissioners would only decide to finish the inquiry by the end of August, they would do some good by letting them do so in their lifetime—(laughter)—and it would be satisfactory to him (the Nltvorl to find that during his year of office it had been brought to a satisfactory close. Whate.Yer they found he hoped they would be satisfied that they had had a thorough investigation. He thought they could not do better than pass the resolution. The resolution, with the addition that the Mayor request the Council's three commissioners to act until the end of the month, was then unanimously passed. Conncillor EVAN,' stiggested that in case the Council's commissioners refused to act, the Mayor should be emnowered, to appoint three gentlemen. The,Mivolt said he did n)t want that responsibility placed on his shoulders. They had too much confidence in him and he did not want it. Councillor EVAN'S then observed that the inqniry would not go on until the Council met again, if the commissioners refused to act. It was decided that if the Council's commissioners took several days before giving a decision the loss of time should be allowed for after the end of the month. ' The suggestion of Councillor EVANS that if the Cor- poration commissioners refused to act, the Mayor should find three other commissioners was agreed to.</p>
