      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>INQUIRY REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE INQUIRY REPORT.</p>
        <p>LIVELY TOWN COUNCIL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>The Complaint of Premature Closing.</p>
        <p>1 INQUIRY TO PROCEED.</p>
        <p>FOR SPATTERED NERVES.</p>
        <p>DR. WILLIAMS' PINK PILLS.</p>
        <p>TORTURING • DISFIGURING</p>
        <p>Skin, Scalp and Blood Humours</p>
        <p>From Pimples to Scrofula, from Infancy to Age</p>
        <p>Speedily Cured by Cuticura when All Else Fails.</p>
        <p>The agonizing itching and burning of the skin, as in eczema; the fright- ful scaling, as in psoriasis; the loss of hair and crusting of the scalp, as in scalled head; the facial disfigurements, as in acne and ringworm; the awful suffering of infants and anxiety of worn-out parents, as in milk crust, tet- ter and salt-rheum— all demand a rem- edy of almost superhuman virtues to successfully cope with them. That Cuticura Soap, Ointment and Resolv- ent are such stands proven beyond all doubt. No statement is made regard- ing them that is not justified by the strongest evidence. The purity and sweetness, the power to afford immedi- ate relief, the certainty of speedy and permanent cure, the absolute safety and great economy, have made them the standard skin cures, blood purifiers and humour remedies of the civilized world.</p>
        <p>Bathe the affected parts with hot water and Cuticura Soap, to cleanse the surface of crusts and scales and soften the thickened cuticle. Dry, without hard rubbing, and apply Cuti- cura Ointment freely, to allay itching, irritation and inflammation, and soothe and heal, and, lastly, take Cuticura Re- solvent, to cool and cleanse the blood. This complete local and constitutional treatment affords instant relief, per- mits rest and sleep in the severest forms of eczema and other itching, burning and scaly humours of the skin, scalp and blood, and points to a speedy, permanent and economical cure when all else fails.</p>
        <p>Sold throughout the world. Cutieura Resolvent, 2s. 6d. (in form of Chocolate Coated Pills, ls. lid. per vial 01 60), Ointment, 2s. 6d., Soap, Is. Depots: London. 27 Charter - house Sq.: Paris. 5 Rue de la Pair; Boston. 137Columbua Ave. Potter Drug &amp; Chem. Corp.. Sole Proprietors. sar- Send for "How to Cure Every Rumour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>The morning service on Sunday was tilen by Mr. Grady, the subject of whose sermon was "Growing in Grace." In the evening Councillor James Evans preached from the text "The Son of Man is come to seek and to save that which was lost," (Luke 19c. 18w.), and after pointing out man's need of repentance, the preacher showed Christ's willingness and power to save from sin and death unto life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket Street.</head>
        <p>Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket Street.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded congregation in Ebenezer Church Buttermarket-street, on Sunday when the Rev. Alfred Barratt, the new pastor, preached his first sermons in that place of worship. At the morning service the preacher spoke excellently on his call to the ministry and was listened to very appreciatively.—ln the evening the pastor Spoke on "The Cross of Christ ; the highest object of human glory." He took for his text the words, " God forbid that I should glory save in the Cross of our Lord Jesus Christ "—(Gal, vi., 14). Everybody had an instinct of glory. Some had glory in wealth, some in power, others in wisdom, skill, self-righteons- ness,amusement and many other worldly matters. But the true Christian gloried in the Cross of Christ, not, the wooden or brass image, but the bleeding hands and feat, the wounded side, the crown of thorns and the agony of our Lord Jesus Christ in His crucifixion. The ways of obtaining salvation through the Cross were by Ist, repentance; 2nd, personal dedication; 3rd, leading others to salvation. There were several obstacles in the way of the Cross such as atheism, animalism and selfishness, which were great attributes to sin. Selfish- ness was the very essence of sin. Let them all put these things out of their lives, he said,and seek the only true salvation. The choir rendered the anthem " Out of the Depths," Mr. W. Jowett presiding ,at the organ. On Tuesday evening, Mr. Barratt addressed a fairly large congregation on' God's Lo 7e to man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CHESHIRE HARVEST. Promising Condition of the Crops.</head>
        <p>THE CHESHIRE HARVEST. Promising Condition of the Crops.</p>
        <p>The harvest all over Cheshire will be both beneficent and abundant, says a correspondent of the "Daily Dispatch." Brilliant sunshine one day, mingled with cool, refreshing showers the next, have turned green into gold, and the fields are ready for the ingathering. Oats were the first to ripen; barley, of which only small patches are to be found in North Cheshire, followed, and soon wheat will assume the required colour dear to the heart of the farmer, while from a practical point of view the expense of harvesting will this year be reduced to a minimum. Corn cutting in North Cneshire has only just commenced, but this week will see it in full swing, and with a continuance of the present weather all grain crops should be gathered by the end of August or in the beginning of September which will be a strik- ing contrast to last year when potatoes and corn rotted in the fields and rain was the one dull and dreary record with scarcely half a dozen fine intervals in the whole season.</p>
        <p>Speaking generally, oats will turn out better than expected, both in weight of ear and length of straw] We have been shown some nice samples of oats taken from the fields a few miles from Stockport. Their condition is excellent, and they will bulk well. In some cases on heavy lands, patches have been beaten down by the rain, but otherwise there is little to com- plain of. Wheat also looks exceedingly well, and is turning slightly, but will not ready for cutting for a week or two. Hear, again, the rains, combined with bright sunshine,have had a beneficial effect. The acreage sown, however, is said not to be so large as in former years. In regard to barlew, we are told of one field near Chester of about 100 acres sown with Garton seed which was a marvel in its way. The stalks were sfs. or so high, the ears remarkably heavy, and it was quite a picture of waving gold. This cereal, however, is reported to be generally short in the straw on some lands.</p>
        <p>Pastures are not looking well. The drought has kept them exceedingly bare, but there has been a visible improvement in their condition during the past week. There is also a better pcospe3t now of fairly abundant after grass, and second crops of clover especially are likely to make up a good average. Turnips and mangolds look better now than they did early in the season, and owing to the favourable turn the weather took at the crucial time will exceed ex- pectations then formed. Li some fields there are traces of mildew in the swedes, but otherwise there is little to complain of. Late potatoes are expected to yield heavily in Cheshire, and it is satisfactory to hear that up to now there is little or no trace of disease. As usual," up-to-dates" are a great favourite, and are fetching high prices in Chester Market. Early apples, such as Keswicks and Suffields. are now appearing, and here again, owing to having been fed by the late rains, are a striking contrast both in size and flavour to the immature " windblown "things sent into the market a fortnight or so ago. Pears will be exceed- ingly heavy also, and plums and damsons will make a fair show, the latter turning out better than was expected early on in the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>AFTHR TWENTY-FIVE YEARS.</p>
        <p>Joseph Fairhurst, of 6S,Claughton-street,and his wife, Matilda Fairhurst, of 33, Rigby-street, appeared at St. Helens, on Monday, after being married twenty-five years, when Mr. H. L. Riley and Mr. E. IV. Swift, the solicitors in the case, said they did not think it was practicable for the parties to live together any more. The man had acted in an abandoned and cruel manner, but he was only earning 17s. 4d. per week, and, as the wife had the assistance of the elder children, she would agree to an order of 2s. Gd. a week rathPr than have the whole of the unpleasant details- given publicly.</p>
        <p>The Whitefield Breweries, Ltd., situate at Besses-o'th Barn, together with the licensed and other properties connected therewith,put to public competition on Wed- nesday by Messrs. Wilson and S3n as a goinst concern were withdrawn at £lOO,OOO.</p>
      </div>
