        <p>ALL kinds of BOOKS BOUND in any style from the simplest to the mostielaborate. pROSPECTUSES &amp;.• BALANCE-SI! ETS. pLACARDS. EXTRA LA ROE SIZE OUR speciality. D DRESSES. Pamphlets, Secretaries Reports &amp;c., printed In any size and quantity. RULES PRINTED and BOUND for Odd fell° 778 Foresters, Druids Shepherds' Sick, Burials an Trade Societies.</p>
        <p>desirable for the people to know, and there is not the slightest doubt that in this instance another Colonial Conference would reveal the fact that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S whole agitation is based on an impudent imposture. The Representatives of the Colonies would be certain to make it clear in the first place that their loyalty to the mother country is in no wise depend- ent on fiscal arrangements. They have already proteated to this effect with the energy and emphasis of men who feel that their personal honour is called in question. Mr. LAURIER, for example, has told us in the plain- est possible language that the lowering of tariffs against us was not intended as a bribe but as an evidence of good feeling. In the second place a Colonial Conference would at once make it plain that the " offer " of which Mr. CA MBERLAIN is never tired of speaking is a fig- ment of that politician's brain. There has never been any such offer. The present Colonial Secretary admitted this fact in definite terms only the other day. Yet the raging and tearing propaganda of his predecessor is based on these two proposition s that if we desire to retain the Colonies as portions of the Empire we must bind them to us by system of preference tariffs, and that they have themselves approached us with an "offer," the rejAction of which will not only undermine their loyalty, but will make them desirous and even determined, to set np in the world as independent countries.</p>
        <p>, The situation would really be very serious if it in any way corresponded to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S descripticn of it. We should be face to face with a cruel alternative. We should have to choose be- tween losing our Colonies and ruining ourselves. Happily enough, this is not the real condition of things. There is not the slightest ground for sus pecting our Colonies of disloyal intentions. Ther is not the slightest ground for supposing that they would enter into the arrangement which would be necessary if Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S proposals were to be carried out. There is not the slightest ground for thinking that the y would ever have raised a fiscal difficulty if they had not been urged by the Statesman who goes on talking about an "offer ' which they have never made. The views of the Colonies on fiscal questions as between our- selves and them are plain beyond dispute. They are quite ready to heighten their tariff wall against our foreign rivals if we desire it, and are preparei to offer compensating advantages; but they will not lower their tariff wall against our- selves. These are the plain truths the Colonial Conference would demonstrate, and we are not surprised, therefore, that no Colonial Conference is to be held. Nir. BALFOUR is doubtless aware that Mr. CILIMBERLAIN'S words on the subject were a mere manner of speech.</p>
        <p>None of Mr—.--Bar, THE BREWERS AND FOUR'S plans realise THE BISHOPS, his own expectations Arrangements were made by force of which the Licensing Bill •was to pass through the House of Lords without amend- ment. However, as the people of England must have been astonished to discover, there is a limit to the patience of the House of Lords, even when a Conservative Government is in power .Those Peers who thfnk it worth while to attend to their Parliamentary duties have given large majorities to the Government but they have considered it to be entirely consistent with their honour to swallow the Licensing Bill at a gulp. The single amendment that has been made is of no great consequence, but it is an ind ication to the Prime Minister tha however subservient the House of Lords may be, it is determined to preserve appearances. The Licensing Bill will have to go down to the House of Commons again, a circumstance which is obvi- ously very embarrassing to the Prime Minister. When questioned on the subject, he said that he is not quite certain when the Bill can be taken. This is a matter of course. Mr. BAEFoUR is never quite certain about anything. The Licensing Bill may now, however, be con- sidered to be in effect a part of the Statute Book of the Inaltn. It is a most astonishing piece of legislation, and the Prime Minister is probably as much surprised as anybody else that it should have been successfullycarried through. When he was interviewed,by a deputatior...of public-house owners at about the time of the Save n Oaks election, and when he said comforting but apparently indiscreet things about what must be done for the publicans, there was nobody who believed that he actually contemplated the conversion of an annual licence into a freehold. He was considered to be merely keeping one of the great interests at heel by means of promises that were not intended for realisation. This was probably Mr. BALFOUR's own view of the situation. It turned out,however, that when he had spoken as if the public house were the most sacred interest in the land, he was not permitted to sink into the belief that only, words were required from him. He went through the experience of those who signed the sort of contract that is supposed to have been common in the Middle Ages. He had not purchased freedom, but slavery. The owners of public-house property whose p )litics are th eir trade, as they are never tired of saying, are practical people who demand that a Statesman shall act, and not merely say civil words of com- miseration. Mr. BALFJUR, possibly to his con- sternation, found himself being dragged behind the Car of Juggernaut, and as he was a Prime Minister supported by a majority which was als in awe of the publicans, it has happened that the brewery and distillery interests are now even more securely endowed than the Ohurch of England. The Church, it is to be particularly noted, has done nothing effective, or even reputable, to pre- vent this. The struggle made by the ARCEIBISHor of CANTERBURY to impose a time-limit was really of a formal kind. The Bench of Bishops did not stand out for any great principle. It merely endeavoured to make a bargain. The people have seldom been more fully betrayed by the Church. We took a moderate position," said the Amu- BISHOP of CA NTE RBU Ry. His Grace and the Bishops really took a contemptible position. If the Licensing Bill had been the measure of a Liberal Governrcent, they would have fought it tooth and nail. Tne Aacuersuor now says that they will do their very beat to work the measure well. Stuff and nonsense ! They will have nothing to do with the working of the measure. Their pusillanimity—the right phrase in their sub- servience to Toryism—has fastened on the people of this country the very worst piece of legislation that has been added to the Statute Book in recent times, and it is legislation that is opposed to every principle they profess and every object of the religion which they are so generously paid to teach and uphold.</p>
        <p>A Town Council meeting towards the middle of August is not conducive to a cairn and deliberate consideration of public affairs, if one may judge from the experience of Tuesday last. It was warm out of doors but it was still warmer in the Council Chamber, and as the temperature rose in the heated assembly it seemed to affect the tempers of members und make them more than usually hasty. The meeting had not been in pro- gress many minutes before there was a cash of words, and the inevitable "scene" followed The Mayor became the central figure of a highly dramatic episode. The licence of the George Hotel was the subject around which the warfare raged. When the Corporation traffics in licences there is generally trouble ahead and the George Hotel licence has proved no exception to the rule. Councillor Maginn questioned the Mayor's right to sit and vote on the question—a challenge which immediately roused the ire of his worship. Quite a dramatic touch was given to the scene when the Mayor rose from his seat and flourishing a paper said " There's my resignation !'"The document was produced with such lightning rapidity that one wondered in amazement whether the Mayor had anticipated events and brought his resignation in his pocket ! At all events he was induced to withdraw it and Alderman Bo;ton is still Mayor of Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Corporation has certainly not enhanced its reputation by its conduct in respect to the George Hotel licence. Tile licence was offered for tender, conditional upon the magistrates consenting to its transfer to other premises—a condition which is open to a very wide interpretation and may mean the transfer of the licence to an entirely new district, as in the case of the Green Dragon which has entered upon another stage of its existence at the Brooklands. The tenders were obtained in a business-like way and when they were opened it was found that the Mayor was in competition with three others for the licence. His offer of £1,500 was exceeded by that of Messrs. Peter Walker- and Sons who bid £2,550. lie a sense Messrs. Walker relieved the Mayor from rather an awkward position. It is inconceivable that t,he Non-Trading Committee would have overlooked such a :glaring instance of direct trading with the Corporation as the purchase of a licenee from them would have been by the Mayor. The Finance Committee accepted Messrs. Walker's tender, but to the sur- prise of the Council, the Chairman of the com- mittee (alderman Fairclough) moved that it be deferred for further consideration urging as a reason that the new Licensing Bill had altered the circumstances. The Council agreed to the course suggested and since then Messrs. Walker have withdrawn their tender. The next step in this remarkable sequence of events will be awaited with interest. We still think that the Corpdtation would be taking a wise course if they followed the example of other municipalities and dropped the licence. lindar the new act they would be entitled to some compensation, but it is an open question whether they would succeed in getting anything substantial. The benefits to be derived from a reduction of licensed houses cannot altogether be reckored in £ s. d.</p>
        <p>The trading inquiry was another subject on which a considerable amount of heat was shown. Alderman Smethurst "relieved his mind" by enter- ing into an explanation of some small trading transactions .with the Corporation by himself and his son. This was hardly relevant to the point at issue, which was whether the report of the three Commissioners appointed by the Corporation should lie on the table until the report of the Non-Trading Commissioners hai been received. The Council has acted sensibly in refusing to accept the report sent out until the report of the Non-Trading Commissioners is com- plete. This was the only way to ens'ire the confi- dence of the public. By fixing a time limit for the completion of the inquiry the Council have also exercised a wise discretion. The Commissioners appointed by the Corporation have taken umbrage at the action of the Council and have refused, to act any further in the matter. Such a sLep is regrettable and may still further prolong the completion of the inquiry. It will now De for the Mayor to apnoint three other gentle- men to act as " watcners."</p>
        <p>The members of the Council who ventured to refer to the Medical Officet's report did not receive- much enc.iuragement—in fact there seemed to be a disposition on the part of the Mayor to narrow down the discussion as much as possible. His worship has a peculiar habit of interrupting mern hers when they are in the middle of an argu rnent, and nothing can be more tantalising to a speaker. If the health of Warrington is to be im- proved, much more will have to he heard of the subject in the Council Chamber than we are accustomed to hear. It is almost a useless ex- penditure of time on the part of the Medical Officer of Heath to prepare such a volumin- ous report, and make so many valuable sug- gestions if the document is simply to be viewed as " showing us he has done his duty in the best possible manner." The figures relating to the infantile death-rate are as Alderman Thnnett pointed out,really appalling. When the fact is realised that nearly half the death rate occurred among children under five years of age, it will be felt that it is time to look into the contributory causes and find remedies. The smoke nuisance is also a sub- ject which deserves more serious consideration than the Council gave to it on Tuesday. One enterprising local firm has already shown us that this nuisance can be abated if dealt with in a scientific manner. The nature of the occupations carried on in the town makes it impossible to entirely remove the pall of black smoke which overhangs us, but much might be done to lessen its density.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott has suggested a reduction in the charges for vapour and slipper baths, but the committee are obdurate. Tney can get plenty of customers to pay the present charges and they see no necessity to make any concessions. The baths I are already costing the ratepayers P.. 500 a year and the meiners of the committee seem indisposed to run the risk of losing more. This is one way of looking at the matter. But it is questionable whether the encouragement of cleanliness by the establish- ment of baths is not a good investment after all. Many would go unwashed were it not for the public baths, and by extending their use and bringing these facilities within the reach of all by a nominal charge, the Baths Committee might do something which would contribute to the health of the community. The Corporation, however, has not shown any great desire to reach the gnat unwashed. The Water Corn mittee is still sending in bills for water to the authorities of the Ragged School. The workers in connection with Ors institution are giving 100 poor boys and girls a "tubbing" each week, and for the privilege of carrying out this self-imposed but very necessary work they are being plied with demand notes for the water rate !</p>
        <p>Although it is best known as a grimy manufir3 taring town, Warrington may, in a sense, be regarded as an agricultural. centre. It is bordered on the Cheshire side, by rich agricultural tracts and that tin farmers rear their stock to the best advantage was evidenced by the splendid display at the Warrington Show on Thursday. Thanks to the keen interest and , energy of the president and offi.2ials, the show has now attaineo a wide reputation, and in many r respects can vie with most local exhibitions in England. This year the fine specimens of horseflesh were again the outstanding feature, and the task of the judges was by no means an easy one. It is gratifying to note that Warrington firms were creditably represented, and the show is another of the useful institutions wi4ich encourage owners and drivers to take a kindly interest and pride in their charges. The weather was not of the best, and the attendance suffered slightly,but notwithstanding the recollections of the Warring- ton show of 1904 will be of the happiest descri-p 't ion.</p>
        <p>By the death of M. Waldeck-Rousseau France' has lost one of her greatest servants. The ely Premier was not perhaps an heroic figure; Theatricals, which are part of the machinery politics on the other side of the English Chetihe.; were perhaps less apparent in this case than in of any of his contemporaries. His early years 9S a politician had taught him if not to despise at lea.° to measure the political crowd at its true value' His freedom from the hysteria of his fellow; politicians was probably the force whic enabled him, when he was called upon te undertake the difficult and delicate task—h°° difficult few indeed even at this day can realise -- to set straight the twigle of the Dreyfus affair. M. Waldeck-Rousseau approached the task With courage and resource, and he earned the undyin,g gratitude of all the most stable portioc of ill, countrymen by the calmness and firmness wit" which he proce,decl, undeterred by the warring elements around hiin,to the fulfilment of kis object, the removal, as far as lay in his power, of the stain which the Dreyfus aflair had laid upon the fair fame of his country. Here in Eogland, where the qualities which he exhibited are more familiar than they are in Franco, and adjudged of better value, his work was appreciated.</p>
        <p>Among the very few subjects pertaining ° labour discussed during the closing Sessl°ll.„ of Parliament, that of factory legislatin' and administration, says " Engineering," w313.t„,,he most inwortant from a practical point of vie 7" There was complaint that there were only sllcd women inspectors, while 1,500,000 women atl. girls were employed, besides those engage:ll° laundries, and a great number of outworkers. rbe Home Secretary replied that he had c°3l° to the conclusion to provide for an increas! next year. The administration of the Tract Acts, employment of children, the rev.° tion of fires, &amp;c., were promised attentiol; One member dealt more particularly wl,t accidents, falsification of particulars, and tal insufficiency of inspectors, and a,lso the systenl,f, " time-cribbing " in respect of stopping the Inec"hie nrey, &amp;c. The Home Secretary declared that sugestlons made were important and valual4'644 The most satisfactory feature in the debate the declaration of t;vo large 'manufacturers favour of inspection, both supporting the claim f° an increase of inspectors. Both cond-unned "tini; cribbing," and cheating the workpeople by the falsl s cation of particulars. Under such circumstance, the Home Secretary could well promise considel tion ot all the points raised. There was, indee no voice raised in opposition. Formerly,theto an outcry on the part of employers whenover.o proposal was made to extend inspection, incren inspectors, to tighten the law as to falsification 99 particulars, to fence machinery, &amp;c. Now there' a consensus of opinion on all these points.</p>
        <p>" Only recently," said the Duke of DJvonsli,roB it Eastboutne, "has the country begun 11 orzancipite itself from the intolerable tyrant-1Y 0, elucaciou examinaiions." It is easy to 11331cfa general stateinent of this sort, but is tbe statelo°,7o in accordance with fact? Has the country b 0 to emancipate itself from examinations, and 3' succi tests of knowledge tyrannical and intolerablei; :early, popu!atiou increases and the cc/11301'1i C. , as Oen for salaried posts becomes keener, the 011"; s safeguard against undue favouri:-Ism and adv., incompetency, from 'Deli many public doPa. - merits have suffered in the past, is exat3l°ob tion. Many a battle has been lost throll3,it splendid soldiers being led by officers igtode of tactics and the rudiments of battalion or brig.'i movements. Imagine, if one can, calling 111 physician on a ma te,. of lie or d‘iatii who 11401. been unexa.m.n,d ; or employing a solicitor, , accountant, wh, can shew to guar,ute,,s of 031 d petting. True, the clergy, of the EitabllBli3o Church are not required to pass examinations ich elocution, albeit that they are expected to PlO for half-au-hour twice every Sunday. The res-er is that the majority of the English- clergy weha,lo their ccngregations, and complain that the 1:13a-00 does not attend worship as it ought to do. are examinations tyrannical? To the ch°4... patent and ambitious nay offer posslu:die ties of recogulion for industry and pr°vlr many free scholarships. To do away with °",, systems of examinations is impossible. Theres are too many institutions who depend on the fe,s derived from exiininatious to cause them c'e relinquish such tests in a hurry. Take only orld profession—that of music. The Associated Bjar., of the Royal Academy and Royal College of *ll9,l'P the Incorporated Society oi Musicians, and Triulti College of Music, London, examine uhousands candidates annually,not only in the remotest Parh, of Great Britain and Ireland, buu throughoilD t"i British Colonies ; whilst the Royal Coliego 01,1 Organists, the Guildhall School of Music, 13'07,1 Manchester College of Music,Royal Irish Acticle7d of Music, Dublin, the Glasgow Attieuvaulliti other well-known institutions carry out perio"1"0, examinations on a large' scale. Abolish tho 1 comes derived from examinations, and ,3 abolishes many a useful establishment doing g°- work.</p>
        <p>An International Fire Service Congress Will Ab; held this month at Budapesth, organised 1107,8 the auspices of the International Fire Serv-rg Council,whose permanent offices are in Luxeelbucts" , The Hungarian Fire Brigades' Union act as h°':,„"g The congress will be chiefly occupied in diseust; sevet.ell important papers dealing with (a) 3 Baltynore Fire ; (b) Theatre Safety; (c) Fire yeetion in Factories and Mills ; (d) the ImPre.rg;,l3.irr tion of Wood and Textiles ; (e) Mechami2al -0.-11 Ladders ; and(f) Fire alarms. The Congress , e serve as the occasion for a general meeting 0` Intelnational Fire Service Council,which will.c°•o Feder the subject of a universal system of statitrs a dictionary of fire technology, and other riVi°-,-yl3 of general utility. It is also anticipated 'll--111 "Engineering" that some further steps -of be taken in the very grave matter of the nomenclature and standardisation. 430 fire-resisting materials, the general prinol P' of of which were settled at the London emigres:lsc- last year, but require extending so as more 0%1, tually to safeguard the public against being posed upon with useless or unreliable mater,j;l3.-t Some effort will,eo doubt, also be made to Poe. further bogus fire-tests of the showman `te sialweir These tests aro frequently arranged to official tests in everything but the technical esstehiS tials. Great Britain will be represented f° 'the conference. A special commission formed 13YO to British Fire Prevention Committee will Budapesth, and, besides attending the confere'ncee they will study matters of structural impert3Thet and appertaining to the fire service in cities on the way, such as Vienna, Mlich)l/..e. Frankfort. This Commission will include reP.'oll' sentatives, not only of the British Plevelljes, Committee and of the National Fire BrlBl. Union, but representatives of the professl°7l, (I- partly paid, gnu volunteer Fire Service, of bu ing constructien and building control, and surance Survey. Each member of the Conimi%ed takes up a special subject during the journeY ;,.1" ey the resultant joint report, which will be isstle",„ to the British Fire- Prevention Committee, iS gUr'tiorl 1 contain much of importance to the firc-prot i ec- nterests in this country.</p>
        <p>I Wedding at Cairo-street Chapel. I There was a pretty wedding at Cairo-street Liv,--plog Warrington, on Tuesday, the contracting parties u",O 18' Mr. Albert Cottrell, formerly of Warrington, but 1-iter of Sheffield, and Miss Rosie May Lowe, only of Mr. and Mrs. E. W. It. Lowe, of Warring,to:‘,;(l. Great Sankey. The Rev. C. Harvey Cook offic''.os The bride who was given away by her father, tell, becomingly attired in cream voile with hat to 01,3013 She was attended by two bridesmaids the Misses '70,D0 and Lizzie Cottrell, sisters of the bridegroonk core were tastefully dressed in blue voile, with black Pi of. hats. Mr. E. E. Lowe, of Plymouth (brothe'o. the bride), discharged the duties of best 133,0, Mr. Ernest Wilkes presided at the „ea,r,ftil,e and saveral approprinte hymns were sung. Aiwieigi' ceremony a reception was held at the Winn33:i Cafe. The usual toasts having been honaure' the happyc ouple departed for Derbyshire, wher-, solts honeymoon is being spent. Many handsome Pre were received.</p>
