        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>AUGUST 13th, 1904.</p>
        <p>The Prime Minister has given Sir</p>
        <p>COLONIAL CONFERENCES.</p>
        <p>HOWARD VINCENT a</p>
        <p>promise to "bear the matter in mind." The matter in ques- tion is the Imperial Conference which was , recently suggested by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN as the means of getting hims elf ouu of a diffi- culty. Mr. BALFOUR, it appearb, is not in the least inclined to arrange for such a gathering of Colonial representatives as would at once reveal the weakness of the augmentative structure which was so hastily erected by his former col- league when the necessity for diverting public attentiori from South Africa became very pressing. He told Sir HOWARD VINCENT and Mr. CHARLES 'MCARTHUR that there have been two Colonial Conferences within the last few years, that he hopes there will be some more, but that fcr his own part he does not intend to take any steps at the present moment for repeating the former experiments. " will you bear the matter in mind ?" queried Sir HOWARD VINCENT. "Yes," said Mr. BALrouR with the easy gaiety of a man who k•_-_ows that the promise implies no obligation. Conferences and Royal Commissions_ it was a "Royal Commission or other suitable tribunal "that Mr. CHARLES MCARIMUR asked for _are usually a very convenient means of shelving a question which has become a subject of ember. rassment. However, these inquiries are never sanctioned by Governments when it is probable that they will reveal In ore than it is considered</p>
      </div>
