      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>'Coming Events. The Rev. John Davies will preach at Golborne street Church to-morrow (Sunday). An open meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Society will be held at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street to-morrow afternoon. The Rev. John Davies will be the speaker. The annual sale of work in connection with Emmanuel Church will be held in the schools over -.4.rpley station on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, .N ovember 9th, 10th and 11th. The famous Shrewsbury great floral fete will be held on Wednesday and Thursday next. There will be a magnificent display, and a host of special attrac- tions is announced. Special excursion trains will run from Warrington and district. Personal. Mr. Skelland, who has been staying at Southport to recruit his health, has returned to Warr.ngton in im- proved health and has re-entered upon his Ragged • School work.</p>
        <p>A Wire Works Closing Down. We learn that Smith's Wire .Works in Dallam-lane are to be closed, the employes having received notice of dismissal.</p>
        <p>Successful Fancier. At Buxton Dog Show on Saturday, E. Wignall, a Warrington exhibitor, was one of the principal prize winners in the hull dog class.</p>
        <p>Warrington Butcher's Failure. On Friday, Mr. Fowler, solicitor, filed a petition on behalf of John William Bower, butcher, lq, Butter- market-street, Warrington, who has been adjudicated bankrupt.</p>
        <p>Smallpox in Warrington. There have been no further cases of smallpox in Warrington this week and the authorities are hoping that we have got to the end of the epidemic. There have been nine cases altogether, five of which are still in hospital. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OP TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STRERT, Warrington.—Advt. Branch of the Free Trade League at Altrincham.</p>
        <p>The Altrincham division branch of the Free-trade League has elected Mr. William Long, of Tb.elwall Heys, chairman, Mr. Alexander Muir, honorary treasurer, and Mr. F. R. B. Lindsell honorary secretary. The branch is now fully constituted. A Year's County Court Work. The business transactions at the Warrington County Court during the last official year, as given in the Blue Book, was as follows :-4,767 actions entered for trial, with debts and claims amounting to £12,805. Of these, 1,645 were determined and judgments obtained for £7,771, exclusive of costs £715 and fees £1,973; total amount paid in to the credit of suitors 17,214, and £1,280 was paid out to them. There were £OBB executions against goods issued, five sales made ;2,662 judgment summonses issued,l,7sl heard; 1,272 warrants of commitment issued, 524 debtors arrested, and 40 imprisoned.</p>
        <p>ASK for CIT'SNDIGHAM'S NIINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Town Counzil Committees. Alderman Hutchinson has been appointed a member of the Property Purchasing Committee of the Town Council in the place of the late Alderman Heaton. He has, however, resigned his seat on the Museum Com- mittee, which vacancy has been filled by the appoint- ment of Councillor P. Peacock, —At a meeting of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, Councillor Wilkinson tendered his resignation as a member, but the committee refused to accept it. Councillor Davies has been appointed a member of the Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLA.CE BOOTS and &amp;WES, UNDER the CLOCK. BRED GE-STREET.—AdtI.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. Joseph Caldwell. Mr. Joseph Caldwell, who for the past thirty years had carried on the business of a grocer and provision dealer in Wakefield-street., died on Monday night at Douglas at the age of sixty-three years. He went to the Isle of Man last Thursday week and became ill almost immediately on land- ing. He stayed at Vancouver House and was attended by Dr. Marshall, but despite that 'gentleman's efforts he passed away, death being due to syncope and failure of the heart's action. Mr. Councillor Wilkinson, who is a son-in-law of the de- ceased, went over to Douglas on Tuesday, and the remains were brought to Warrington. The funeral will take place at the Cemetery on Sunday. _ .</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. P.S.A. Meetings. Mr. Warham presided at a meeting of this society, held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey street, on Sun- day. Mr. R. Wood sang "The Sailor's Grave" and "Where Violets Grow," being accompanied on the piano by Miss Wood. Mr. Wright gave an address, and alluded to the sad double drowning fatality,one of the young menbeing a member of their society. Suitable hymns were sung for the occasion, and a vote-of con- dolence was passed to the parents and relatives of the two unfortunate youths.—The monthly open meeting of the Buckley street society was held on Sunday. Mr. J. J. Fairhurst, who occupied the chair, gave an en- couraging address from the text " Rescue the perishing." Mr. NV. Shaw read the lesson. The speaker was Councillor Barton, who took for his sub- ject "Perseverance and endurance." Miss L. Fair- hurst was heard to great advantage in the contralto solo" 0 rest in the Lord." Miss M. Plinston, A.L.C.M. was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>Fatal Heat Seizure. Mr. R. Dobson,coroner,inquired at Great Budworth on Saturday into the death of James Green, forty-one, a carter, of Old Gardens, Winwick-road, Warrington. The deceased on the afternoon of the previous Thursday, a terribly hot day, left Northwich for home seated on his lurry. Shortly afterwards a drayman found him lying dead on the road. The horse was standing quite still. One wheel had partially passed over the man's chest,and there was an abrasion of the head as if the horse's hoof had accidentally struck it. Mr. Browne,solicitor, who appeared for the deceased's employer Mr. James Toole, marine store dealer, said all the evidence pointed to Green being perfectly sober. They could only suggest that, owing to the heat, he had fallen exhausted from the lurry, and had thus met his death.—The jury agreed that this was a probable explanation of the sad occurrence. Verdict : " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. A Cat's Expensive Taste. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before Judge- Bowen Rowlands K.C. an action was heard in which Albert Widd, of Earle-street, Earles- town recovered .five guineas and costs from a neigh- bour named William Henry Walker, a carpenter, whose cat it was alleged had eaten eve chickens.— Arr. Browne represented the plaintiff and Mr. E. A. White appeared for the defendant Prom the evidence it appeared that Widd is a succes- iul breeder of brown leghorns, of which he makes a spebiality. For two years in succession he has won the Crystal Palace Challenge Trophy. The chickens which it was claimed the defendant's cat had destroyed 'were bred from this prize winner, and they were valued at two guineas each, at which -Price others of a like quality had been sold. On one. occasion, the defendant's cat waa seen by Daniel -Whitby and Sarah Littler to leave the plaintiff's pen 'With a chieken in its mouth. The plaintiff com- -Pained to the defendant but no notice was taken and 'eventually the plaintiff set a trap near his pen. On the following morning, the defendantes cat was found dead in the trap, and on seeing it, he admitted 'that it was his, For the defence it was argued that it had not been proved that the defendant's cat had taken five chickens and that there were cats very like it in the neighbourhoqd. On one occasion the plaintiff Complained to a Mr. Emmett that his cat had killed a Chicken. He afterwards found that he was mistaken. is Honour held that the plaintiff had proved his case and gave judgment 'for the amount claimed—five B'uineas.</p>
        <p>Musical Success. At the recent examination held by the Victoria College of Music, Miss Sarah Rigby, pupil of Mr. C.H. Dawson, passed with honours in the Intermediate grade of singing. Howley Band of Hope. About 400 members of the Howley Band of Hope enjoyed a trip to E:rodsham on Saturday through the kindness of Mr. R. A. Naylor. On arrival the chil- dren wended their way to Overton Hills, where all kinds of games were indulged in, after which tea was partaken of. The return journey was made about nine o'clock, all having spent a most enjoyable day. RECE ROOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER TILE CLOCK.—Advt Licensing Transfers. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, the follow- ing transfers were granted :—Britannia Inn, from exors of Ann Holland to F. It. Molyneux ; Raven Hotel, from William Dillon, deceased to W. C. Brooks; off-licence, Frogliall-lane, from A. B. Binks to J. T. Collins; off-licence, Knutsford-road, from Joseph Bate to Thomas Bate. BAssrxErrEs AND Mair, CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GA.RLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 35, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Music in the Park. To-morrow ,'Sunday), at 3.15 the Legh-street Brass Band will occupy the stand, and will Fender the following programme, Mr. Fred Podmore conduct- ing :—Andante "To Three my Heart," Beethoven: march" The Double Eagle," Wagner ; song "Nazareth," Gounod,(solo euphoniumMr.G.Smith)selection" Elijah," Mendelsohn, including "Thanks be to God," "If with all your Hearts," "Baal we cry to thee,"; air " Cujus Animam," Rossini,(solo trombone Mr. H. Spruce) ;song "A Dream of Paradise," Gray, (solo cornet Mr. A. Landor) ; march "Farewell my Comrades," Rimmer ; "God save the King." Presentation to an Oddfellow. An interesting presentation took place at the usual monthly meeting of the "Loyal Garibaldi" Lodge of the National Independent Order of Oddfellows. Bro. John. Moss, N.G., occupied the chair, and Bro. W. Bonnell, V.G., the vice-chair. The lodge was also honoured by the presence of the officers of the dis- trict—Bre. George Hands, Grand Master, and Bro, William Pennington, Deputy Grand Master. At the request of the N.G. the Grand. Master presented Bro. John W. Meachin with a framed emblem of the order, bearing the inscription "For services rendered."— Bro. Meachin, in responding, said he had tried to do his best for the lodge. PITBLICI BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for REST ROCT in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Drank Carbolic Acid. .Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station on Thursday touching the death of William Henry Morris (50), residing at 2 Owen-street, and employed as a time keeper at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles' Dallam Works.—The evidence showed that about a month ago deceased suffered from Influenza and was away from work for ten days. Since then he had experienced pains in the head. Before going to the forge early on Wednesday morning he complained of feeling unwell. At 9.40 he went into the store room, poured the contents of a bottle of carbolic acid into a can, and drank the poison. Previously he had seen a storekeeper named Thomas Owen, and bade him "good-bye." Dr. Adams was sent for, and applied a stomach pump, but the poison proved fatal. —The jury found that deceased committed suicide whilst of unsound mind. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OR TONICS. I 63, SANKEY-STREET, Warriugton.—Ndvt. Liberal Voters Please Note ! The new list of voters for 1904 is now published and affixed to the door of every place of worship in the Borough, and can also be seen at the Liberal Club, Bewsey-street. In view of the fact that the next general election will in all probability be fought on this register, it is incumbent upon every Liberal to see that his name is on the list. Every Liberal who has been a householder for 12 months ending July 15th, last, should examine the list for himself, and if his name is omitted, or if there is any error or mis- description of any kind whatever, he should inform Mr. Clayton, the Liberal agent, at once, so that the vote may not be lost. Lodgers who occupy one or more rooms of the clear value of £lO are also entitled to vote, and should claim immediately. Claim forms and all assistance will be readily furnished on applica- tion at the Registration offices, Liberal Club, War- rington. All claims must be served upon the overseers onor before August 20th. Volunteers Return from Camp. Looking the picture of health the members of the local volunteer battalion returned to Warrington after a fortnight under canvas, on Saturday night. They left Perham Down about noon in two special trains, and after a long and tedious journey, reached War- rington about 9.30. They were accorded a hearty reception by thousands of townspeople who lined the route to the Drill Hail. The camp has been mlst successful as the following extract from Saturday's Brigade orders will show :—" Colonel Quale Jones, C. 8., commanding the 30th Field Army Brigade, wishes to inform all ranks Inw satisfied he was with their work during camp. The desire to profit as much as possible during this period was very marked, as well as the improved manner in which the men turned out. The companies were better handled by the com- pany officers and section commanders. The camps were better kept. He begs to thank his staff for their untiring zeal, and commanding officers for their loyal support." ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CL ASS MINER&amp; WATERS.—Advt. Local Druidism. After the completion of the ordinary business of the "Loyal Stonehenge" Lodge of the Order of Druids, on Saturday evening, a Presentation took place to one of the members, Bro Thomas Parr, who had met with an accident whilst following his ordin- ary occupation, and had been awarded a grant of £2O from the Accident Compensation Fund in connection with the order.—The Vice-Grand Master (Bro. David Houghton, D.C.S. of the Warrington Equalized Dis- trict), in making the presentation, said the fact that Bro. Parr would now have the £2O added to his sick pay was a striking demonstration of the usefulness of the accident fund. He understoz4l that he had now been in the lodge for 51 years, and he rejoiced to think that in his old age the trouble brought on him by his accident would be relieved by this useful addition to his ordinary benefits.---The Secretary, on behalf of Bro. Parr, thanked the Board of Management for the grant which had been made.—Bro. Battersby, in pro- posing a vote of thanks to the Vice-Grand Master for his attendance, congratulated him on the high honour which had been conferred upon him, and hoped that the illness from which he was now suffering would soon pass away, so that he might have health and strength to carry on the good work he was engaged in.—Bro. Houghton, in reply, said he was proud of the position of Vice-Grand Master. He would strive to do his duty, so that when the time came for him to relinquish his chain of office he would have nothing to regret.—During the evening the Secretary presented the quarterly balance sheet, which showed the lodge to be worth £1,217 is. 4d., with a membership of 240.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, an d 55, Bridze-street,Warringion.— Advt. The Double Drowning Fatality. Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held an inquest at the Police Station on Saturday, touching the death of Joseph Tavlin (16), of 24, Lloyd-street, and William Charles Head (18), of 40 Lloyd-street who were drowned in the St. Helens and Sankey Canal near Buttermilk bridge on the previous Thursday after- noon.—Evidence of identification was given by James Tavlin, bricksetter's labourer, father of the boy Tavlin, and Rebecca Head, step-mother of Head. They last saw the boys alive when they left home about one o'clock to go for a walk. —Robert Weigh, a boy living at 48, Mill-lane, said that at two o'clock he and the deceased lads went to the Sankey Canal to bathe. After bathing they went for a walk and returned to the canal They all undressed, and Head was the first to enter the water. For a short time he swam with the aid of a cork belt, but on trying to swim without it he got into difficulties. Tavlin dived into the canal to rescue him, but both clung together and were drowned. Tavlin was a very good swimmer, but Head could only swim alittle.—P.O. Dugdale, of Sankey, spoke to recovering the bodies with grappling irons.—The Coroner said. that although the case was a very sorrow- ful one, it was a simple matter for the jury to decide, He was not a swimmer but he knew from those who were that it was a very difficult thing even for an ex- pert to render assistance to one who conk'. not swim. —The jury returned a verdict of " Accidentally drowned."—The Coroner said they were all very sorry that the parents had lost their boys. It might be a melancholy satisfaction, but still it was a satis- faction to Mr. Tavlin to know that his son lost his life in trying to save that of anothet.—ln accordance with the wish of the jury the Coroner entered on the verdict in Tavlin's case that he lost his life in trying , to save another.</p>
      </div>
