      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 13, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 13, 1904</p>
        <p>considerable economy to themselves. He said the smoke was absolute waste, and they all knew it was an absolute nuisance. The Council would be entirely remiss if it did not awake to a sense of responsibility in the matter. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillot4oaN Roma-I's said that with reference to West-street, something was standing in the way to prevent the Paving and Sewerage Committee going on with the work. _</p>
        <p>The BOROUGH SURVEYOR said ths• Council had made application to the Local Government Board to botrow money for street improvements, and the:y could not pro- ceed with them until they witained the Board's sanction. West-street was included in the application. Councillor JoRN ROBERTS said the committee had been round to many of the streets and they would be improved. As far as St. John's Ward was concerned, he thought it had been well attended to. Councillor POMBERTON said that in many cases the residents themselves were perfectly satisfied. Some of the passages were already paved with cobbles and could not be considered insanitary. Of course, the committee admitted that many streets needed paving. The applica- tion at present before the Local Government Board was for permission to borrow £B,OOO or £9,ooo,and many streets would be taken in hand. Altogether about £20,000 worth of paving would be carried out. In regard to the smoke nuisance, they all knew that Alderman Bennett hadan eye for the beautiful, and it was very nice: but they could not live in a manufacturing town and have no smoke. ,There was a large fortune awaiting the person who would invent a pate it to make manufactur- ing towns smokeless. Warrington compared favourably with any town in England of its size in regard to the smoke nuisance. In some cases where black smoke was not emitted, the works might be turning out even more deadly fumed. Alderman Bennett was alittle too severe on the manufacturers. If he wanted an ideal place he would have to go a mile or two beyond Hill cliffs. The MAYOR said he did not like the assertions about Warrington being,•bad and unhealthy. Els was cultivation. plants which generally grew right on the top of the Andes—where the air was supposed to be the purest in the world—and they flourished in Warrington better than in their native place. Alderman BENNETT : Wilderspool. not Warrington. The MA.YOR : It is in the borgugh. Continuing, he hoped Warrington would not be slurred at as an unhealthy spot simply because a tramp introduced small-pox or something of the kind, and because people would not us l as much of Crosaeld's soap as they ought ta. (L Lit ;us?. ) Warrington was not the bad place that people wanted to make it out to be. It had been slurred at on every hand, both on the ground of bad health and alleged bad management, shady tricks and transactions. As long as he plssibly could, he would stick up for his own town. (Hear:, hear.) Councillor ROGERS said he was surprised to hear Alderman Bennett complain from time to time about the smoke. He was only sorry that the whole of the chimneys were not smoking. It was not owing to the unhealthiness of the town that the Clergy Daughters' School had been removed. There was not a single case of sickness in the Training College, which was next door. Certain people who were interested had said the town was unhealthy to suit themselves. _</p>
        <p>The IVIA.vou.: We shall lose nothing. If one door closes, another opens. Alderman Sm EV:HIRST said the members of the Paving and Sewerage Com uittee had done their best for the borough in regard to the paving of streets. Councillor DODDS said.the people creating the smoke would be only too glad to get rid of it, because it was a great Waste. They were as anxious as Alderman Bennett to get rid of it. Still, he felt that the people of Warrington would like to see a great deal more smoke. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SurroN said the Health Committee were responsible to some extent for looking after the smoke, but it was almost impossible to keep on fining people for creating smoke without incurring a great deal of censure from those who o3aupied the works. The firms who turned out the densest and blackestsmoke were not the greatest offenders against the health of the town. He did not believe that smoke did any damage whatever in that respect. Taa greatest damtge came . from other chimneys which often turned out noxious vapours in the night-tirae. If attention was given to those, there would be a great deal less disease in the town. The minutes were then confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S HEALTH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL AND THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Alclemma Bennett and " Extremely Tragic " Facts.</p>
        <p>THE SMOKE NUISANCE</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Medical Officer of Health which was recently published in the "Examiner" gave rise to some interesting discussion at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. In moving the adoption of the minutes of the Health Committee Alderman SuTroN said the general health of the town was somewhat better than it had been although scarlet fever still continued to be very preva- lent. At the present time there were about seventy patients in the hospital, but many of them, he was told, would be discharged this week. He regretted to say that smallpox had again broken out in Warrington. This was the fourth out break since they got rid of last year's epidemic Altogether, nine cases had been removed to the hospital and two had proved fatal. One had been discharged and of the six remaining in the hospital, two were very serious indeed. Tee cases had been brought into the town by tramps, and it was really very important that something should be done by the Government to prevent tramps going about spreading disease from one town to another. It was difficult to know what ought to be done. The London County Council had the matter under consideration, and theyhad already decided to call together all the sanitary authorities in the kingdom to discuss the question at an early date. He hoped the Warrington Corporation would do its best to assist them in bringing about some remedy for the existing evil. CouncillorJAmEs EVANS se3onded. Councillor STOI7 thought it very important that the Medical Offl.cer's report should be fully and freely dis- cussed. One of the most important subjects alluded to, was the paving of streets and passages. It was stated some months ago by members of the Health Committee that there were more cases of disease from paired streets than from streets which were unpaved. He knew for a positive fact that in two unpaved streets many cases of infectious disease had been reported. West-street in particular was in an abominable condition. Building operations were completed some years ago, but part of the street had not been paved. The tenants had been try- ing to improve its condition by depositing loads of ashes and clinkers therein some places the surface of the street was higher than the front door steps of the houses, so that when there had been heavy rain the occupants had to step into several inches of water on leaving their dwellings. The matter of back passages was even more serious. Unfortunately he happened to live adjoining some of the worst samples of unpaved back passages in the town. That was in St. John's Ward.</p>
        <p>Alderman Surrox said he did not want to 'interfere, but Councillor Stott was going upon ground which had nothing whatever to do with the Health Committee. He believed it had been found on inbestiga,tion by the Medical (Meer that it was not a fact that there were more cases of infectious disease in unpaved streets than there were in paved streets. The Paving and Sewerage Committee had to deal with the streets and passages. Councillor STOTT said he was mentioning these matters to bring them prominently before the Council, and he did not think they were wasting time in dis- cussing conditions which were detrimental to the health of the town. _</p>
        <p>The MA.YOR. : Certain members of the Council think it their boundeu duty to instruct every particular com- mittee in its duty. Councillor STorr : I don't think that reference is meant for me.</p>
        <p>The NIA.YOR. : It is meant for whoever the cap fits (Laughter.) Councillor STOTT: As a member of the Council lam to some•extent responsible for the health of the town. The MAYOR: I hope you don't take the health of the town on your shoulders altogether. . Councillor STOTT: I cannot but accept my share of the responsibility. I have been returned by the rate- payers, and that is more than some members of the Council can say. The MAnroß: You are taking it in the wrong spirit. We are here to conduct the town's business. Continu- ing he pointed out that a special meeting would be required if every committee attended to every little complaint that was brought forward. Councillor STOTT asked what good the medical officer's report was, if the Council could not discuss it. The MA.voR : To show you he has done his duty in the best possible manner. Councillor STOTT: He makes certain suggestions, and if the committees won't carry them out, the Council ought to compel them to do so. The IVla.vott: You ought to pass a vote of censure upon them. Councillor STOTT said he would take other steps to bring these matters prominently before the Council. Alderman BENNETT said the report was one of the most important documents submitted to the Council for many months, If they gave the whole evening to the discussion, they would not be doing injustice to the matters. If there was one question which in face of recent statistics was of vital importance, it was the health of the town. Dr. Gornall gave an immense amount of time and thought to the report which did the very highest credit to his ability and the faithful- ness with which he discharged his duties. It was scant courtesy on the part of the Council to let the report lie on the-table and practically ignore it. There were many points in it which were extremely suggestive. One was the very high infantile death-rate. It was really appalling to look at the figures. There were practically as many deaths under the age of flve,as there were from five to the utmost age that any inhabitants of the town attained. The deaths of children under five numbered 602, and the total at ages over five was 635. Nearly half the death rate therefore occurred among children under five. That struck him as extremely tragic. Obviously, as long as that state of things con- tinued there must be something very wrong and rotten in the sanitaryconditions. The insufficient knowledge of the parents and the unsatisfactory supply of milk to children were, of course,lcontributory causes. Warring- ton might, he considered, follow the example of St. Helens and other towns and provide a municipal supply of sterilised mi:k. That would do a great deal to diminish the death rate. One fact must be rather strik- ing to those who some months ago belittled the danger of measles and regarded it as a disease which they might safely pooh-pooh, and that was that the deaths from measles were higher than from any other cause. If that was not sufficient to make the Council become alarmed and consider things seriously, he did not know wh it was. _</p>
        <p>--The IVIA.yoR : Surely you don't blame the Council for the deaths from measles.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : lam trying to call the atten- tion of the Council to facts which seem to be of the utmost possible importance. The MAYOR: I cannot get at your arguments yet. Alderman BENNETT : That is not my fault. (Laughter) Continuing, he said there was a glaring omission in the report, as no figures were given in reference to his old friend the smoke nuisance. Obviously Dr. Gornall was not satisfied, for in a most scathing paragraph he dealt with the nuisance. Any community ought to hang their heads in shame when they were deserving of a rebuke so dignified and so withering as Dr. Gornall had compressed into a few lines. The doctor said: " The smoke nuisance is, as , might be expected, very bad, and the people so devoted , to money-getting as not to be concerned much by the evils to health arising therefrom, still less caring for the ugliness of it." If that did not make every citizen who had any sense of public spirit or pride in his town feel ashamed, all he (Alderman Bennett) had to say was that it ought to do. To be indifferent to public health was perhaps the worst of the two though to his mind it was a crime to be indifferent to public beauty. When Dr. Gornall said that both the health and beauty of the town suffered from the nuisance, the Council ought to listen to him I and endeavour to see if there was not something in what he said. It had occurred to him that probably the report on the nuisance had been suppressed because it was so absolutely unsatisfactory and unsufficing. For years past it had been a mere mockery to attempt to superintend the smoke nuisance of the town. The Council simply allowed it to g_o on broadcast and made no real effort to prevent it. It must be because they were so utterly ashamed of the ineffective way in which the department performed its duties that the report was excluded. Quite recently through the nuisance they had lost one of the most ancient and valuable institu- tions of the town. _</p>
        <p>The MI.You. : That is not on the minutes. Alderman BENNETT : It is illustrating my point. , The MAYOR: lam not going to have -Warrington black-balled and allow you to say that everything clears out of it because of the smoke nuisance. Alderman BENNETT : It is simply because I love Warrington that I make these remarks. The absolute fact is that the Clergy Daughters' School has left War- ington. after being here sixty years, mainly because of the smoke nuisance. I personally cannot regard the loss of an institution .of that kind without extreme regret. Continuing he said he could not be charged with being indifferent to the trade of the town. Anyone who made that charge must see that it was idiotic. The welfare of the town was his wel- fare, and no one had more interest in it than he had. He said again, not only with the strength of his own conviction bat backed by the expert knowledge of the ablest chemist in Warrington, that the nuisance was not only'an intolerable nuisance but an intolerable sacrifice of money,and might be prevented with great benefit to the industries of the town. One of the largest and most flourishing firms in the town—Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited—had solved the question, and their chimnays were called the smokeless chimneys. The MA.YoR: They turn out sulphuric acid instead of black smoke. What is the advantage. Alderman BENNETT said Dr. Markel had told him that any works in the town could solve the difficulty if they only had the enterprise, and they could do it with very</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>IN AND AROUND WARRINGrON.</head>
        <p>IN AND AROUND WARRINGrON.</p>
        <p>The Health of the Town.</p>
        <p>[uy TdE "FREE CRITIC."]</p>
        <p>Judging from the debate which took place last Tuesdayin the Council Chain her, there appears to be a disposition on the part of several of our Alderme and Councillors to regard anyone who draws atten- tion to the state of the health of the town as its enemy. , Alderman Bennett who endeavoured to gat the Conn- , cil to discuss the matter seriously was simply derided for his pains, the Mayor particularly, being severe upon him. This to. my mind is not the way to treat. the subject of the health of the town. It is they who wish to stifle the discussion who are the enemies whilst those who, like A.1.43,-n ii fl onnett keep the sub- ject before the town are its true friends. Our Medical Officer of Health whose able report should be read and pondered over by all the inhabitants of the town, pointed out the grave conditions in which we are placed and he ought in all ways possible to be sup- ported. It appears to me that our Medical Officer of Health from information which has reached me, could very easily have said more than he did, for the candi- tions in which some of our neighbours are living—and we are all more or less responsible for them,the Council more responsible than anyone else—is simply appall- ing. As Alderman Bennett stated, there is something radically wrong when more than one half of those who are born in Warrington die before the age of five years. And it is no secret that our death rate has several times during the past 12 months been the highest in the country. It is not my purpose in this article to particularise certain streets or certain cases, as it is my intention to do so later on,but I will state that we have too much insanitary property in the town i.e. one cause and a serious cause too of the conditions of the health of the town. I understand too that on dark nights a free view Of the stars can be observed by those who . live in certain houses in the town and although a study of a 4ronomy might be enjoyed by some people, it can easily he imagined what is the condition of these rooms and their beds when the weather is wet and stormy. Then there is the case mentioned by the Rev. W. Bracecamp in a sermon of a dead body being kept more than a week in a house in the town. And perhaps it will surprise some to hear that that has not been the solitary case by any means in Warrington. And what about our unpaved streets and passages, which according to the stereotyped remarks of the Paving and Sewage Com- mittee are going to be taken in hand?" Did not Dr. G-ornall state in his report that one street had not been paved for 20 years? The Medical Officer says:—" The list given of those that require attention contains many names which appeared in a similar list published in 1898 and some even were mentioned b 7 the Medical Officer of Health 20 years ago and remain unpaved to this day." It is all very well to talk about high rates. If you have an unhealthy town—you are bound to have high rates. Personally I was very much pleased to see the report of the sermon by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook and to notice that he was supported in an able letter by the Rev. .T. Vona. 1 should like to see this subject thoroughly taken up in the Press and pulpit so that the town should be thoroughly wakened up to the serious condition it is in so that it will not rest satisfied until it has done all that is possible to make it a clean and healthy town. And it can be so made in spite of all its smoke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUSSIAN FLEET DISPERSED.</head>
        <p>RUSSIAN FLEET DISPERSED.</p>
        <p>Japanese Fleet Undamaged.</p>
        <p>The following telegram, dated Tokio, August 12th has been received at the Japanese Legation : According to the reports hitherto received, the Russian squadron emerged from Port Arthur was attacked by our fleet in the south of Yenta° and dis- persed. Askold, Novik, another cruiser, and a destroyer took refuge in Kaiaschow, and another destroyer in Chifa on August 11th. Five Russian battleships, one cruiser, probably Diana, one hospital ship, and several destroyers seemed to regain Port Arthur on August 11th. Our fleet is believed to be undamaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>1/- TINS ASSORTED BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>CONTAINING 12 KINDS: OVAL RICH TEA POLO KIEL FINGERS ALEXANDRA</p>
        <p>HANOVER RAGLAN THIN ARROWROOT CREAM CRACKERS MEXICAN MILK OAT( WAFERS I CHEESE WAFERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>RECORD ENTRY AND CREDITABLE</p>
        <p>EXHIBITS.</p>
        <p>HORSES A STRONG FEATURE.</p>
        <p>Amid the picturesque surroundings of Walton Park, the Warrington and District Agricultural Society held its fifteenth annual show on Thursday. From very small beginnings the show has grown until it has now a more than local reputation, in fact the quality of the exhibits in some of the horse classes is considered to be equal to the display at any local show in the country. To the esteemed president, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., the society owes a debt of gratitude for the whole-hearted interest he has taken in its pro- gress. Not the least helpfal of the many ways in which he lends encouragement is the concession so readily granted every year of holding the exhibition in the beautiful grounds at Walton. Apart from their adaptability for show purposes, the richly-wooded and trimly-kept grounds have a natural charm which always attracts great numbers of town dwellers. The model stables and piggeries are thrown open to the visitors who find a tour of inspection one of the most interesting features of the day. The Warrington Show is noted for its well-nigh perfect arrangements, the capable officials working with an enthusiasm which always augurs well for success. A. special word of praise is due to Hr. 11. Beresford 'Melville, the inde- fatigable secretary. Thursday opened very unpromis- ingly, a lowering sky and biting wind causing many visitors to come provided with overcoats and umbrellas After luncheon there was a welcome improve- ment. The clouds rolled away, the sun shone brightly, and with the exception of a few smart showers the conditions were nothing to Com- plain of. Peaceful Walton was for the nonce the scene of animation, and throughout the day there was a steady stream of well filled vehicles to the Park. The attendance was about the same as last year ; if anything a slight falling off, due no doubt to the threatening weather at noon, was noted. As to the show itself,it is questionable whether a more creditable collection of stock has ever been seen at Warrington. The entries constituted a record, being fifty in ad- vance of last year. As usual, the horse classes were the strongest feature, but cattle and pigs showed a slight improvement on last year. The produce and polo pony classes were dropped, but there was a class for ponies. Judging commenced at ten o'clock and was in progress with only short intermission until after seven in the evening. The, greatest general interest centred in the jumping con- test, which was full of excitement. So keen was the struggle for honours, that the first and second prizes were divided. Special mention must be made of the selections by the Yorkshire Hussars Band which con- tributed not a little to the enjoyment of the visitors. An excellent programme was submitted in highly creditable style, hr. Samuel Sackley, jun., conducting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FEATURES OF THE SHOW.</head>
        <p>FEATURES OF THE SHOW.</p>
        <p>To go into details, first and bre:l:Lost come the shire horses for which the Warrington Show is famed. The classes this year were exceptionally good, in fact better have never been shown at Walton. Mr. James Gould of Lymm, was, as usual, a prominent winner. His splendid all-round bay Lynam Bloom carried off the dis- trict chlmpion cup pregented by the president. Although this is her first year in the chow world, she has gained laurels at big s hows, including ninth at London and two firsts and a second at Birkenhead. At Warringwn. she was first in her class, and in addition to lifting the championship, secured Mr. Stapleton Bretherton's prize, and a gold medal presented by the Shiro Horse Society. Mr. Gould has also reason to be satisfied with his other exhibits. In the brood mare shire class he secured premier honours with Stanney Luck, a sound black of good scale which subsequently headed the open class. The yearlidg colt Lymm Champion. which was tenth at London aad first and third at Birkenhead, also gained a first prize. Another first went to the .yearling filly Lymm Blossom whose previous successes Include eighth at London, first at Ormskirk and Prescot, first and Shire Horse Society's silver medal at Edgeworth, and first and third at Birkenhead. The winning two ye-tr old filly was Lymrn Bloom, a wide, strong filly with several prizes to her record. The geldings were the strongest class the judges had seen for years. Six extraordinary animals were shown, and the judges had great difficulty in allocating the positions. Mr. Sparkes,of Altrincham, won with Oldfield Duke, a massive, heavy horse with better quarters and thighs than Mr. P. Davies's un-named bay which was also a splendid specimen with beautiful joints and feather. The third, fourth and fifth winners were capital, in fact they were all calculated to win in almost any show in the country. Mr.C.Bell,of Norley,showed a fine two-year-old stallion. The mare and foal classes were excellent, and judging was by no means easy. There was some excitement anent the open gelding class, for Oldfleld Duke not. only met here his stable companion Oldfleld Prince,who was first in London,Duke having been the Royal Lanca- shire winner—but also Mr. E. Davies's Partington Ploughboy, who beat Prince at Birkenhead, and took the 50 guinea championship there. The Duke won, Prince, a year older, was second, and Ploughboy, a year older still, was third. _ _</p>
        <p>The hunter classes also excelled all previous displays. and would have done credit to any local show in the country. The brood mare section, although not as strong as at some of the large public shows in the north of England, contained threeanimals of the right type, and in every way calculated to predominate it, Mr. Schwabe was quickly pulled in first with his perfectly shaped ches- nut Lady Mary. The second is undescribed in the catalogue, and all regular attenders at shows know the third, Mrs. Griffiths's Lady Templar. The foals insome instances were rather unfortunate in point of age, one of Messrs. Parry's animals winning somewhat easily over a very late foal with sufficient bone and power to make a hunter. The yearlings were a small class, but the prizes were well bestowed. The three-year-olds were also numerically weak, but were of sufficient quality to redeem the character of the class. The saddle classes were a grand feature. In the section for mare or geld- ing foaled in 1900 a chesnut belonging to Mr. J. H. Stokes, of Market Harbero', secured an easy victory, notwithstanding that the other competi- tors were decidedly above the average. Of hunters up to 13 stone and not over 14stone 7 lbs, the Right Hon. Lord Revelstoke's chesnut gelding Romeo, the winner of the first prize at the great Peterboro' show and the champion cup at Richmond, disposed of all opposition somewhat easily. In the opinion of the judges, this was as strong a class as has ever been witnessed at a local show. The class for mares or geldings equal to carry 14stone 71bs and upwards ',nought out a stiong field, and after a very severe competition a chesnut gelding belonging to Mr. Stokes was awarded premier honours. In the section for hunters not exceeding 15.2 hands and equal to carry 14stones there were only five exhibits, none of which could be said to be quite equal to the stip ilated weight. The first winner was probably able to carry something like 2 stones less. Hackneys were a fine selection, but the exhibits were chiefly local. Considering that fact, the classes were ex- tremely creditable, especially the foals from the brood , mares which gave splendid promise. In hackney mares Mr. Goodson won well with Polly Ailsa, a good all- round animal which was first at Adlington and third at the Royal Lancashire, and attained many prizes last year. His chestnut filly Royal Danegelt, a wonderful mover which has been successfulat numerous shows,was an easy winner, as was the chesnut yearling Heathfield Daisy which won last year at Manchester, and was third at the Royal Lancashire and first at Adlington this season. The harness classes were excel- lent, four or five animals being among the best of their class in the country. In the big class the winner was Mr. W. E. Inman's Silver Ness, and the second Miss E. S. Ross's Rowton Blackthorn. Lady Defiant in the under 15 hands went away from everything. Rougham Lady Kate, too, gave a good show. in the smaller class Champion Queen Go Bang won easily. There was a very good class of dairy cattle, some of the animals being much above the average and fit to take a place in any show. The silver cup offered by Colonel W. Hall Walker, M.P. (vice-president) for the best tenant farmer's exhibit was secured by Mr. Richard Clark, sen., of ‘Varburton Park who showed a remark- ably fine animal of a quality that is seldom met with. Heifers were a useful lot, and several of the prize winners were really excellent animals. Age bulls were not so good, but the younger specimens were of a much better quality than usual, showing that the society is effecting an improvement in this direction. Pigs generally wore a creditable collection, the sows being particularly good. The gilts were capital samples, and included some fine specimens. The cottage classes were poor, the entries numbering only three, but the winner was a good, big animal with several features above the ordinary. Butter and cheese were of excellent quality, but the butter class was very poorly filled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>JUNIPING CONTEST.</head>
        <p>THE JUNIPING CONTEST.</p>
        <p>To the average spectator who is not thoroughly con- versant with agriculture the jumping contest was by far the most striking feature of the show. Four prizes of £2O, £l5, 10 and £5 respectively were offered, and competition was exceedingly keen. The enclosure with its background of luxurious foliage, was surrounded by crowds of visitors, and had quite an animated holiday aspect. The jumpers had to clear several gates and fences and a pool of water, and the ease with which some of them passed the obstacles won hearty applause. There were eight competiters. A high-spirited mare was Lady Somerset, belonging to Mr. John Taylor, of Ormskirk, who has had the honour , of being introduced to the King. She required ' careful management, but the rider was equal to his task and had hard lines in not securing aprize.lt was a source of disappointment that Mrs. Blockley, of Madeley, Staffs., did not ride, but sent the brown gelding Omega.</p>
        <p>Se severe wes the contest, that the first and second prizes were divided between Mrs. Blockley and J. W. Foster, Brailsford, Derby. The third award went to F. G. Liddle, Hull, and the fourth to George Ledson, Bromboro'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>JUDGES.</head>
        <p>THE JUDGES.</p>
        <p>The duties of judging were ably discharged by the following :—Shires and draught geldings: Mr, aames Whinnerah, Warton Hall, Carnforth ;Mr Joseph Hill, Smethwick Hall, Congleton. Hunters: Mr. Digby Collins, Newton Ferrers, Callington ; Mr. Alfred Ash- ton, Sandiway, Northwich. Hackneys and harness : The Rt. Hon. Frederick Wrench, Killacoona, Bally- brack ; Mr. It; Heaton, the Ferry, Chatteris. Jumpers: Mr. C. W. Wilson, Rigmaden Park, Kirkby Lonsdale ; Mr. E. W. Griffiths, Plasnewydd, Denbigh- shire. Cattle : Mr. George Moreton, Kinderton Hall, Middlewich ; Mr. William Bateman, Beaumont Grange, Halton R. 5.0., Lancaster. Pigs: Mr. D. R. Daybell, Bottesford, Nottingham. Butter and cheese: Mr. James Cowsill, Altrincham. Shoeing Examination : Mr. T. Hopkin, Strangeways. Manchester ; Mr. A. Munro, Stockport-road, Altrincham. Consulting veterinary surgeons: Mr. Hugh Ferguson, Warrington; Mr. T. E. Jones, Municipal Buildings, Liverpool. Referee, Mr. W. Bainbridge, Warrington. STANDS AND EXHIBITS.</p>
        <p>There were fourteen special stands and exhibits which did the respective firms great credit. The local exhibi- tors were :—Joseph Muskett, exhibit of carriages, &amp;c., Higher Walton; Gartons' Ltd., Seed Growers and Mer- chants, Warrington; Joseph Crosaeld and Sons, Ltd., Warrington; Dutton and Co., exhibit of agricultural implements, &amp;c., Market-street, Warrington ; Mrs. Massey, Acton .Grange Nurseries (exhibit of flowers, plants, fruit, &amp;c., in produce tent.) The Luncheon.</p>
        <p>At noon a large gathering of members and exhibitors was entertained at luncheon by Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall. The guests included—Mr. and Mrs. E. W. Griffiths, Mr. and Mrs. Cyril Greenall, Mr. Harvey Talbot, Mr. John White, Mr. Colin Maciver, Mr. H. Beresford Melville. Captain Withington, Mr. J. E. Seeker, Rev. L. J. T. Darvvall, and the judges. The loyal toasts having been honoured. Mr. Stapleton Bretherton proposed " Snccess to the Warrington Show" coupling with it the name of the President. He said the success of the show was assured. It was going very much ahead and was growing into a big show. One of the best features of local shows was that they encouraged local Went to exhibit when perhaps they did not care to enter a show where a great number of what he might term professionals sent cattle and horses. Even if the success of the show was not assured it would be quite safe with such a president as they had at the head—Sir Gilbert Greenall—who had done so much for it. He would like to take that opportunity of thankine Sir Gilbert on behalf of the company for the great kindness and hospitality he had extended to them on that occasion. (Applause.) He had made every preparation for the comfort of the vieitors in case it was wet, and had done all in his power to provide for the success of the show. (Applause.) The toast was received with musical honours. Sir Gi)bert Greenall in reply, said he thanked them very much indeed for the very kind manner in which they had received the toast of the success to the society which Mr. Stapleton Bretherton had coupled with his name in such cordial terms. He had been connected with the society from the commencement of their shows and had taken a great interest in the development and improvement they had made from year to year. They were all delighted to see the pro- gress which had been attained. They had a most excel- lent exhibition that day and a record entry. The stock of all kinds, not only the horses and cattle, but every- thing they had gone in for had shown a wonderful improvement since the old days when they first started in the Barrack yard at Warrington. He could only , repeat what he had said on former occasions, that it was a groat pleasure to him to see them at Walton , that day, and as long as he had their hearty support he would do everything that lay in his power to promote their mutual interests. (Applause). Mr. J. T. Wood, of Halewood, proposed "The Judges." He said those who were exhibitors and those who were lookers-on—mostly, however, interested in agriculture—knew full well the necessity of having judges absolutely beyond suspicion and who were able to inculcate to those who came there for lessons in agriculture the educational advantages derivable from such a show as that. They had only to look at he list of judges in the catalogue to see how exceedingly care- fully the names had been selected for the respective classes, and those who had been at the ringside and seen the prizes awarded could sincerely compliment the president and the committee of the society on the excellent choice they hail made in each department. (Applause). The Right Hon. Frederick Wrench, of Killacoona Ballybrack, responded. He did not know why he had been selected to reply unless it was that Sir Gilbert was making the very doubtful experiment of introduc- ing the Irish eleme it. (Laughter.) If there was one thing they thought they knew in Ireland it was a good horse, and if there was one thing they thought they did well it was the management of the Royal Dublin Society's Show, which was held this month and at which ,he hoped a great many of them would be present. He might say after the experience of that show, that he had never seen a show of the size of the Warrington Show where all the arrangements were more perfect, and he was quite sure he was only expressing the feelings of his brother judges as well as his own when he said they would come from a great deal further than Ireland in order to help Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall in the admirable work they were carrying out amongst agricul- uralists. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>PRIZE LIST. SHIRES.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST. SHIRES.</p>
        <p>District Classes.-Brood mare 4 years old and upwards with foal at foot: 1, James Gould, tVlodel Farm, Lymm, near Warrington; 2, Charles Bell, Norley Hall, Norley, Cheshire; 3, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm, near Warrington; he, Edward Gibbon. Yew Tree Farm, Lymm near Warrington; r, Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne C. 8., Hale Hall, near Liverpool. Mare, 4 years old and upwards without foal: 1, Colonel R. Ireland Black- barna, C. 8., Hale Hall, near Liverpool; 2, Exors. of John Blackshaw, Blue Coat Farm, Hatton, near Warring ton; 3, Edward Gibbon, Yew Tree Farm. Lynam, near Warrington; he, Alfred Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook, hear Warrington; r, H. and R. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley, near Warrington. Colt foal: 1, Walter Cunningham. Gorsey Cop. Gateacre, near Liver- pool; 2, Charles Bell, Norley Hall, Norley,Cheshire ; 3, Walter Cunningham, Gorsey Cop, Gateacre, near Liver- pool; hc, Thomas Jeffs, Hapsford Hall, Helsbv, War- rington; r, Walter Cunningham, Gorses, Cop, Gateacre, near Liverpool. Filly foal: 1, Sam W. Gould, Foxley' Hall, Lymm, near Warrington; 2, James Gould, Model Farm, Lyrnm' near Warrington; 3, Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne, C. 8., Hale Hall, near Liverpool; r, John Whitlow, Tannery Farm, Preston Brook, Cheshire. Yearling colt or gelding: 1, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm, Warrington; 2, H. and 11. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley, Warrington; 3, Thomas Percival, Moss Side, Moore, Warrington; r, Joseph Shakeshaft, Cherry- lane, Lymm, Warrington. Yearling filly: 1, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm, near Warrington; 2, John Whitlow, Broomfields, Appleton, Cheshire; 3, Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne, C. 8., Hale Hall, near Liverpool; r, Exors. of John Blackshaw, Blue Coat Farm, Hatton, Warrington. Two-year-old colt or gelding: 1, Charles Bell, Norley Hall, Norley, Cheshire; 2, Edward Gibbon, Yew Tree Farm, Lymm, Warrington ; 3, Errors. of John Blackshaw, Blue Coat Farm, Hatton, Warrington ; r, Edward Gibbon, Yew Tree Farm, Lyrum, Warrington. Two-year-old filly: 1, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm, near Warrington; 2, Alfred Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook; 2, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm, near Warrington. Three-year-old filly : 1, Walter Cunningham, Gorsey Cop, Gateacre, near Liver- pool 2, H. and R. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley, near Warrington ; 3, Alfred Broome, Windraiil Farm, Preston Brook; r, Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne, C. 8., Hale Hall, near Liverpool. Gelding foaled in 1900 or 1901 : 1, A. C. Sparkes, Oldfield, Altrincham ; 2, Peter Davies, Midlands Farm, Warburton' near War- rington; 3, James Whitlow, Garland Hall, Northwich ; r, E. W. Sankey, Croft, near Warrington. Open classes.-Brood mare, four years old and onwards with foal at foot: 1. James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm; 2, Victor C. W. Cavendish, M.P. Holker,; Cark-in-Cartmel ' • 3, Charles Bell, Norley Hall, Norley r, James Gould, Model Farm, Lymm. Colt foal : I.Walter Cunningham Gorsey Cop, Gateacre; 2, John W. Kenworthy, Castle Hill, Kelsall, near Chester; 3, Victor C. W. Cavendish, M.P. Holker; Cark-in-Cartmel. r. Charles Bell,Norlev Hall,Norley ;Filly foal: 1 Victor C. W. Cavendish, M.P. Holker, Cark-in-Cartmel ; 2,Sam W. Gould, Foxiey Hall,Lymm ; 3, James Gould. Model Farm, Lymm: r, Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne, C. 8., Hale Hall. Gelding, four years old and upwards: C. Sparkes, Oldfield, Altrincham ; 2 Edward Davies, Walk Farm, Partington ; 3, A. C. Sparkes, Oldfield, Altrincham ; r, Peter Davies, Midlands Farm, Warbur- ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>H UNT E RS .</head>
        <p>H UNT E RS .</p>
        <p>District Classes.—Brood mare having produced a living foal by, or stinted to, a thoroughbred or regis- tered hunter sire in 1904: 1 and premium, C. Schwabe, Ardern, Altrincham ; 2. Robert Needham,Bexton Lodge Knutsford ; 3, Mrs. M. Griffiths, Broken Cross, North- 1 wich ; r, T. and a Parry, Shipbrook Hill, near North- wich. Foal by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1, T. and G. Parry, Shipbrook Hill, near North- wich; 2, C. Schwabe, Arden, Altrincham ; 3, Hugh Edward Wilbraham, Delamere House, near Northwich; r. Robert Needham, Bexton Lodge, Knutsford. Yearling colt, gelding or filly by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1 and premium T. and G. Parry, Shipbrook Hill, near Northwich; 2, R. Prescott, Aston-1 by-Budworth, Northwich; 3, Mrs. M. Griffiths, Broken Cross, Northwich. Two or three-year-old gelding or filly by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1 and premium, T. and G. Parry, Shipbrook Hill, near North- wich ; 2, Robert Needham, Bexton Lodge, Knutsford ; 3, Robert Shepherd, Parkside, Aston, Preston Brook. Open Classes.--Hunter mare or gelding foaled in 1900: 1. 2 and 3„T. H. Stokes,Great Bowdon. Market. Harboro'. Hunter mare or gelding foaled in 1897,1898,1899 orl9oo, equal to carry 13st. and not more than 14st. 71bs : 1, The Right Hon. Lord Revelstoke,NTarket Harboro' ; 2 and 3, 1 .T. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market Harboro' ; 4, John IDrage. Chapel Brampton, Northampton; r, A. J. Brown, The Grove, York-road, Doncaster. Hunter mare or gelding foaled in 1897, 1898, 1899 or 1900 equal to carry</p>
        <p>14st. 71bs. and upwards: 1. J. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market Harboro' ; 2, John Drage, Chapel Brampton, Northampton; 3 and 4, J. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market, Harboro' ; r, Sir Humphrey de Trafford, Market Harboro'. Hunter mare or gelding foaled in 1896, 1897, 1898, 1899 or 1900, not exceeding 15.2 hands, equal to carry 14st. : 1, Austin C. Carr, Lower Bexton Hall, Chester 2, G. Norris Midwood, North Rode, Congle- ton ; 3, Mr. M. Griffiths, Broken Cross, Northwich.</p>
        <p>HACKNEYS. District Classes.-Brood mare by registered hackney having produced a living foal by, or stinted to, n. registered hackney in 1904: 1, A. L. Goodson, Heath- field, Knuts ford; 2, James B. Aitken, Hall Nook,Penketh; 3 and premium, John Longshaw, Howshoots Farm, Gmppenhall. Foal by a registered hackney out of a mare by a registered hackney sire: 1, A. L. Goodson, Heathfield, Knutsford ; 2, James B. Aitken, Hall Nook, Penketh ; 3, Alfred Jackson, Bank House, Helsby ; r, John Longshaw, Hovvshoots Farm, Grappenhall. Year- ling colt, gelding, or filly by registered hackney out of a mare by a registered hackney sire: 1, A. L. Goodson, Heathfleld, Knutsford; 2, George Smith, Cranshaw Hall, Bold. Two or three-year-old gelding or filly by registered hackney out of mare by registered hackney sire : 1, Sir Lees Knowles, Bart. M.P., Westwood, Pendlebury, Manchester; 2, Sir Lees Knowles, Bart., M.P., Westwood, Pendlebury, Manchester; 3. John Makeague, Golborne Park, Newton-le-willows; r and premium, Exors. of John Blackshaw, Blue Coat Farm, Hatton. Pony brood mare not exceeding 14 hands, having produced a living foal by, or stinted to, a registered hackney, or registered hackney pony stallion in 1904 : 1 and premium, Christian Bower, Appleton, 2, J. L. Fryer, Pear Tree Farm, Padgate ; 3, Frank Houghton, Broom Cottage, Appleton • r, G. H. Wood, r 2 Church-street, Altrincham. Foal by registered hackney, or registered hackney pony stallion, out of mare entered in class 27: 1, Christian Bower, Appleton; 2, Margaret Garnett, Tarbock, near Prescot ; 3, J. L. Fryer Pear Tree Farm, Padgate. Two or three- year-old gelding or filly by registered hackney or regis- tered hackney pony stallion. Two-year-olds not to exceed 13-2 and three-year-olds not to exceed 13-3 : I, Christian Bower, Appleton; 2, Christian Bower, Appleton; 3, Frank Houghton, Broome Cottage Appleton; r, William Fowles, jun., Hatton. Harness Classes.-Mare or Gelding, 15 hands and over: 1, W. E. Inman, Fenay Bridge. Huddersfield; 2, Mrs. S. M. Dixon, the Manor House, Welford, Rugby; 3, Miss Ella S. Ross, Beechfield, Sale Cheshire. Mare or Gelding under 15 hands and exceeding 14 hands: I, J. Reynolds Fox, Brooklyn, Morton, Ga.insborough ; 2, G. Pimlott, Altrincham, Cheshire; 3, John Makeague, Golborne Park, Newton-le-Willows; r, John Kerr, M.P., Gaddesden Place, Hemel Hampstead. Mare or Giolding not exceeding 14 hands: G. E. Franklin, The Field,Derby; 2' Caleb Jones, 80 Duke-street, St. Helens; 3, Christian Bower, Appleton Thorn. Warrington ; r, John Hall, 36, Park-road, Oldham. Light Horses (single or pair) vehicle and harness: 1, W. and J. Strange, Knutsford-road, Warrington; 2, William Johnson, 7, Woolton-street, Woolton, near Liverpool ; 3, M. Shake shaft, Crouchley Farm, Lyrnm, Warrington; 4, Sam. Whitlow, Manor House, Preston Brook, Warrington. Va,nners or Light Lurry Horses (single or pair) vehicle and harness : 1, William Monks, 133, Bridge-street, Warrington; 2, R. Tunstall, 191, Padgate-lane, War- rington •' 3, Joseph S. Garland, Bridge-street, Warring- ton; 4, Joseph Hall, Daintith Farm, Appleton, War- rington. Heavy Horses (single or team) vehicle and harness: 1, A. C. Sparkes, Oldfield, Altrincham; 2 Edward Davies, Walk Farm, Partington, near Manches- ter; 3, Exors. of John Blackshaw, Blue Coat Farm, Hatton ; 4. The Warrington Slate Co., Ltd., Bold-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>CATTLE.</head>
        <p>CATTLE.</p>
        <p>Distric: Classes.—One dairy cow in calf (to calve within two months after date of show) : 1, Samuel Davies, Platt Farm. Heatley: 2, G. J. Baxter, Bradley Brook Farm, Stretton : 3, James Parr, Hatton ; hc, Thomas Warburton, Daresbary. One dairy cow in milk: 1, Richard Clark. sen., Warburton Park; 2, R. L. Warburton, Hatton; 3, Thomas Warburton, Daresbury; ii c. R. L. Warburton, Hatton. Heifer born in 1902: 1 and 2, John R.Newton,The Bent, Warburton; 3 and r, Samuel Davies, Platt Farm, Heatley. Heifer born in 1903: 1, Richard Clark,sen.,Warburton Park ;2, Samuel Davies, Platt Farm, Heatley; 3, John R. Newton, The Bent, Warburton; r, Exors of Joseph Knowles,Old Farm, Appleton. Bull, born in 1902 or 1903: 1, Samuel Davies, Platt Farm, Heatley; 2, Charles Bell, Norley Hall, Norley ; 3, Samuel Davies,Platt Farm,Heatley; r, James Parr,Hatton. Bull, born in 1902 or 1903 : 1, R.L. War- burton, Hatton; 2. Thomas Warburton, Daresbury ; 3, Samuel Davies, Platt Farm, Heatley; r, Peter Tickle Preston Brook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>PIGS</head>
        <p>PIGS</p>
        <p>District Classes.—Breeding sow in pig, any age or breed. 1, Mrs. PHter Leather, Spark Hall, Stratton; 2, Thomas S nith, Union Bank Farm, Bold ; 3, Randolph W. Platt,White Honse,Plumbley ;r,E. H. Yarwood.Prim- rose Hill, High Leigh. Best. cottage pig : 1, John Bramhall, Hatton; 2, and 3, Joseph Miller, Appleton Thorn. Best gilt (any breed) born in 1901: 1, John Bramhall, Hatton; 2, Randolph W. Platt, White House, Plumbley; 3, Thomas Booth, Booths-Hill Farm, Lymm; r, Thomas Smith, Union Bank Farm, Bold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CHEESE,</head>
        <p>CHEESE,</p>
        <p>Three cheeses 40Ibs.each or over: 1, John Dil - worth, Lower Hall, Manley 2, R. Prescott, Aston-by- Budworth, Northwich ; 3. Thomas Dawson, Dunham Hill, Helsby ; r, William Gerrard, Stockley Parm,Arley, Northwich. Three cheeses above 20Ibs. each and under 401bs. each: 1, John Dilworth, Lower Hall, Manley; John Moore, Newton, Preston Bro(mk; 3, Eaton Brether- ton, Newton,Preston Brook; r,J. E.Gerrard, Arley Moss.</p>
        <p>BUTTER. 2lbs. Butter made up in ilbs. and slightly salted 1, Farmers' Stores Dairy, Warrington : 2, and 3, Sir Richard M. Brooke,Bart. Norton Priory, Runcorn; r,_ Mrs. Thos. Booth, Booth's Hill Farm, Lymm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SPECIAL PRIZES.</head>
        <p>THE SPECIAL PRIZES.</p>
        <p>The special awards were made as follows : CHAMPION CUP (presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.), for best exhibit in district shire horse classes.— James W. Gould, Lymm. _</p>
        <p>SILVER CUP (presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall), for best shire foal in the show.—\lr. Victor C. W. Cavendish, M.P., Holker. _</p>
        <p>SILVER Cm. (presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall) for best shire exhibit by one of his tenants.---Mr. Alfred Broome, Preston Brook. _ _ .</p>
        <p>SPECIAL PRIZE of .C 5 (presented by Mr. F. Stapleton Bretherton).—James W. Gould, Lymm. SILVER CuP(presented by Colonel W. Eiall Walker,M.P.) for best exhibit in district cattle classes, the property of a tenant farmer.—Richard Clarke, Warburton Park. THREE SPECIAL PRIZES of £2 (presented by Lady Greenall) for best shod heavy and light horses.—James Whitlow, Garland Hall, Northwich ; Joseph Warbur- ton, Bell House Farm, Grappenhall, and S. Schawbe, Arden, Altrincham (prize divided) ;,IW. and J. Strange, Warrington, and Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.. Warrington (prize divided). PRIZES presented by Messrs. Day and Sons, for best exhibits in certain cattle classes, the property of tenant farmers.—James W. Gould, Lymm ; Samuel Davies, Heatley. _ _ _</p>
        <p>PRIZES OF £5 £3, and .£2 (presented by Mr. J. W. Kenworthy. Keisall) for the three best shire , foals got. by any of his horses.-1, John Whitlow, Preston Brook; 2. James Fairclougl, Weaverham ; 3, George Smith, Bold.</p>
        <p>_ _ GOLD MEDAL (presented by Shire Horse Society) for best mare or filly belonging to a member of the society. —James W._Gould,.Lymm. _</p>
        <p>SILVER NTEDAL (presented by Hunters' Improvement Society).—T. and G. Parry, Shipbrook Hill, near North- wich.</p>
        <p>SILVER MEDAL (presented by Hackney Horse Society) for best hackney or pony mare,filly or filly foal,registered or eligible for registration in the hackney stud book. —Sir Lees!Knowles, Bart ALP., Pendlebury, Manchester. GOLD MEDAL (presented by Hackney Horse Society) for best mare or gelding exhibited in single harness.— G. Pimlott4 Altrincham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SALE OF SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S</head>
        <p>SALE OF SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S</p>
        <p>HACKNEYS.</p>
        <p>At Walton Hall, Warrington, yesterday, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., disposed of a high-class stock of pedi- gree hackneys and hackney ponies. There was a g ood attendance of buyers and about 75 animals were disposed of- The sale realised 2,581 guineas, or an average price of 34 guineas. Eight yearling hackney colts fetched an average price of 48 guineas. Mr. R. G. Ingham, of Elland, gave 71 guineas for" Terrington Signature," a fine chesnut colt, and Mr. R. A. Montgomery secured "Terrington Activity" for 70 gnineas. The yearling fillies sold at an average price of 35 guineas. "Terring- ton Saphira " brought the highest bid, Mr. Scott, of Carluke, being the purchaser for 48 guineas. A trio of three-year-old fillies fetched an average price of 45 guineas,Mr. Scott and Mr. Ingham being the purchasers, the last-named giving 50 guineas far" Terrington Ade- laide." There was an exceptionally good lot of brood mares and foals, and some of the mares brought good prices. For "Lady Hutton," a brown mare foaled in 1888. Mr. Makeague, of Newton-le-Willows, gave 105 guineas, the highest price realised at the sale. " Bright Dorothy," a chesnut foaled in 1897 was secured by Mr. J. Clappenson, of Altrincham for 65 guineas. Mr. Stott gave 58 guineas for" Daisy Dane- gelt," a chesnut foaled in 1894 and Mr. J. Strange, of IVarrington, gave 57 guineas for "Terrington Maritana." The foals brought an average price of 22 guineas, the highest bid being that of Mr. J. Ball, of Poulton, who secured Daisy Danegelt's filly foal for 32 guineas. A trio of four year old geldings broken to ride in single and double harness were brought under the hammer and fetched an average price of 57 guineas. Mr. Scott, the highest bidder, gave 71 guineas for Tissington Merry- man, a bay 13 3., foaled in 1900. The pony brood mares and foals realised on the average 22 guineas. There was keen competition for Tissington Humph a brown pony mare foaled in 1898, and it was eventually knocked down to Mr. G. W. Gould, of Lyavn for 44 guineas. There were five three year old pony fillies offered for sale and they realised an average price of 53 guineas. The best was "Tissington Dolly-Varden," purchased by Mr. E. Jones, of St. Helens, for 100 guineas. Sixteen yearling pony fillies and colts fetched an average price of 23 guineas. Mr. Bainbridge was the auctioneer.</p>
      </div>
