      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HEALTH AND STRENGTH.</head>
        <p>HEALTH AND STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>Nature's best gifts are those that contribute towards health and strength. The active principle of the best remedial agents (sarsaparilla, burdock, gentian, lavender saffron, dandelion, and quinine) constitute Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the most reliable tonic, purely vegetable. Health has many handmaids, and all depend on strength of nerve and muscle. We exact too much from these in the daily competitive struggle for success in life,and we have to suffer inconsequence—indigestion weakness, nervousness, sleeplessness, liver disorders, chest affections, and numerous other complaints. NOVF all these are successfully combated with the sus- taining assistance of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the best remedy of the age. It is safe, sure, and permanent in its effect. If you find you are almost unable to follow your daily vocation, immediately strengthen your constitutiln by taking a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. They make the weak strong, and give new heart and "go "to the weary toiler. They revive the spirits and strengthen the nerves. By giving tone to the whole constitution they ward off fevers and other ailments. No family should be without a bottle. An occasional dose, taken when a man has felt slightly out of sorts, has prevented many a long illness. We assert theabove confidentially. Man is much more subject to the influence of his surroundings than is generally supposed. The ill effects upon the general health of living in close, ill-ventilated, and badly-lit homes, offices, or work- shops, are, of course, well known to everyone who has studied hygiene; while the life-giving effects oj sunlight and fresh air are equally well known. The same may be said of the results of the various climatic changes. The injurious effects of a prevailing east wind are evident to all observant persons; and the depressing influence of an approaching thunderstorm on some constitutions has also been observed by most people. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters may be termed an insurance policy against "catching cold," for it gives stamina and a healthy vitality to resist climatic changes and is a reliable safeguard against colds, chills, and coughs, which frequently happen through the weakness of the constitution being unable to resist the effects of the changeableness of the weather. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters never disappoints when given a fair trial, and no medicine has had so many imitators,but the purchaser has the remedy against such deceit in his own hands. Note particularly the name" Gwilym Evans " on thelabel, on the stamp, and on the bottle (a threefold precaution), without which none are genuine. Refuse all others then you will have the satisfaction of benefiting by the unrivalled virtures of this great tonic. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each by chemists and patent medicine vendors, or will be sent direct by the proprietors, without any extra charge to any address in the United Kingdom. Sole Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited,Llanelly, bouth Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>UNFRIENDLY NEIGHBOURS AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, Eliza Rimmer, Pike-street, summoned Peter and Emil?, Tom- linson, also residing in Pike-street, for assaulting her ou July 16th, and Mrs. Tomlinson summoned Mrs. Rimmer for a like offence. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins re- presented Mrs. Rimmer, and Mr. Browne appeared for Mr. and Mrs. Tomlinson. Mr. Jenkins stated that the parties were neighbours. On the day in question is client was going down a passage leading to Gaskell- street when Mr. Tomlinson came up. He threatened her in a rude and insulting manner. She remonstrated quietly with him and he struck her a violent blow in the face without any reason whatever, a little boy being near at the time. She then went and complained at the Police Station,but on returning she was met by Mr. and Mrs. Tomlinson, who had apparently been waiting for her. They threatened her again and Mrs. Tomlinson struck her in the face. They continued to threaten and strike her on the way to her house, and as a result both her eyes were black and her hip were sore from kicks. Several witnesses were calledsupporting this statement An entirely different story was told by Mr. Browne. This was one of the worst cases, he said, he had taken part in. Mrs. Rimier was a singular woman and imagined that people passing always stared at her in an insulting manner. On the date in question Mr. Tomlinson, who did not know her at all, was going down the passage and Mrs. Rimmer called him a " skenning " cat and picked up a stone and threw it at him, cutting his cheek. Mr. Tomlinson went home and told his wife about the matter. Mrs. Tomlinson, who was a much smaller and weaker woman than Mrs. Rimmer, asked the latter what she had been doing t- her husband, and Mrs. Rimmer replied that she would do the same to her. She then pickeq.up a stone and threw it at Mrs. Tomlinson causing a wound behind the ear. She then knocked he, down, and began kicking her, and was also seen to "dance" upon her. The result of this was that Mrs. Tomlinson was covered with blood and in a terrible condition. She became unconscious and had to be carried into her house by her husband and a St. Helens gentleman, who was passing at the time of the row Since then Mrs. Tomlinson had suffered considerably from her injuries. Her body was black and blue and she was unable to even dress herself for a week. He thought severe punishment ought to be meted out to Mrs. Rimmer. William Ashby, St. Helens, corroborated Mr. Browne's statement. Police-sereeant Bowyer said he saw Mrs. Tomlinson after the row. She was covered with blood, and in a shocking condition. The magis- trates said the evidence was very conflicting, and after giving it careful consideration they had decided to bind all the parties over to keep the peace for six months in a surety of ZIO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.--Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Sutton. Bennett, Francomb, and Smethurst,Councillor Thornton, Messrs. Birchall,Pilling,Jolley,W. Roberts,</p>
        <p>and Reynolds. , ASSAULTS ON CHILDREN.—Janies Kilduff, 14, Cumberland street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in St. Mary Street and with assaulting James W. Wright and Harry Wright, aged nine and seven years respectively, on Saturday night. P. C. Plumb saw a large crowd in the street, and on proceiad- ing there found the prisoner creating a disturhance. He had previously struck the boys and knocked them down—Prisoner, who alleged that some boys had annoyed him, was fined 10s. and costs. ALLEGED VIOLENT ASSAULT.—John Broderick, 12, Haydock-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Turner-street on Saturday night and with assaulting Thomas Taylor.—Superintendent Monaghan, in asking for a remand until to-day, said Mr. Taylor was a tradesman living in Turner-street. About midnight on Saturday he was standing in the street, in company with his son7when the prisoner, who was stripped to the waist, came upon the scene and challenged him .to fight. Mr. Taylor turned round, and prisoner took a running kick at him, injuring him so severely that he became unconscious. Prosecutor was picked up and taken into the house and was afterwards attended by Dr. Adams.—The remand (Vas granted. FIGHTING.—AIfred Beckett, better known as "Lawyer," 7, Arnold's-yard, who made his 13th appear- ance, was fined 10s. and costs for fighting in Scotland- road, contrary to the bvelaws, on July 2Sth.—John Weaver, 11, Turner-street, and George Daniels, 2, Pin- shop-yard; James Kirk, 8, Admiral-street and John Carey, 3, Gas-street, were each fined 7s. for fighting.— James McTichard, and Mary McTichard, 11, Haydock- street, had to pay 35. 6d each for a similar offence in Winwick-street. on Bank Holiday. DISORDERLY BEHAVIOUR.—The following were fined -3s. 6d. each for disorderly behaviour :—Thomas Newall, 1. Lomax's-yard, Al ice Newall, 54. Battersbv-lane, Robert Boscow, 18, Turner-street, Kate Newall,'l, Lomax's-yard, Mary Griffin, 10, Turner-street; and Delia Garrity, 12 Blackhurst-street, was fined 58. 6d.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton(in the chair), Dr. Fox, Messrs. Richardson and Holmes.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A TAliNEßY.—Frank Armstrong, 52, Leonard-street, and Joseph Caldwell, 152, Winwick-rd., were charged with stealing from Messrs. Arthur Waring and Co.'s tannery,Winwick-road,a quantity of provender, sacking and hair, and a wire screen of the value of Os.— Superintendent Monaghan said Armstrong had been employed by the firm as a carter for the past eighteen months. Caldwell worked for his father, who was a .carter, and occasionally had to cart from the tan-yard. Receiving certain information,Councillor John Roberts, the manager,instructed a foreman named Ruben Higham to secret himself on the premises during the dinner hour on Monday. Higham 'did so, and saw Caldwell enter the yard with his e.art. He was joined by Arm- strong and both men went into the stable and came out carrying a bag of corn which they put in the cart. Cald- well afterwards went to another part of the yard and loaded his cart with hair, old sacking,and a wire screen. He left the tannery but was followed, and both prisoners -were given into custody.—Councillor John Roberts said the firm did not wish to press the charge. Armstrong had a delicate wife, and some of his children were also in bad healr). Previously he had held a good character. —Prisoners ~-ere fined 10s. and costs (El in all) each.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair)</p>
        <p>and Mr. L. W. Reynolds.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED ASSAULT WITH A POKER.—Patrick Burke, fl.sh-hawker,-School-brow' was charged on a warrant with assaulting Daniel Rowlands, a neighbour' on Bank Holiday.—Mr. Jenkins prosecuted and Mr. Steel defended.—Mr. Jenkins, for the prosecution, stated that on the night in question prisoner came to the complain- ant's house and commenced to abuse him because he belonged to the Salvation Army. Afterwards he assaulted complainant's wife and daughter and then struck complainant a very violent blow on the head with a poker, rendering him unconscious. Rowlands was taken to the Infirmary by a constable,/ and it was found that the wound was a bad one, but not of a dangerous character. He had been unable to work for about 10 days. During the quarrel,the:prisoner received</p>
        <p>a thrashing from someone, but the prosecutor had nothing to do with it.—Daniel Rowlands sorroborated this statement.—At this stage Mr. Jenkins applied for an adjournment for a fortnight, stating that Mr. Steel in the meantime would try to arrange a settlement.— The Bench agreed to this course.</p>
        <p>THURSDA.Y.—Before Messrs. John Fairclough (in the chair). C. White, and W. Roberts. WINDOW-BREAKER SENTENCED.—Sarah Carrington, from St. Helens, was charged with breaking a window at the house of Ellen Wright, 21, Fennel-street, on Wed- nesday, and doing damage to the extent of ss. The prisoner smashed the window with a stone, saying she wanted to be locked up.—The prisoner, who had a bad record, was committed to gaol for two months. FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Roberts and Thornton, Messrs.</p>
        <p>Pilling, Hepherd and Burgess. NUISANCE ABATED.—lnspector Flood applied for the withdrawal of an adjourned summons against Mr. A. Mynekyme, Market-street, for creating a nuisance alleged to be injurious to health at Arpley. He said Mr. Mynekyme had decided not to carry on the business of glue-making agais.—The magistrates agreed to the withdrawal. _ _ _</p>
        <p>DESERTION OF WIFE AND CHILDREN.—Edward Jeffries was charged with running away from the borough, leaving his wife and three children chargeable to the Union.—Mr. B. Shearer said prisoner left the town in July, and the family.had to be admitted to the Workhouse. Prisoner probably went away in search of work, but did not make any arrangements. On return- ing he commenced work in town, but did not pay his wife anything.—Prisoner said he did not start work until Wednesday last.—The case was adjourned for a month in order to give prisoner a chance of supporting the family.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND AND WlFE.—lsaac Joseph Hurst, 4, Gilbert street, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife Elizabeth Hurst, 12, Atherton-street.—Complainant said defendant continually abused her.—He had made her black and blue and had threatened to kill her.—An adjournment was granted to give the parties an oppor- tunity of coming to terms. CASES Dlsm ISSED.—Several assault cases were heard but the magistates dismissed them all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH RESISTERS.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH RESISTERS.</p>
        <p>Three Watches Sold by Auction.</p>
        <p>Three watches taken in default of payment of the education rate from the Stockton Heath passive resisters, the Revs. J.S. Hu*hes and Herbert Dunnico and Mr. Law were:sold by auction in the Warrington Market place on Monday afternoon. Mr. William Hickman, who has officiated at several previous sales, gave his services as auctioneer, and was assisted by his son, Mr. Frank. Hickman. Not much interest was taken in the proceed- ings, the spectators consisting mainly of the attenders at Mr. Hickman's weekly sale which was interrupted for a short time whilst the watches were being disposed of. As usual, Mr. Hickman was in a cheery mood, and had no difficulty in keeping his audience in a good humour. In the course of a few preliminary remarks he said there would have to bea revolution in this country in connection with the" education business." In a short time we would have a new Government, and they would give us a a splendid Education Act—the right sort. "We auctioneers will then be out of work " he added, "and I hope you will pension me off for ever." In a few minutes the watches were knocked down for the exact amounts required for the Education rate. Mr. England purchased two at 15s. 4d. and 16s. 2d. res- pectively, and the other was bought by Mr. Edwin Brad- shaw, the assistant overseer, for 16s. 4d. The Rev. J. S. Hughes proposed a vote of thanks to the auctioneers, and to Mr. Bradshaw, for his courtesy throughout the arrangements. This passive resistance was a matter of conscience with them, and was their protest against what they regarded as the iniquitous Education Act. They were not going to make anything like a speech, as they were having a special demonstra- tion at Stockton Heach to declare their convictions. He might add that the Rev. H. Durinico would be back from his holiday in time for that meeting, when some statement would be made as to the stand they had been compelled to take on this question. Mr. Law seconded the motion, and said this was not the first occasion Mr. Hickman had sold passive resisters goods free of charge. They were very grateful to him. He did not know how many more times he would have to do it, but he was sure that when the electorate of this country had the opportunity given them of ex- pressing their opinion and giving their verdict on the Education Act it would be a very decided one. He felt sure that the next House of Commons would hays a very different complexion on it to the present one,and would hasten to amend the Act—which so many thousands had expressed their disapproval of, and which deprived them of-giving full expression to their conscientious convic- tion.</p>
        <p>The motion 'having been unanimously carried Mr. Frank Hickman returned thanks. Though he was not, perhaps, as conversant with the Act as some of those present, he thought the conscientious feelings of every- one ought to be respected. If they could get a Pal lia- ment of any colour to amend the act, let them have it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PROTEST MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE PROTEST MEETING.</p>
        <p>The three passive resisters of Stockton Heath with the assistance of the Rev. J. F. Matthews, of Latchford, and others propose holding an open-air meeting ia Victoria-square, Stockton Heath on Monday night at 7.30, if the weather is favourable, for the purpose of explaining their position, stating their views on the Education Act, and also, it is stated, in order to enlighten some of the ratepayers of the district as to the unfair position in which they and others are placed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>VOTES OF PASSIVE RESISTERS.</head>
        <p>THE VOTES OF PASSIVE RESISTERS.</p>
        <p>The passive resisters of Fulwood, near Preston,whose names have been removed from the register by the overseers, have signed claims which will be presented to the revising barrister, and he will be asked to decide whether non-payment of the portion of the poor rate devoted to educational purpow.s disfranchises them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF Mn. W. PICKTON.--it is with deep regret we announce the death of Mr: William Pickton, who passed away at his residence, Warrington-road, on Tues- day morning, at 3 a.m. The deceased gentleman had been under medical care for some time, but had recovered sufficiently to take his usual walks around the village. He was out on the Monday night, and his sudden death caused a painful surprise in the district. The deceased was well-known in Warrington and neighbourhood, and his fine presence, and his con- versational powers, made him a popular addition to any company. He had retired from business for some time, and his wife died, some 18 month ago. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at St. Mary's Cemetery, the Rev. J. R. ;Tones, M.A. officiating. Among those present were Mr. J. Seed, Manchester (brother-in-law), Mr. Alfred Seed(nephew), Mr. and Mrs. John Clacker, Messrs. D. Houghton, J. Sampson.J. W. Baker, and E. Foster. The bearers were Messrs. Joseph Tabberer, F. Dumbill, John Appleton, and A. Davies. The coffin was of polished oak, with handsome brass mountings, and the plate bore the inscription" William Pickton. died August 9th, 1901. Aged 75 years." Mr. James Edgerley had charge of the funeral arrangements, and there were a number of village people in the Cemetery to witness the last rites.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DEATH OF AN OLD WARRINGTON GUARDIAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD WARRINGTON GUARDIAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Clarke, of 17, Bridge-street Earlestown, who for 20 years was a member of the Warrington Board of Guardians passed away at his residence on Wednesday night in his 71st year, after a fortnight's illness. Deceased had been suffering from inflam- mation of the kidneys and was unconscious for nearly a week prior to his death. Mr. Clarke was born at Bridgemere, a small village near Nantwich in Cheshire in 1833, which place he left in 1851 to commence work as a blacksmit4 at the London and North-Western Railway Company's depot at Ordsall-lane, Manchester. Two years later the first batch of men were trans- ferred from Ordsall-lane to the Wagon Works at Earlestown and Mr. Clarke was included in the num- ber! After a short spell at the Viaduct Works, he launched out in business for himself as an ironmonger tinsmith and blacksmith, occupying a shop in Bridge- street, Earlestown. In six years this business increased so much that he built a lamer shop adjoining his old premises and this he occupied up to the time of his retirement into private life some four years ago. In 1885 ho was elected on the Warrington Board of Guardians, and he served on that body up to the time of his death. His aspirations in public life also led him to contest seats on the Improvement Commis- sioners of the District on two occasions, but on both occasions he suffered defeat. He was one of the earliest movers in the Primitive Methodist cause in the Earlestown district, and with other gentlemen was the means of placing that body on a .firm footing. He acted in various official capacities in connection with the now defunct Ratepayers' Association,whilst in his dealings with politics he was a staunch Liberal. The funeral will take place this (Saturday) afternoon at the Cemetery, Newton-le-Willows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Trip to Lymm.</head>
        <p>Trip to Lymm.</p>
        <p>On Monday, SO members of the West-street section of the Salvation Army had an enjoyable drive to Lymm. Mr. Winstanley's farm was the destination of the party, and here an .excellent tea was provided. Before leaving, an open-air meeting was held at the Cross, when songs and solos were rendered. Lieu- tenant Bowman had charge of the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S BATHS WANTED.</p>
        <p>Tha Auditors' Report.</p>
        <p>A STATEMENT ON THE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) presiding. There were also present :—The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Fairclough), Aldermen A. Bennett, John Evans, R. %V. Francomb, J. Smethurst T. H. Sutton; Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, W. Brough„ T. Burton, S. Davies, J. Dodds, D. S. Duncan, James Evans, L. Ellison, W. J. Forshaw, A. Horrocks, T. J. Hulse, J. Lord, J. Maginn, P. Peacock, J. Pemberton, J. Roberts, W. Rogers, J. Sankey, F. Stott, H. Thorn- ton, W. J. Wilson; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas (water engineer), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), J. G. Gornall (medical officer), J. M. Murray (director of education). AUDITORS' ANNUAL REPORT. COUNCILLOR DODDS EXPLAINS.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS, referrini-t;-&amp;-;;litors' report, said they would notice that theauditors called attention to the fact that a considerable sum was not paid within the financial year although the liability was incurred within the financial year; and the attention of the Finance Committee, and of the Council indeed, was called to a minute passed on February 6th, 1900, to the effect that heads of departments be instructed to get in accounts against the Corporation before the end of the financial year. He did not want to find fault with the auditors, but it seemed to him that they had gone outside their duty somewhat in taking up this position and referring to accounts which had not been paid. The Municipal Corporations Act 1882 provided only that the Treasurer should submit his accounts with the vouchers and papers to the Auditors. It was there- fore quite clear to him and he thought it would appear the same to every one of them that the treasurer's accounts were only such accounts as had been passed by the spending committees for payment and sent on to him, and he was not responsible for those which had not come before him duly passed by the respective committees. But neither he nor the Finance Committee wanted to find fault with the auditors for rooting into anything that they thought • might help the town and the Council in keeping .the officials up to the mark. So far as Mr. Fairhurst was concerned he had done all in his power to get all accounts in, and he would say in regard to trading or working or debit and credit profit and loss accounts there was practically nothing left out of the accounts at all. He complimented the Chairman of the Gas department on the circumstance that there was not £6O outstanding that ought to be included in their accounts. As business men they all knew how impossible it was to get people to send their accounts duly. He thought it was a compliment to the gas committee that they had done so wellas that and parti- cularly to Mr. Taylor, who was, he supposed, the head of the financial department there. In fact he believed £BO covered the whole of the items on trading account that were included in this £2,700. The bulk of it was for accounts that would go against the rates, and it would simply mean changing the liability from the people to whom they owed money at the end of the year—changing the liability and making the Council owe to the bank instead of them. They were £l,OOO further behind at the end of this year than they were for the previous one, and that was why the Finance Committee had instructed the Town Clerk to request the heads of departments to be more active in fu- ture in getting in their accounts,so as to have them all included in the financial statement for the year. He trusted that would satisfy everybody in regard to the remark of the auditors. There was no allusion this year, as there was in previous reports, to the amount of trading done through companies by members of the Council who were shareholders in those companies. There had in fact, been little or any of such trading. He was pleased at that, and he thought those members who didn't agree with him would agree in this. They could agree in this without changing their opinions. All of them would feel that it would be better if they could go on in that way, that was without any sash trading in the fa tare. (Hear, hear.) THE WATER SUPPLY. INIP3R.CAN V S PATE AEN BY AL9E2NIAN FRANCOMB. PROPOSED RESERVOIR AT ROWSWOOD.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Water Committee, on July 18th, the Town Clerk reported that Sir Gilbert Greenall had agreed to extend the time for the construction of the reservoir at Rowswood for four years after the expira- tion of the time fixed by the existing agreement. Alderman FRANCONIB, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said they considered Sir Gilbert's offer a very generous one. The Rowswood reservoir matter was not really before the Council at thi present time, but the reservoir would give the town water from a watershed covering 910 acres. The capacity of the reservoir would be 190 million gallons, and the time required for its completion five years, with another year for it to fill. The cost would be £90,000. It was intended solely for the use of manufacturers, and if they wanted Warring- ton to flourish they must have some smoke coming out of the tops of the chimneys, and they must supply the manufacturers with good water suitable for engine pur- poses. During the last few weeks,the Alderman continued, wehave been rather short of water, and we were afraid that the Appleton reservoir would become dry. As the works of our town are taking 500,000 gallons of this water daily it would have been a calamity, as large numbers of workmen would have been thrown out of employment. The weather has been exceptionally hot and dry, and it took Winwick all its time to supply the domestic requirements of the town. Last year we expected to receive a large extra supply from our new wells at Winwick, but this has not been realized,as the work is not complete. It is quite true that we are pumping about 400,000 gallons per day from Delph-lane, but this quantity is sent to Hill Warren reservoir, and gives the people of Stockton Heath an excellent supply of Winwick water, while the people of Warringtun have never yet received any benefit from the new water scheme. You see from the local papers that Penketh and Sankey have been short of water during the past few weeks, and, although the Water Committee have done all in their power to assist them, they have found it an impossibility to do so. The two districts are sup- plied by gravitation from the reservoir at Winwick, and the pressure has not been sufficient to force the water into the tanks of the houses. This reservoir has been as low as 7ft. 9in., and consequently the pressure has been very low. It is now 18ft., and. consequently, Penketh and Sankey should have a good supply. We believe our troubles are now over, and that we shall have an abund- ant supply of water for many years to come. At the present time we have a new reservoir which has not yet been of any service tiv us and which will hold 4A million gallons. As this is joined to our other reservoir at Winwick, which holds 21 million gallons, you can easily see what an advantage this will be in any time of emergency. Delph-lane pumping station is situated 11 miles from Winwick pumping station, and is connected with the reservoirs at Winwick bymeans of 20 inch and 27 inch pipes. It is capable of delivering three million gallons to Winwick daily if required. In addition to Delph-lane Ave have three extra wells, 400 yards from each other, which will soon be connected by an adit 6ft. high and 4ft. wide. There is only 90ft. between wells No 1 and No 2 and 20ft. between wells N 0.3 and No 4to complete the scheme. At the present time we are pumping from No. 2 and No. 3 wells 3,000,000 gallons of water per clay,and this is all running to waste in the various 'itches in the neighbourhood of Winwick We are confident of having an abundant supply of water, and we have the analysis of Professor Boyce, of the University College, Liverpool, which is so good that your Water Committee are perfectly satisfied with its purity. Alderman EVANS seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS said a year ago they had a proposal to spend some £70,000 on the reservoir at Daresbury. Now they were told they had ample water for many years to come. He wanted to know if there was any necessity for going on with it.</p>
        <p>Aldernaan SiIETHURST: We have four years in' which to consider it.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB said the committee had plenty of water for domestic purposes, but it was well known that Appleton water was very much better for manufac- turing purposes. The water in the Appleton reservoir was very low at the present time, there being only 9,000,000 gallons—a quantity that would last them several weeks. They were hoping that that quantity would last them out. If it did not they would have to supply manufacturers with Winwick water. It was a question whether it would not be wise to make' the reservoir at Rowswood. He thought they ought to look ahead. The, Daresbury scheme which had been referred to was hatched when he was ill and he never knew much about it.</p>
        <p>-ihe minutes were then passed. ALDERAtAN BENNETT AND THE PADGATE TRAMWAY.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST proposed the adoption of the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee. Alderman BENNETT drew attention to the statement which had been prepared by the Borough Treasurer showing the approximate cost of each tramway route, based upon the length of single track, and also showing the estimated sinking fund. The sinking fund on the Cemetery route, he said, was less per car mile than any of the other routes, and if they considered the receipts they would find that this route was the best pay!ng line of the whole system. He considered that the Padgate- lane residents had been badly treated. They hadapplied for an extension of the line from the Cemetery. The MikYoa : This is not on the minutes. Mr. Alderman BENNETT : Really unless you can illus- trate your remarks with examples it is of little nee speaking. Continuing, Mr. Bennett said that in Stock- ton Heath the Corporation had gone outside the boundary with the tramways, whilst a small concession had been refused to the residents of Padgate-lane who were inside the Borough. The minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>VIE HEALTH COMMITTEE. COMPENSATION FOR THE SEIZURE Olv CATTLE</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee it was resolved that the tender of the National Telephone Co., Ltd., to• supply, deliver, and complete a telephonic installation at the fever hospital, Aikin-street, at .C 42 according to specification, be accepted. A letter was read from the Clerk to the London County Council acknowledging receipt of the Town Clerk's letter of the 29th ultimo, and stating that he had taken steps to place the letter before the Chairman of the Select Committee on the Tuberculosis (Animals) Compensation Bill in order that the Committee may be acquainted with the views of the Town Council of Warrington. It was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to prepare plans for the erection of an administrative block at the Sanatorium and that the Medical Officer of Health be instructed to consult with the Borough Surveyor as to the details. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Alderman I Surrox said the telephonic installation would enable the members of the staff in the different departments of the hospital to communicate with each other more; readily than they were able to do at present. The ; hospital buildings were extensive and it was necessary i in order that the duties might be satisfactorily per- formed that the matron and nurses in each department should be able to speak to each other without having to send messengers. in regard to the letter from;the London County Council jrc Tuber- culosis (animals), Compensation Bill, the views of the Committee were that some compensation should be given by the Government to parties sustaining loss through being compelled to destroy the carcasses of cows found to be affected with tuberculosis. A butcher purchasing a beast which had all the appearance of a fine, healthy cow, very often found on its being killed that. it wasa.ffected with tuberculosis. The animal had to be destroyed, and the loss fell upon the butcher. The committee were therefore of opinion that the Govern- ment should be asked to pass some measure to give relief to purchasers under such circumstances. Perhaps if the farmer or the party who sold the cow was called upon to contribute towards the loss it might have the effect of making him more careful in endeavouring to prevent the disease by providing proper cowsheds. In regard to the proposed administration block, the Local Government Board required that such a building should be erected. Councillor EVANS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor PETER PEACOCK moved that the minute respecting the telephonic installation be referred back, and that the Corporation Electricity Department should do the work. Alderman SUTTON said it was on the advice of the electricity department that tenders were invited, and the Telephone Company's was the lowest. The elec- tricity department could not do the work for that sum. The amendment was withdrawn, and the minutes were approved. An interesting discussion on the health of the borough is reported in another column. NIORE ABOUT TEM PAILS CONTACT. THE CONDITION OF SANKEY BROOK.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of th 3 Sanitary Works Committee a letter (printed in our last issue) was re td from the solicitor to Messrs. Rhodes and Son, Stourbridge, com- plaining that a statement made by Councillor Stott with reference to the contract for pails and ashbins was en- tirely void of foundation and its circulation had injured the firm's business. The Council was asked to pass, a resolution to the offect that the allegation that the firm was n.t payingitherecognized rate of wages was not made upon reliable information.—lt was resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to reply stating that in view of the fact that the Council had passed a resolution accepting their tender for the supply of pails and ashbins was in itself sufficient to prove that they believed that the firm paid the recognized rate of wages in the district. Mr. Ruidock attended the committee,and reported fully upon the salvias of water taken from Sankey Brook, which the had treated on a temporary bacteria hed with very satisfactory -esalta . After discussion the further consideration of the matter was deferred. Alderman EV:tNS moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said the committee were of opinion that some good would corns from the treatment of samples from Sankey Brook. They were hopeful that the effluent now turned into th 3 brook would be very much improved. Alderman SMETHURST seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT supported the minutes because he strongly objected to the Council doing what Messrs. Rhodes and Sem asked through their solicitor. He con- tended that his statement at the Council .meeting was not absolutely void of fouadation. With regard to the argument that the circulation of his state:nent had injured the firm's business, he would p-,int out that he did not even get a seconder vhen he moved that the resolution be referred back, and that ought to be stelicient to prove that the Council did not agree with him. He alone was not going to accept the responsibility for any loss to the firm. That, be con- tended, had resulted from the issuing of a circular by an association of men in the district containing a list of "fair "firms. Messrs. Rhodes and Son were not on that list which had been sent to every Trades Council and to members of Town and City Councils which dealt in that class of goods. The minutes were approved. BATHS FOR WORKINGMEN WANTED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee, a letter was rea.d from Mr. Councillor Stott requesting a reduction in the charges for the use of the Baths and suggesting the provision of open-air swimming baths: It was resolved that the Town Clerk inform Mr. Stott that the matter to which he referred had already received the careful consideration of the committee and that they could not at the present time entertain the proposals. The IVIAvon. moved and Alderman SMETHURST seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor STOTT moved that the resolution with reference to the baths be referred back.</p>
        <p>The 111.1yoR said the committee had discussad the provision of open-air baths when Mr. Jenkins was a member of the Council, but they came to the conclu- sion that such baths were unsatisfactory. He (the Mayor) saw an open-air bath in a field the other day,and a dead dog was floating in it. (Laughter.) The con- dition of the water was such that it must be injurious. If that was a sample of open-air baths,Warrington would be better without them.</p>
        <p>Councillor PETER. PEACOCK said the charges for the public baths were high. The committee ought to en- courage rather than discourage bathing. The 'MAYOR: We have,no objection if some of you will come and conduct the business of the committee and show us how to do it better.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEAoocK: We are sent here as repre- sentatives of the ratepayers, and we are allowed to make suggestions, although they are very often ignored or sent back. When councillors make suggestions they ought to be treated in the spirit in which they are intended.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: What do you mean by that? I say distinctly we bave gone into the subject thoroughly; in fact in reference to baths we have allowed a concession to Crostield's— _</p>
        <p>--Councillor PEACOCK-:. I am not talking about Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT said that in his letter he asked that the charges for vapour and slipper baths should be reduced. Many deputations had asked him to try and obtain the reduction especially as regarded the slipper baths, as the price was prohibitive to the working classes. In many other towns the charges were much lower—in fact, he did not know of any other town or the size of Warrington where there were less facilities for slipper and vapour baths, in regard to the charge. It was too much for the average labouring man to have to pay sixpence and a shilling. If the reason for the refusal to grant concessions was that theicommittee would lose a little money,let them charge for admission to the parks, museum, and art gallery. The baths werejust AS great a necessity. Councillor PEMBERTON said it was for the public health that the working-clames should have bathing facilities. In Liverpool, workingmen had for many years been able to obtain a bath for one penny, but they had t take their own soap and towels. The MAYOR: There are often twenty or thirty people waiting to get into the private baths at Warrington. You chin% expectiwe are going to reduce the charge and have fifty or a hundred waiting at half price. Councillor PEMBERTON : We should borrow money and provide more baths. The MAYOR: Exactly, but we have never been asked to do so.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he had been in the Council 10 years, and he remembered Mr. Jenkins bringing for- ward the matter of open-air baths. At that time a difficulty was experienCed in finding a suitable site. The MA. YOR : We have the same difficulty now. Alderman Sm ETH URST : We have better public baths now than then.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : The Mayor has just told us that the slipper baths are too well filled. The MAYOR : Are we to provide more baths for ques- tionable characters who leave disreputable things behind them?</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : They would leave fewer dis- reputable things behind them if you provided provision for their cleanliness. He did not think they were doing enough for the working-3lasses in this matter. The MAYOR: They are not all of the working-class, some of them are tranlps.</p>
        <p>--Aid;;;;;;T3.--s-Err- (Continuing), said no money could be better spent than in enabling the working-classes to have the privilege of an occasional bath. Alderman SUTTON stated that the baths were already costing the ratepayers a considerable sum of money. The Corporation had been losing between £sooand £6OO a year on the baths. Members of the Council had told the ratepayers that they were going to reduce the rates and yet they are continually shouting for more expendi- ture. Councillor DODDS : I should like the Alderman to name those who have so expressed themselves. Alderman SUTTON: Everyone of you; yourself included. (Laughter.) I don't exclude you any more than anyone else. You told the ratepayers a good deal of what you were going to do. What I have said applies to almost every member of the Council. Councillor DODDS I challenge Alderman Sutton or any other member of the Council to prove that I have ever promised a reduction of the rates. The MAYOR: He did not speak of you in particular. Councillor DODDS : He said ' Alderman SirrToN I said everyone. You promised to do all you could in the interests of the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS : What I said was : "Do not expect that the rates are going to be reduced. The rates, I added, are bound to go up on account of what has been going on." It is unfair for the Alderman to say "You are one who promised a reduction of rates," when as a matter of fact I distinctly stated the contrary. (Hear, hear.) A vote was then taken. There voted for the amend- ment: Alderman Bennett, Councillors Dodds, Peacock, Lord, Sankey, Pemberton, Stott, and Horrocks. The MAYOR: The amendment is lost. THE MANCHESTER sare CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in moving t'ae confirmation of the minutes of the Rivers Committee, said they had managed to do their best for the ratepayers in reference to the Man- chester Ship Canal Bill. He did not know whether they had pleased everyone. He expected someone would move a vote of censure upon them for doing their best. (Laughter.) Alderman FRANCOMB seconded. The MAYOft : Wnere is the vote of censure? (A laugh.) The minutes were then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>A writer in a trade contemporary is of opinion that, water cooling is essential on a motor cycle of,or exceed- h.p., and is indispensable when the machine is fitted with any form of carriage attachment. In the face of this pronouncement it is curious to reflect that, at the recent trials held at Godstone for this particular type of machine, not one of the cycles having • water cooling, managed to complete the first fifty miles. The stoppings were not solely due to complications with the water arrangements; but the fact that each one should have been obliged to succumb comparatively early in the day suggests that the majority of them are not yet so designed as to give in practice all the theoretical advantages of water cooling.</p>
        <p>It is also extraordinary to remember that the only tri-carl which has distinguished itself in open competi- tion this year is the " Trimo." This was the sole machine of the tri-car class to reach Edinburgh within the specified 22 hours upon the occasion of the Motor • Cycling Club's run from London. Moreover, it scored ! in the Auto-Cycle Club's hill-climb near Goff's Oak, and won the gold medal in the Passenger Trialsit is provided • with a fan special for cooling the motor, and its long list of successes seem to point to the fact that,properly designed, the motor cycle, of even high h.p., will be quite satisfactory with an air-cooled engine.</p>
        <p>! A crude idea for strengthening the ordinary diamond- framed. in order to render it suitable for a motor has just been published. It is nothing more than supple- menting the lower down tube with two lengths of stout wire, secured at one end to the bottom of the sOering socket and at the other to the bracket. A patent is ap- plied for. Riders who are taken by the scheme, but cannot afford to pay the price of this patent frame, can , adopt the idea to their existing mounts by substituting ' string or crochet cotton. Either of these would lessen the tendency to fracture just as much— or as little—as the advocated wire and are within the reach of the slenderest purse. 'Of course, however much any one partictilar tube is strengthened, the fact remains that the diamond frame represents a rough square in outline, and a square is the weakest figure in mechanics, just as a three-sided figure is the strongest. Therefore, the correct thing to do is to triangulate the frame itself, and not to attempt to , bolster up a weak design with useless additions which achieve nothing.</p>
        <p>As we have predicted would be the case, there are signs of the general affection for the honeycomb type of radiator waning. This is hardly to be wondered at. The" Loyal "radiators, first introduced into England upon the Decauville cars some few years ago, have always been found most satisfactory, and, moreover, are easy to repair. The honeycomb type, on the other hand whilst perhaps presenting a somewhat neater appear- ance, are practically done for should they be damaged in a collision, and this, taken in conjunction with the fact that, in the first instance they are espensive,renders their presence a rather costly item upon a car.</p>
        <p>By the way, a word in connection with the fan customarily used with this pattern radiator may not be out of place here. There is a certain amount of adjustment allowed for the chain, which must be taken up from time to time as it becomes slack. When the limit has been reached, and the chaift becomes loose—for every chain will stretch, but, being non-elastic, will not recover—adopt the same plan as one would were it fitted to a cycle. Remove one of the links and again alter the adjustments so that now there is plenty to take up again as the stretching increases.</p>
        <p>The mania for touring up and down the mountain-side is still rampant with a certain section of the motoring community, although little is gained save advertise- ment—of a kind—for the car which is used for the en- terprise. Putting aside any consideration of the fool- hardiness of such an undertaking, it is not fair to the more legitimate users of the narrow mountain roads. The sudden appearance of a car, with its throbbing engine and the impervious tooting of its hooter has hitherto been quite unknown in such regions, and might be quite sufficient to cause a terrible accident when encountered by the astonished horses attached to a diligence, or carriage. Considering that many of these roads skirt a precipice, from which there is little or no protection afforded it is really a mystery that the recent incursion of the motor into the mountain fastnesses has not been attended with more serious results.</p>
        <p>There-is no doubt, of course, that the feats in them- selves have been magnificent. Captain Deasy's tour across the Alps, for instance, has been a splendid ex- position of the pluck of a man and the reliability of a. motor. But if the reliability can be equaly well ex- p loited in some less risky way, is it not better still? The amateur driver, for instance,who has just published the result of a holiday trip to Scotland and back on his 10 h.p. Decauville, which he drove himself through- out the whole of the trip, really does more to show the actual reliability of his particular car, because the ex- cellence of its running has not been due in any way to the presence of a motor expert at the wheel.</p>
        <p>Dunn" the whole of . the distance th;s gentleman covered, the only attention the engine required was the adjustment of the cummutator spring. Tyre troubles— to the extent of a couple of punctures—of course inter- vened, but these cannot 1313 counted against the engine, Automobile Club practices notwithstanding. More- over, the car seems to have talTi the hills in splendid style and to have come home going fully as strongly as when she started. It is such a trip as this that gives the public confidence in an engine, because she is driven, as we have said, under normal touring con- ditions without being specially tuned up or overhauled before setting forth, and is .controlled by quite an average amateur having no special experience of motors.</p>
        <p>A great many hard and fast rules have been drawn up for the Auto Cycle Club's 1,000 miles trials which will have just commenced by the time that these words are in type. It is to be hoped that in the multitude of councillors there will be found to be wisdom and not merely organised muddle as was the case last year. We shall not readily forget how one competitor arrived at a control, according to the time-sheets, before he bad been timed out of the last ! This discrepancy sounds rather like a looking-glass" experience, but it was merely due to/the fact that it had not occurred to the time-keepers to compare watches. When suchan import- ant item as this is omitted, any one, who is an old hand at road-racing, is justified in feeling a little dubious concerning the management,and sceptical with regard to the ultimate good to be derived from the experience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>FARMERS AND THE MARKET.—Several cases of in- terest to farmers attending the Stockton Heath market were down for hearing at theDaresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesdaybef ore Mr. W. Lon g (in the chair),and other magis trates.—John Higgins, Albert-road, Widnes was sum- moned for being away from his horse and lurry and not having proper control at Stocktnn Heath on July 18th. —Sergeant Barlow proved the case and said there was a great deal of traffic and it was necessary to keep the road clear.—Defendant said there were some fifty or a hundred other farmers attended the market and had been accustomed, since the market was established to leave their vehicles on the side of the road, while they bought and sold. No complaint had ever been made previously. —The Clerk said there were several other cases similar to the present and perhaps it would be better to have them decided together.—Superintendent Ennion stated that no warnings had been sent out previously.—The Chairman said that as the Bench understood no warning had been given by the police and considering that the market had been going on for so many years, they would dismiss the case that day, but they hoped that the i farmers would take t as a proper warning in the future. The Bench would deal with the cases as they thought fit.—Mr. Browne, so!tor, said be had been instructed to defend a case against Mr. Wright, of Antrobus.—Mr. Long : Well, our decision now will be a fair and proper warning for the future.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, the Chapel-en-le-Frith magistrates imposed a fine of 10s. and costs on John Laidlaw for taking two small trout, value Gd., from a brook at Castleton close to the military camp. Defendant, who belongs to the 2nd Durham Light Infantry, wrote expressing his regret and offering to pay any fine that might be imposed. The magistrates' clerk said it was a wonder the soldier did not frighten the fish away.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS, KNEE CAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicos Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Leg, Sprains, Sr,c. They are porous, light in texture' and inexpensive and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking.—Prices 4s. 6d., 78. 6d. and 103. 6d. each. Postage free.—WHITE &amp; WRIGET, Surgical Instrument and Truss Makers, 98,Renshaw at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CROWDED WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>A CROWDED WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ADDITIONAL ACCOMMODATION. New Building Decided Upon.</p>
        <p>The proposal 9f the Warrington Board of Guardians to erect a nect building, intermediate in character between the Infirmary and Workhouse proper, for the reception of 100 aged and infirm inmates, was again under consideration at the meeting of the Board on Wednesday. Mr. A. Brooks presided, and there were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Miss Broadbent, Rev. Father Mercer, Dr. Murray, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. W. Hick- man, A. Jolley, J. Payton, S. Davies, T. H. Ashcroft, J. Billinge, William Houghton, G. Mather, J. Ball, J. Turton, J. W. Knowles, G. Thompson, R. Forshaw, W. Flannaghan, R. Bolton, J. Backhouse, P. Longton. and J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent Padgat(a schools),_ _ _</p>
        <p>THE BANK BALANCE. AN INCREASED CALL TO BE MADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN asked what balance there would be at the bank after the day's cheques had been paid. The CLERK replied that the balance at the bank was £4,567 Bs. The cheques .that day amounted to £l5OO, leaving a balance of £3,000. The Clerk •added that all the calls had been paid with the exception of Croft amounting to £4O. Mr. HICKMAN : That means that we have only £4O to come in, and that we have to carry on the work of the current half year with our bank balance. The CLERK said he would have to make a very early call on the townships, and he would have to increase the call no doubt. Instead of having a balance of £2OOO at the end of the half year, as anticipated, it would be quite as much as they could do to meet current expenses. He was also afraid that when they received the County rate call they would find it had been very much increased owing to the Education rate. This would mean an increased call on the townships.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MR. JENNER FUST.</head>
        <p>MR. JENNER FUST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenner Fust, Local Government Board inspector, wrote stating that owing to the death of his father, it would be impossible for him to attend the Board that day. Mr. FlicKmAN asked by whose authority, Mr. Jenner Fust was invited to be present. The CLERK explained that he received a letter from the Master stating that the Building Committee had unanimously decided to write Mr. Jenner Fust to meet them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Mr. HICKMAN: When did the committee meet?</head>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN: When did the committee meet?</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father MERCER said the decision was arrived at at the last meeting when there were seven or eight members present. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY observed that if any one challenged the accuracy of the statement he was prepared to deal with it.</p>
        <p>Mr. KNONN LES said he did not suppose the members challenged the accuracy of it, but they challenged the fairness of it until they got a proper explanation. The Clerk read a letter from Messrs. William and Seger Owen, acknowledging the receipt of a letter appointing them architects for the proposed new build- ing at the Workhouse. It was always a pleasure, they stated, to undertake any work for the Board.</p>
        <p>MR. HICKMAN AND THE PROPOSED ADDITIONAL BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>MOTION TO RESCIND THE BOARD'S DECISION. In accordance with notice of motion, Mr. Hickman moved that the resolution of the Board at the last meeting relating to the erection of additional buildings be rescinded. In advancing 'reasons why the Board should do so, Mr. Hickman said there was only a small attendance at the last meeting of the Board when they were passed, owing to the holidays. He contended that not sufficient tine had elapsed for the members of the Board to form an accurate opinion as to whether it was Inecessary to erect such a building as that proposed. Last winter was a very lung and cold one and he ad- mitted that they had an increase in the number of inmates in the hospital and the other parts of the house Several times before in the history of that union there had been a rush of inmates into the house in consequence of bad trade, but in the course of a few months it had righted itself and the number in the house had become normal. He contended that they ought to wait another six months and see what their experience was during the winter. He did not think they had exhausted their ingenuity in this matter. A building of the kind proposed would be a comparative failure. They would require a building not for one hundred persons but five hundred. The in- mates would not come from the infirmary but from the workhouse. He believed that the old infirmary block could be made use of for the purpose required.ln going through it that morningr he found it was being used as a paint shop,joiners shop,store-room,fitting shop, carpen- ters shop, and one or two small storerooms. There were also some empty rooms and one large room which was only partly furnished, containing seven beds. He con- tended that this building was sufficient for their pur- poses. The workshops could be removed to a corrugated iron building to be erected in the grounds at a tithe of the expense required for the proposed new building. Another reason why the building should be deferred was the present high rate of interest which would be demanded for a loan. The Board succeeded in borrow- ing the money for the new infirmary at 2/ per cent—a very good stroke of business—but they could not borrow to-day at anything like that rate of interest. They would have to pay 4 or 4-1 per cent. The financial posi- tion of the Board did not warrant them spending money on buildings so long as they could manage to meet the difficulty of accommodation by alterations and improve- ments to the old block of infirmary buildings Ratepayers were groaning under the weight of heavy taxation at the present time. There were many poor people outside the Workhouse, some of whom had been obliged to sell their " traps " in order to pay their rates. There were tradesmen also who were paying their heavy rates with their creditors money. It. behoved the Guardians to endeavour to relieve the burden of the rates. Since 1898, the Board had built a new infirmary, a new laundry and workshops and sheds. for stone-breaking. They had purchased a new weigh- ling machine and spent £3OO on asphalting. He ventured to state that the Guardians would not have expended their own money at that rate, and he hoped they would avoid putting any more buildings down in that corner of the earth. (Applause.) Mr. JOSHUA BALL, in seconding, said they had heard from the Clerk that the ensuing precepts would be larger,andlif the Board went on spending money as they had been doing, they would have to build one huge Workhouse and all become paupers or officials. There were many poor people outside the Workhouse who had a difficulty in paying their rates. He thought the pro- ject might be deferred fora short time, even if some of the inmates were cramped a little. (Hear, bear). Mr. S. DAVIES said he could not understand why there should be all this hurry to go into this expenditure. It almost made members of the Board wicked enough to think that there was some personal interest to serve. He hoped that might not be so. He saw no reason for this hurry, though it must be patent to every member that they had passed through a winter, such as perhaps none of them had seen before or would for many years see again. It might not recover this next winter, but he contended that they would be in no worse position as a Board than last winter in regard to accommodation. Considering the financial straits of many tradespeople, the Board ought to hesitate before launching out into any scheme that would increase the present high rates. He had Mr. Jenner Fust's authority for saying that the present was not an opportune time for going into an expendi- ture of this kind. He (Mr. Davies) was of opinion that with careful supervision they might go on for the next 8 or 10 years. He would like to ask the Clerk what would be the annual charge on the poor rate if the building cost from .£lO,OOO to £15,000. The CLERK said he did not think he could borrow the money at less than four per cent, and the money and interest would have to be repaid in a period of 30 years Mr.IGEonGETHOMPSON pointed out that the decision of the House Committee was not a unanimous one. ' There were five for, three against and one did not vote. He heard a member of the committee say that he would be loyal to the committee. He (Mr. Thompson) wanted to be loyal to the ratepayers (hear, hear). He did not think the Workmen's Compensation Act had resulted in a number of old men being thrown out of work, and caused a greater number of indoor paupers. The Board had spent something like £60,000 on new buildings dur- ing the last few years. Mr. HICKMAN : More than that. Mr. THOMPSON contended that the Board ought to hesitate before increasing the expenditure. He was in favour of the old infirmary buildings being used in preference to any more building operations. The work- shops should be away from the house, for he felt that it was very dangerous to have paint stores and joiners shops in the centre of a block of buildings. Mr. J. W. KNOWLES commented upon the danger alluded to, and said if a fire broke out there it would be attended with serious consequences. He felt that whatever accommodation the Guardians provided at the Workhouse they would always be able to find paupers to fill it. He contended that there were some artisans out of work who would not take up employment outside their own particular trade. He was in favour of the Guardians providing relief in kind on the understand- ing that the able-bodied men who applied for it should be obliged to do some work in return for it. By this means they would be able to deal with men who could work but who would not. He considered drink and gambling were responsible for much of the poverty which existed. Mr. Knowles commented upon the period of depression through which we were passing and characterised Mr. Hickman's statement as "able eloquent and fetching." Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said the unfortunate House Com- mittee appeared to be receiving very severe castigation because it had done its duty. He suggested that it was not only desirable to feel strongly on this matter, but to think clearly. (Hear, hear.) Not a single argument had been brought forward for rescinding the resolution passed at the last meeting—a resolution passed not by a narrow majority but without a dissenting voice</p>
        <p>'Aruch had been said against the proposal on financial grounds, but he would like to point out that the financial standpoint was most carefully considered by the Com- mittee. What was being advocated was being advocated in the interest of economy. It was quite possible to avoid spending money on the erection of the proposed building, but if the Board thought that was going to save them, they were shutting their eyes to facts. When they were unable to receive paupers into the house, the only other alternative was to pay much more in out - relief. They could not get away from their respon- sibility. The proposal of Mr. Knowles to choke off the able-bodied had nothing to do with the present scheme. The new buildings were for the aged, sick and infirm. .The administration of the house had been greatly im- proved during the past few years and it would have been improved much more rapidly but for the opposition which Mr. Knowles had some years ago to the scheme of improved classification. MI-. Knowles afterwards admitted that he was mistaken.</p>
        <p>Mr. KNOWLES : Tackle your subject and not quite so much Mr. Knowles. Mr.A. J. JOLLEY continuing said Mr. Knowles had told them that the Guardians made the house too c om- fortable and at the same time told them that the prison was more comfortable than the workhouse. (Laugh" ter.) Mr. KNOWLES : I said some people preferred prison to work.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he had no desire to misrepre- • sent Mr. Knowles. He agreed with the contention that it would be better to remove the workshops from the centre of a block of buildings, but were those who ad- vocated it prepared to carry it to its logical conclusion and propose that workshops be erected in another part of the grounds. If so he would be prepared to support them. Mr. Ball had asked whether they could not keep the inmates cramped in the building fur another winter. Even the waiting rooms for the outdoor poor were used to provide sleepily,' accommodation. Did the members who opposed the building extention, think that a cold: draughty room,which with 20 people sitting in it woula be inconveniently crowded was good enough to provide sleeping accommodation for fourteen paupers. Mr. BALL said many a hard working man had in c,asea - - of sickness to be inconveniently cramped in his home, and why should not paupers be cramped occasionally. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said it seemed to be assumed that the difficulty was of a temporary character. There svae the strongest evidence that it was of a permanent charac- ter. For Feveral winters there had been overcrowding. It was of a progressive character. The population Of the Union had increased 50 per cent within the last two census periods and it was growing rapidly. He did not consider that Mr. Hickman had treated his colleagues fairly in this matter. Mr. HicxmAri: I have an answer to that. Mr. JOLLEY contended that there had been no hurrY in the matter. The decision was come to after long, patient and careful consideration. He should consider himself unworthy of the confidence of the House Com- mittee if he advocated anything he did not believe Was in the best interests of the Union and the most economical for the ratepayers. The CHAIRMAN said those who had spoken in support of Mr. Hickman's resolution seemed to think that the were the only economists. The members of the sub- committee were equally anxious to be economists on behalf of the ratepayers and to be equally lord to them. It was merely a matter of opinion as to which was the most economical policY. With the exception of Mr. Davies, whose support of tbe resolution was very half-hearted, there was not one vain had spoken who Nias thoroughly acquainted with the wants of the house or attended to their duties. Hickman was like a will-o'the-wisp. He had been absent for months together and admitted ignorance of thing° which had been before the committee for months Past. Those who knew the most were those who had made the recommendation. Mr. Jenner Fust had told them that the Local Government Board were receiving complaint° from the inspectors that there was not sufficient accom- modation. Was it better to provide the accominn" dation under pressure or do it more economically with- out pressure. Mr. BILLINGE spoke in support of the committee'S • proposals and pointed out that the amount paid in olit- relief had steadily increased owing to the lack ef accommodation at the house. Mr. FORSHAW contended that it was false econornYte pay out-relief instead of being in aposition to offer indoor relief. The CHAIRMAN informed the Board that during the past two years out-relief had increased by £5OO per annum. Mr. HOUGHTON stated that having regard to the_ increase of population it was in the interests of the, Board to make further provision for indoor acconime; dation. It was his experience that those unions wbie had plenty of indoor accommodation were the Wet economical. Mr. HICKMAN replied to the criticisms which ht been made. He did not think the financial arguing_ ought to be ridiculed as it had been. Referring to lir irregular attendance at the committee meetings, Air. Hickman said no one knew better than the Chairman the causes which had prevented him from attending: For two years he had been ill, and twelve months Or 'morning his wife died. He contended that his natal', ances would compare favour4bly with those of 00'32 members and he would like the attendances to be Pile; lished. He had previously suggested the utilisation n't the old infirmary, but there was a determinationamong°, certain members of the sub-committee not to suppop anything but what they themselves wanted. On a vote being taken, the following Hickman's resolution : Messrs. Hickman, Knowles, Dr. Mercer, Longton, Thompson, Ashcroft, Davies, Bolton, Ball and Payton (10). voted for U. The CHAIRMAN announced that the amendment WO lost. Mr. HICKMAN withdrew two other motions relating to the proposed extensions.</p>
        <p>THE PRICE OE AN INVENTORY. • Mr. HICKMAN then moved that a resolution granting the Master £2.5 for preparing an inventory of the Work: house be rescinded. He contended that a professional valuer would have done te work for .215. Mr. Jolley said the Master had paid £lO for assistancoe Mr. AsHcuorr seconded the proposition, and said was too much. They could have got the work done 10` £5. On'a vote being taken the resolution was lost,and tho Board rose. Mr. HICKMAN announced that it was his intention Ve retire from the Building Committee and also from tu Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BETTER THAN MONEY IN THE SAVINGS</head>
        <p>BETTER THAN MONEY IN THE SAVINGS</p>
        <p>BANK.</p>
        <p>The working man to-day gives more attention w the food he has to eat than his forefathers gave as bte., realizes that his health—his bank—depends verilargelJ upon the properties of what he eats and drinks. It follows, then, that the popular article of food i° that which is easy to digest; but more popular still ar: preparations which are found to aid and strengthen ttte digestive organs, and also to act as an energiser for whole system. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is such a food. It places a means in the hands of everyone to bnilc! up and maintain a strong constitution, which enablse' its possessor to travel his life's journey without tile. aches and pains which are in so many cases preventabled Thus we come round again to sound common sense base on experience..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>N.E. LANARK ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE N.E. LANARK ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Another Liberal Triumph. Polling took place on Wednesday in the North-E°t be Lanarkshire Division to fill the vacancy caused by t death of Sir William H. Rattigan,the Unionist meelbeo for the division. There was three candidates—Provo Findlay (Liberal), Mr. G. A. Touch ((Jnionist). and Or' J. Robertson (Labour.) The result of the poll was declared at midnight a° follows : Provost Findlay (L) G. A. Touch (U) J. Robertson (Lab.)</p>
        <p>__— Liberal majority over Unionist 942</p>
