      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>FOUND BURIED IN A SAND PIT.</head>
        <p>FOUND BURIED IN A SAND PIT.</p>
        <p>A Girl's Unfortunate End.</p>
        <p>The circumstances attending the death of a little girl named Edith Harriet Jenks, 4 years of age, daughter of John Henry Jenks, wiredrawer, 15, Athergill-street, whose body was found in a sand pit,off Marsehouse-lane on Saturday morning, were revealed at an inquiry con- ducted by Mr. Brighouse on Tuesday afternoon.—Mr. F. G. Taylor (Messrs. H. Greenall and Company) solicitor' watched the proceedings on behalf of Colonel Ireland Blackburne, the landowner. It appears that on Saturday morning about eleven o'clock, a ten-year-old boy named Horace Povey, son of William Povey, G, Marsh-street went out for the purpose of getting some soil with which to pot some plants. He was digging in the pit when, on turning the soil over, he came across a portion of a girl's dress. He informed Mrs. Whitfield wife of Henry Whitfield, a wiredrawer, living at 9, Fothergill-street, who proceeded to the scene. On turning over 14 cwt. of soil which covered the child, it turned out that the body was that of Edith Harriet Jenks, who had been missing from her home since the previous evening.—Evidence of identification was given by Mrs. Jenks,who stated that she last saw her daughter alive at 4.30 on Friday afternoon' when she was playing with, other children in the field in front of the house. When she did not return, a search was made in the district, but without resalt.—ln answer to the Coroner's inquiries, it was stated that the seam of sand in the pit was Mt. 6in. high. Mr. Taylor observed that it was incredible how anybody could be smothered there. One would think that even a child of the tender years of the deceased would have had the instinct to have lifted itself up. He expected that the child had been digging at the bottom of the seam and caused a quantity of the soil above to fall upon her: The land in which the pit was situated was bounded by two sets of fencing, the inner one consisting of barbed wire. His reason for coming to that inquest was with regard to the question of the fencing. Colonel Blackburne was greatly troubled by trespassers—people crossing the land in order to take a short cut. Children would persist in playing there—they could not keep them out. —The Coroner: If you had children like me and grand children, you would know what they do when they are about four years of age. Neither police nor fences would prevent them going in.—Mr. Taylor pointed out that Colonel Blackburne had spent endless sums of money on fencing but workmen cut the fences down in , order to cross the land.—The Coroner: That is scarcely ' our business. The Coroner added thatiparents could not be expected to have their children tied to their apron. strings. They bad domestic duties to perform, and of course, the children went out to play. A juryman ob- served that the, children got in between the wires, and all the fencing in the world would not prevent them going i4i. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental I death. The Coroner said if it was his own child he ' should be very sorry but he could not have blamed [anybody.</p>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade in Camp. Sixteen lads from St. Cletnent's Mission, Warring- ton, under the charge of the Rev. A. S. Cameron (chaplain) and Sergeant-Inspector Sweeney (acting as sergeaut-major), were among the 850 youths and 100 officers belonging to the North-eastern section of the Church Lads' Brigade, who were last week encamped between Marske and Saltburn. The boys were drawn from the Newcastle, Durham, York, Ripon, Wakefield, and Liverpool regiments, which are co-extensive with the dioceses.</p>
        <p>Shortly after noon on Thursday,a determined attempt at murder and suicide took place at Pulross Road, Brixton,in a house tenanted by a Mr. and Mrs. Williams. The man, who has for some time been strange in his manner, attempted to cut his wife's throat with a razor, and was only prevented by some passers by. He then rushed upstairs and cut his own throat. His life is des- paired of, but Mrs. Williams's condition is not so serious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Accommodation Difficulty.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Accommodation Difficulty.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. T. Baxter pre- siding. The:e were also present :—Admiral Jones-Parry, Messrs. S. Woodward, T. Wright, C. E. Linaker, J. B. Chadwick, C. Roylance, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, A. G. Guest, G. Pilling, H. Eyes, J. Spruce, J. Mounfield, A. Broome, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, E. Bretherton, C. Horton, A. H. Talbot, J. Bolshaw, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), J. Cunnah (master), T. Cooke, T. Riley and W. R. de Prez (relieving officers.)</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF A NURSE.</p>
        <p>Nurse R. Bottomley wrote tendering her resignation and stating that she had been appointed as hospital nurse at the Prestwich Union Infinnary.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the Visiting Committee the resignation was accepted and it was decided to advertise for a successor at a salary of £3O with rations, uniform, &amp;C. THE EPILEPTICS AND FEEBLE-MINDED.</p>
        <p>The clerk to the Bucklow Union wrote that at the last meeting of his board the following resolution was passed : "that the Board is willing to act in conjunction with other Boards of Guardians in the county in con- sidering the advisability or otherwise of providing joint institutions for epileptics and feeble minded and that a committee be appointed to confer with represen- tatives of other Boards on the matter." Four represen- tatives had been appointed. The Runcorn Board were asked to pass a similar resolution and appoint repre- sentatives.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward proposed that the Board should declare its willingness to act in the matter, and that four representatives be appointed. Mr. Linaker seconded and remarked that he thought the means suggested were the only way of dealing with the cases.</p>
        <p>The motion was agreed to and Messrs. A. R. Norman, T. Baxter, G. Pilling and C. E. Linaker were appointed representatives.</p>
        <p>THE ACCOMMODATION DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that at a special meeting of the Visiting Committee held that morning the question of providing better accommodation for the nurses was considered, and the following resolution was passed . " That it be a recommendation to the Board of Guar- dians that Messrs. W. and S. Owen, architects, War- rington, be requested to inspect the present accommo- dation at the Workhouse, and make a report as to what additions and alterations are necessary to meet the present requirements ; and that Messrs. Fletcher, Spruce, Woodward, Pilling, Morris, and Baxter be appointed a committee to confer with Messrs. Owen at their inspection." The question of extending the water main for fire extinguishing purposes was considered, and it was resolved that Messrs. Timmins and Sons Ltd., be requested to give an estimate for extending the main from the stable yard to the young men's yard, and from thence through the old men's yard to the boys' yard, and for fixing hydrants in the young men's yard and boys' yard. The Clerk said that with reference to the first resolu- tion it would be necessary to give notice to -move its adoption.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Spruce moved the adoption of the recommenda- tion relating to the water main, and gave notice that he would submit the other proposition at the next meeting. Mr. Monks seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker thought the committee to be appointed ought to give the Board some idea as to whether the extra accommodation could be foun4 in portions of the house not at present used, or whether it would be necessary, to erect another building. If he had that information he would be much more willing to appoint Messrs Owen. As it was the Board might go to a great, deal of expense on the preparation of plans and after- wa.r.sls object to the scheme.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris said he thought the feeling was that a separate building should be erected for the accommoda- tion of the nurses.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker said he had always understood that a part of the house now disused could be very easily con- verted into a nurses' home. The Board ought to do all they possibly could to keep down expenses. (Hear, hear.) If it was recommended that a heavy expenditure be embarked upon he should oppose it. Mr. Woodward thought Mr. Linaker's mind would be relieved when heunderstood that the information before the committee was that there was lack of accommoda- tion for they nurses in the house, and there was also a demand by the inspector that the lying- in hospital should be extended. The question that sirose was whether, when the nurses were removed from the main buildings, the portion thus relieved would be convenient to be adapted for the extension of the lying-in hospital. The com- mittee understood not, and the whole thing seemed to be in such a state of indecision and uncertainty that the committee felt they ought to have expert opinion as to what really could he done in the matter, before plans were preoared. For that purpose, the committee was Appointed to meet Messrs. Owen and discuss the pros. and cons. of the question. He for one would oppose any large expenditure in the matter, but he felt in face of the continual changes of nurses that something would have to be done.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker : I quite agree with that. The proposition was agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>In presenting the statement of accounts Mr. Wood- ward alluded to the unfavourable state of the balance. At the last meeting there was an adverse balance of £l3 14s. id. That had now been increased by £146 so that the total deficit was £159 14s. id. It was occasioned, he believed, by an extra call made upon them by the County Council of which they had had no previous inti- mation, and they hoped to restore their solvency before the end of next week. New calls were ready, and pro- bably there would be some payments before the cheques sanctioned that day were presented. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A Mortuary Required.</p>
        <p>This Council held its fortnightly meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. S. Woodward presiding. There were also present :—Admiral Jones Parry,Messrs. G. Pilling, T. Wright, A. H. Talbot, C. E. Linaker, J. B. Chadwick, J. Mounfleld, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, C.Roy- lance, T. Baxter, A. G. Guest, H. Eyes, J. Spruce, J. H. Davies, A. Broome, J. Smith, E. Bretherton, C. Horton, J. Bolshaw, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health),J. Swinton and W. D.iggle (surveyors), and J. Farrington (inspec- tor of nuisances).</p>
        <p>A. DANGEROUS FOOTPATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur de Pr-e-z.,- d;ii", to the Moore' Parish Coun- cil, wrote calling the attention of the District Council to the footpath leading from Moore to Daresbury through the" Eight Acre," which was in a bad state of repair and very dangerous to people using the same. This letter was forwarded to the surveyor who reported that the footpath in question was in the parish of Keck wick. _</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the footpath was entirely through fields, and the matter should therefore be dealt with by the Keckwick parish meeting. The Council instructed the Clerk to reply to the Parish Council to that effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>COMPLAINT FROM WALTON.</head>
        <p>COMPLAINT FROM WALTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ward, clerk to the Parish Council of Walton Inferior, wrote calling the attention of the Council to the bad condition of the road beyond the Walton arches by the river. The Clerk said it would appear that the road was in had condition pretty often, judging from the frequency of complaints. Mr. Swinton was instructed to report on the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Messrs. John White, Mclver and Co., Warrington, wrote asking if the Council had considered the question of supplying water troughs in the fields from the mains recently laid at Preston-o'-th'- Hill. The Warrington Corporation supplied several troughs in Walton and Dareshury, and a fixed stun of 10s. per year was charged. The Clerk was instructed to reply that the Council were prepared to consider the question if any applica- tions were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HELSBY APPLICATION.</head>
        <p>A HELSBY APPLICATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that at the last meeting the, minutes of the Helsby Parochial Committee were sub- mitted, and contained suggestions for the supply of water to the Helsby Cable Works. The Council passed a resolution that the matter be submitted to a special committee, but that committee was not appointed. The suggestion was that the firm should be allowed to take the overflow from the waterworks pumping station for five years free of cost. The matter was referred to the Helsby Sewerage Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SUPPLY OF WATER BY METER.</head>
        <p>SUPPLY OF WATER BY METER.</p>
        <p>The Council fixed the charge for the supply of water by meter in the parishes of Dutton and Aston at Is. per 1,000 gallons.</p>
        <p>THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the members of the -committee met on the site of the new hospital that morning and found that the unfortunate friction which occurred a fortnight ago had been allayed, and things seemed to be running smoothly. The Clerk of Works ,reported that his demand that faulty bricks Scc., should be remedied had been complied with, and, the work now appeared to be progressing satisfactorily. The architect reported that much greater care was being taken with the work, and a better spirit was manifested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright reported that there was a balance in hand at the last meeting of £1033 9s. sd. The receipts during the fortnight amounted to £542 Os.2d. and the cheques drawn that day totalled £1358 Ss. Gd. leaving a balance of .£217 Is. id.</p>
        <p>SEWER EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>The Council accepted "-iK.-3.Tenilei: of Mr. Thomas Stringer at £29 19s. Od. for the extension of a sewer at Appleton.</p>
        <p>THE COMPLAINT FROM MOORE.. Mr. Swinton reported that the London and .North Westorn Railway Company had agreed to cover in the</p>
        <p>Judge Bartlett, of New York, accompanied Mr. Justice Darling on the bench in the Law Courts, London, on Thursday, and was an interested listener to the pro- ceedings in court.</p>
