      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>sewer at the bottom of Mr. Bleckley's garden at Moore the condition being that the Council paid the cost of the labour. This course was sanctioned. HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported that during the fortnight then had been a case of scarlet fever at Stockton Heath, an two cases of diphtheria at Overton.</p>
        <p>MORTUARY REQUIRED AT WESTON,</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Weston Parochial Com- mittee it was resolved that owing to the abnormal number of deaths from drowning at Weston Point it he a recommendation to the District Council that a mortuary (temporary or permanent) be provided. Mr. Farrington reported that he had inspected a building at Weston Point which the Parochial Com- mittee had suggested might be made into a mortuary, if satisfactory arrangements could be made for acquiring it. The building belonged to the Manchester Ship Canal Compaay and was situated near the Runcorn and Weston Canal. At present it was not used for any pur- pose, and in his opinion it could be put into suitable condition for a mortuary from £lO to £l5. The Clerk was instructed to write the Manchester Ship Canal Company to ascertain whether the building could be acquired for the purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON BURIAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BURIAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A Prosperous Year.</p>
        <p>The members of the above society assembled at the British Workman Public House, Horsemarket-street, on Monday, the occasion being the 33rd anniversary of the establishment of the Society. The attendance was good, the meeting being presided over by the President, Mr. Joseph Adams, who was supported by the Vice- President, Mr. Joseph Ashton ; treasurer, Mr. James Parr ; trustees, Messrs. Peter Edgar,William Eckersley ; auditor, Mr. John Jones ; general secretary, Mr. Lawrence Weir, and others.—The Chairman in his open- ing address, alluded to the marked improvement the society had made since the members had settled down to the new name of the society and been free from the annoyance of the continual threats of the officers of the Order to sever their connection or go over altogether to the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds. But through the foresight and tact of their General Secretary and the counsel of the other officers, they had been able to steer the society through the turbulent waters of oppression which threatened at one time to wrest the power out of their hands of dealing with their own affairs. He complimented the members upon the prompt manner the death claims had been met. They were heavier than the previous year but at the same time, the society had made a more substantial gain, and the lapsed membership had been considerably les..—The General Secretary, Mr. Lawrence Weir, pre- sented his annual report which showed that the total worth of the Burial Society amounted to £2862 Bs. Bd., compared with £2661 115.4 d. for the previous year, a gain of £2OO 175.4 d. The numerical strength was 136 1 compared with 1331 last year. In conclusion the ' Secretary pointed out the advantages to be derived from the new scale of benefits to be submitted. Members would be entitled to a bonus after 10 years of 10s., and after 25 years of 11 and a free policy, contribu- tion to cease after 50 years continuos membership. It was proposed to increase the age limit to the 30 years in both sexes. The past year had been one of the most prosperous years they had experienced. The lapsed membership had only been 21, and many of these would re-enter during the year. The auditors reported that the accounts and registers kept by the General Secre- tary and Treasurers' and Collectors Books had been examined and were satisfactory. Mr. Joseph Ashton was elected president, and Mr. Joseph Mather vice-president ; Mr. James Gresty was elected trustee, and Mr. Joseph Adams auditor. The remainder of the agenda consisted in a notice of motion to improve the benefit table, and give advantages to those who had helped to build up the society.—A long discussion took place on the suggested revision of rules.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was accorded the retiring president, Mr. Joseph Adams, for the manlier he had conducted the business during his term of office.—Mr. Adams assured in response that whatever he had done for the society had been a pleasure to him to carry out, and he should always consider he was one of the committee, and should place his services at their command.</p>
        <p>CITY OF BRADFORD gXIIIBITION.—During the August Bank Holiday week a figure of attendances was regis- tered at this most popular resort which has been slightly exceeded only by the record at Whitsuntide, when, of course, the public holiday is much more extended than in August. Much has been said already about the, quality of the entertainments,and it need only ben dded as a proof of the public appreciation of these features, that, owing to the great favour in which they are held, the buildings have had to be extended and seating accommodation added to in all directions. Not suffici- ent attention, however, has been directed to the loan of collection pictures which is a feature of the exhibition. The fact has now come to be recognised that the Cart- wright Hall Exhibition of pictures—illustrating the progress of British Art from the time of Hogarth to the present day—serves its purpose in quite an excep- tional degree. The experts have been and have testified. The most striking testimony is that offered a few Idays ago to the Select Committee of the House of Lords appointed to inquire into the administration of the Chantrey bequest. This bequest was made for the special purpose of creating a representative exhibit of British Art. On July 29th one of the witnesses before the Select Committee alluded to was Count Kessler, Vice-President of the German Association of Artists (Deutscher Kunstlerbund), and he instanced the collec- tion of pictures at the Bradford Exhibition as being decidedly a better illustration of British Art than the Chantrey Exhibit. "Having visited the Exhibition at Bradford," he said, "he had formed an entirely different impression of modern English art than he had got from his inspection of the Chantrey Exhibition. In his opinion many of the pictures enhibited there were so much better than those of the Chantrey collection." This opinion of a distinguished foreign authority is, naturally, valued highly by the Exhibition authorities as a recognition of the very special pains bestowed then upon their collection, and they hope that the British public will not miss an opportunity of seeing an Art collection which calls forth such conspicuous praise.</p>
      </div>
