      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICH</p>
        <p>'0- CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>-- Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Rev. H. Dunnico and His Critics</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Dunnico and His Critics</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,--Will you kindly allow me a short space in which to reply to a letter by.W. H. Bell, jun., which appeared in your last week's issue. In the course of his reply to the Rev. H. Dunnico, Mr. Bell stated that he had been invited and had given a paper at that gentleman's chapel. Does he mean one given at our Bible Class a Sunday or two ago? If so, then he was never invited. But instead of that being the case, he as anxious as ever to air his views, went and asked our pastor to allow him to do so. Having practically little to do with the appointment of speakers, our pastor referred him to me as secretary to the class, and Mr. Bell meeting me asked if I could find him an open date on which he could give us a paper. Being uncertain about having a speaker for the following Sunday I pro- mised to let him (Mr. Bell) know in a few days. Before that, time expired, however, he again met me several times.,and being unable to get any one else for that date, I ultimately gave him my consent to let him come,just to fill the gap. lam making this explanation to show that instead of his services being solicited, which he would have your readers believe, they were placed at our disposal. I wish to inform all who may think other- wise, that he is not a member of any of the institutions connected with our church.—Yours sincerely</p>
        <p>J. H. PEPPER.</p>
        <p>12, Wellfield Street, Warrington. [This correspondence is now closed—ED. Er.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Welsh Quarryman.</head>
        <p>Welsh Quarryman.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l venture to think that the following paragraph rom the Welsh press has a peculiarly pathetic nterest</p>
        <p>"WELSH QUARRYMEN SENT HOME."</p>
        <p>"A party of Welsh quarrymen, including two natives' of Llanberis, Griffith Griffiths, Brynhyfryd,and William John Parry, 8, Well-street,and eight others hailing from Bethesda, departed for the States about six weeks ago, having been engaged by Mr. Thomas Evans, manager of a slate quarry at Rockmart, Georgia. When they reached Philadelphia (about 1500 miles from their destination), they were stopped by the emigration officers. After being detained for a week, the authori- ties decided that the party had gone out under a con- tract, and had, therefore, committed a breach of the Emigration Laws. They were ordered to return home. "The expenses were defrayed by the Rockmart Slate Quarry proprietors." The above facts are vouched for by persons of repute in the neighbourhood. They show how tenaciously the still locked-out Penrhyn quarrymen cling to their hereditary craft, which has come down to them through five generations. Their skill, I venture to submit,.ts a national asset well worth conserving, and my Society has been formed to find them employment at suitable quarries in North Wales, and thus obviate such occ.ur- rences as the above. We are now engaged in prospecting carefully to that end. Will those who feel interested in this project communicate with me at 30, Temple House, Tallis-street, London, E.C.—Yours Stc., C. SEIERIDAN JONES, Secretary. August 9th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Balkan Danger.</head>
        <p>The Balkan Danger.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Before the holiday season iTrings dispersal we venture, with your permission, to remind your readers that the Balkan volcano so far from being extinct is more correctly described as in a state of semi-eruption. The leaders of the insurrectionary movement are diplomatic enough to wait while European feeling de- mands a trial of the "reforms," and it may be that a general rising will not take place this year. But there is apparently no less disorder than last summer ; family honour, life and property are not secure; and another crisis calling for a diplomatic move may arise at short notice. Telegrams which have appeared in the news- papers have demonstrated that the so-called reforms are little better than a sham. This is bad, but the saddest feature for Englishmen is that our Government has incurred the suspicion of concealing their break- down.</p>
        <p>Lotus lookat the facts. n order has been issued forbidding the foreign officers to receive petitions from the inhabitants of their districts, and forbidding the authorities to take notice of any complaints trans- mitted br them. Questioned as to this, Lord Percy stated that the Grand Vizier had informed Sir Nicholas O'Conor that be did not know of any such instructions haQing been issued. in "The Times "of July 13th we read that the naves, or second-class reserves, had been called out. This is a clear vio- lation of the express stipulations of the Murzsteg Pro- gramme, which insisted on the disbanding of these troops, describing thorn as responsible for" the greater part of the excesses and cruelties" committed in the country. In response to a question on July 28th, the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairsreplied that he had no information. Surely it is worth while to apply for some information on this most serious matter.</p>
        <p>We are now informed that Austria and Russia, disre- garding the wishes of the Porte, are proceeding to double their present staff of gendarmerie officers. Such a spirit of independence toward the Turk is welcome, but the danger of allowing these two Powers to arro- gate to themselves the sole right of independent, action cannot be over estimated. Many more officers are needed to make the gendarmerie efficient, and it is to be hoped that the Western Powers will be equally bold. What then is required when the next crisis arrives? The osiginal mistake was the mandate to Russia and Austria-Hungary in February 1903. Even if it was con- venient that they should execute the Reform Scheme,it was not necessary that they should frame it. Their in- terests were obviously divergent from those both of the local nationalities and of the Western Powers. But if we overlook the original mistake, what are we to say of its repetition three months later—the date of the Murzsteg Scheme—when the failure of the mandate policy had been demonstrated by a aeries of massacres? Once more the Macedonians were promised that Europe would do its duty; they were induced to stay their rebellions hand, andl2o villages, for whose devastation the two Powers had kept the field clear, existed for six months on British charity. Lord Lansdowne has gained a name for humanity and energy by giving good (and quite ineffective), advice to the Powers and the Porte and by condescending to support the claim of charitable people to give away their own money by their own agents. We are not ungrateful; but if the honour and the ultimate interest and prestige of Great Britain are to be maintained, a radically different polilv is needed. Humane words prevent no atrocities. What we want is to avoid the effects of the mandate policy by which two interested Powers have been helped to enfeeble the peoples of Macedonia, and to pave the way for a partition of the Balkans between themselves. We want at the first opportunity a proposal of joint action by the Powers— or even by England with France or Italy alone, if others fail—imposing on the Sultan that European control which Lord Lansdowne himself declared to be indispens- able.—We remain, Sir, your obedient Servants, For the Balkan Committee. NOEL BUXTON (Chairman,) W. A. MOORE (Secretary.) 10, Adelphi Terrace, Strand, W.C., August Bth, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>INQUIRY. COUNCIL COMMISSIONERS REFUSE TO ACT AGAIN. • Frivolous Gossip."</head>
        <p>THE INQUIRY. COUNCIL COMMISSIONERS REFUSE TO ACT AGAIN. • Frivolous Gossip."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR has received the following letter from the Council Commissioners on the Municipal Trading Commission in reply to the debate at the Town Council on Tuesday night, when the Mayor wal requested to invite them to continue the inquiry until the end of August:— _ _</p>
        <p>Sir —The resolution of the Council transmitted to us by the Town Clerk shows either lamentable weakness or a not less regrettable inability to realise the position. We presumed it was the wish of the Council that the Inquiry should be thorough and complete. It would appear that we were in errorand that the patient collec- tion and siftinF of the evidence and the declaration of an honest and impartial verdict by us is not acceptable to the majority of the Council. We have faith in the good sense of thq majority of our fellow townsmen, and we are convinced that when thefull minutes of the pro- ceedings are published our action in closing the Com- mission will Se seen to have been amply justified. It having been placed beyond doubt that the allega- tions of corrnption amongst the members and officials of the Corporation which preceded and produced the Inquiry rest on nothing firmer than frivolous gessip,we refuse to take part in the proposed re-opening of the Commission.</p>
        <p>(Signed) THOMAS BTRCRALL. LitiSMCTS GREENING. ALFRED JOHN JOLLEY.</p>
        <p>To the Mayors</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Spark Causes .Death.</head>
        <p>A Spark Causes .Death.</p>
        <p>At the Police Station on Thursday, Coroner Brig- house and a jury inquired into the circumstances attending the death of Charlotte Robinson (22), a cook employed by Mr. Vere B. Davies, Birchdale, Stockton Heath. The evidence was that on Wednes- day the deceased was closing the oven door in the kitchen when a spark flew out of the fire and ignited her dress. She screamed and a coachman rushed in and extinguished the flames with a rug. She was taken to the Infirmary and died the same night.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</p>
        <p>FALL RIGHTS RESERVED.I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search Lights on Land, Labour and Social Problems." THE OSWESTRY ELECTION.</head>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search Lights on Land, Labour and Social Problems." THE OSWESTRY ELECTION.</p>
        <p>"The result of the polling at Oswestry is perhaps the most remarkable in the whole series of bye-elec- tions which have been held since the fiscal campaign was inaugurated, not even excepting that at Norwich, The verdict is a decisive condemnation of the tariff reform movement."—" STANDARD."</p>
        <p>The Tories are only just beginning to realise the I gravity of their defeat at Oswestry. t fell upon them like a bolt from the blue, and staggered and stunned them till they did not know where they were. It is beginning to dawn on them now that they have been badly beaten in a fair and square fight on a plain issue; and they have lost a constituency that was one of their greatest strongholds. While they are mourning over their bad luck, and blaming each other for the loss of the seat, and abusing their opponents, let us have a look behind the scenes and try to learn the lessons of this glorious victory. NEEDED SONIE FAITH AND COURAGE TO ATTACK A CONSTITUENCY LIKE OSWESTRY.</p>
        <p>At the last election, in 1901, Mr. Allan H. Bright fought for the seat against the present Lord Harlech, and was defeated by 1,088 votes. When he undertook to champion the Liberal cause,this time it was felt that he had special claims on his party, and he deserved all the help and encouragement we could give him. Word was passed round that Oswestry was a political Port Arthur that must be captured at all costs. I was invited to join this forlorn hope and make a determined attempt to lower the:flag of Toryism that had dominated the constituency for more than seventy years. But there were serious physical difficulties to be over- come. The constituency is about forty miles long and thirty miles wide. The only town in this vast area is Oswestry, which has about 2,300 electors. Three large villages—Ellesmere, Baschurch, and Gobowen—have about 600 voters each. More than half the electors are scattered broadcast over one hundred straggling villages, and they can only be reached by motor cars or conveyances from Oswestry and Shrewsbury. It required a host of speakers addressing ten or a dozen meetings every night to reach these outlying voters, and lay the issues plainly before them. During the first week of the campaign the Tories re- garded our efforts with mingled pity and contempt. They thought they had a prescriptive right to the seat. Ever since the Reform Bill of 1832 it had been a perfectly safe seat for them, and no Liberal candidate ever had the ghost of a chance of winning it. During the last Itwenty years it had only been contested three times, and the Tories always won with about a thousand of a majority. But our most serious difficulty was the influence of militant landlordism. The people have been taught to pray—</p>
        <p>God bless the squire and his relations, And keep us in our proper stations. They have been made to feel by bitter experience that the man who owns the land can control the people who live on his land. Tradesmen tell how they suffered after the last election because they did not vote as the squire commanded them. The squire, and the parson. and the Tory farmer, withdrew their custom, and induced other people to follow their example. One shopkeeper received notice to quit on account of his vote, but he was fort9nately able to buy a bit of land that did not belong to the squire. He built a new shop of his own and kept his trade together, and now he can afford to defy his persecutor and challenge him to do his worst. The same spirit of intimidation and coercion is used against the - agricultural labourer. His employers ask him how he is going to vote. They tell him that the ballot is no secret, and they can find out how he voted. The Tory young-bloods had the impudence to ask me who I wai, and where I came from, and what right I had to invade their territory, and seek to convert their workpeople and tenants to Radicalism ? And when they could not silence me by arguments, or abuse, or by" booing,"they let loose drunken men into our meetings, and got up dog fights for our diversiom. I have had three dog fights and four drunken brawlers, and half a score of hecklers to deal with in a single speech. So you can take it from me that it needed all the faith and courage we had got to sustain us in that fierce uphill battle. lI.—IT WAS A STRAIGHT FIGHT ON A PLAIN ISSUE.</p>
        <p>We talked about tariff reform and Free Trade and the war in South Africa and Chinese labour and education at the beginning of the campaign but as the fight waxed hotter we narrowed our con- troversy down to Chamberlain's fiscal policy. Oswestry is "within the sphere of Birmingham," and Mr. Bridgenaan, the Tory candidate publicly proclaimed himself a whole hogger, so we were bound to fight that issue to the bitter end. Lying literature was scattered broadcast, and the agents of the Tariff Reform League swarmed like locusts all over the country side.</p>
        <p>Wherever these Birmingham agents went we contrived to be on their track a day or two afterwardiEL But it was dreary, monotonous work repeating the same illus- trations and arguments several times over night after night. I had to talk to grown-up men just as I would talk to boys in school,and make the fiscal policy so plain that the dullest of them could understand it. I happened to use the word "exports" one night, and a drunken man said, "What are exports ?" I said, "They are things you send out. If you go home drunk, and turn the wife and children out of the house, and chuck your furniture into the street, you are exporting." He accepted my explanation cheerfully enough but he said I ought not to be so personal in my remarks when people ask me civil questions. Then I had to explain that imports are things brought in, and that we grow rich when our imports exceed our exports, and we grow poor when we are sending out more than we are taking in. The Birmingham lecturers had been trying to make them believe that the nation is going to the bad because it is receiving too much, and sending too little out, but I soon knocked the bottom out of that. Then we tackled the problem of taxing the foreigner. At a meeting of about tw.) hundred people I offered to buy a new hat for any man who could prove to me that the foreigner ever pays our import duties. This sporting offer brought up two or three Tories to try their luck with me. I asked them who paid the tax on tobacco, and tea, and French brandy, and Hollands gin, and Jamaica rum? When they began to answer these conun- drums they tied themselves into such knots as they little eapected and they covered themselves with ridicule. _ _</p>
        <p>We had to take the Birmingham leaflets one by one, and expose their falsehoods and their fallacies so clearly that everybody could understand them. One of their statements was to the effect that Free Trade had ruined a long list of Welsh tinplate works, and thrown their workpeople out of employment. There is not one word of truth in this yarn. All the firms mentioned in that listare running full time and paying good dividends, except one, and that is temporarily closed for new and improved machinery. Two or three weeks of patient, persistent teaching of this kind gave that constituency such a shaking up as it never had before. The fallacies of Protection and Re- taliation were torn to pieces, and held up to ridicule and contempt. Then our friends the enemy began to lose their tempers and get very angry with us. One of the Birmingham speakers came down from his platform and rushed into the crowd, and struck a young man in the face for asking an inconveni- ent question. At another meeting one of them used offensive words concerning his audience, and there was a free fight. in which the Birmingham men got the worst of it. When our canvass books began to come in, and we were able to tabulate our returns, our spirits rose. We knew that the agricultural labourer was the predominating partner in that constituency, and when we had captured his vote we knew that we were strong enough to win the seat. We kept our oWn counsel, and we worked like Trojans, and the Tories had no idea of the surprise we had in store for them till the declara- tion of the poll. When they found they had lost the constituency, and we had turned an adverse majority of 1,088 into a majority of 385, they were dumb—they opened not their mouths. But what does that victory mean to them and to their party ? It is the death knell of Mr. Chamber- lain's wild-cat schemes of tariff reform, and it is notice to quit to every whole-hogger who has to fight for his seat at the next election. After clearing such a hedge as this we need not jib at anything. The Tory newspapers can read the signs of the times. I The "Morning Post' says: "It would be futile to pre- tend that the result of the poll at Oswestry is not a blow to the prestige of the Government. In many respects, indeed, it is the worst blow sustained since the general election." The "Standard" says: "It is an instructive experi- ence for the promoters of the fiscal agitation. For fifteen months they have been hard at work in the oonntry. yet they have made no progess at all. The facts show clearly that the movement is a colossal failure. It is not very difficult to assemble large audiences of well-dressed ladies and arouse their enthusiasm by eloquent rhetoric. But the issue lies with the voters. and they have not been impressed, except unfavonrably. The campaign has disturbed and alarmed the country and has broken lip the most powerful party we have had for several generations. The sooner it is frankly abandoned the better will be the chances of repairing some of the mischief it has caused." In the sight of such bye-elections as Norwich, and Mid-Herts, and East Dorset, and Devonshire,and Oswes- try, it is amusing to hear the " whole-hoggers "breath- ing out threatenings and slaughtel• against the Conser- vative Free Traders. They are threatening to oppose every one of them at the next election, and drive them from public life, unless they swallow the principles of the Tariff Reform League. You can take this tip from me. They will have to fight fortheir own seats shortly, and they must expect</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 13, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 13, 1904</p>
        <p>to share the fate of Mr. Bridgeman at Oswestry when! the day of battle comes. The country is sick and tired of the Birmingham quack doctor and his fiscal pills, and it will make things uncommonly hot for him and his party whenever it has a chance. I have emerged from a long and strenuous fight at Oswestry with a clear head and a whole skin, and whenever there is a big fight of the same kind may I be there to see it and have a hand in it.</p>
        <p>thii,tender was received from Messrs. Walkers' then I will withdraw, but it is not straight to ask people to tender and then ask for the consent of those people to withdraw it. Every member of this Corporation is as jealous of its honour as I am. That is, I don't think there is a man in this Council Chamber that would forego a business transaction of this sort—get out of it The DEPUTY MAYOR: We don't want to get out of it. We only ask to defer it for further consideration. The MAYOR: The Corporation will lose nothing. If I don't know,' don't know who should. Councillor Prziagwrox observed that if the question of the Licensing Bill had been taken up before the tenders were asked for he would not have objected to that position. The MAYOR: You are quite right. They ought not to have been asked for. They were wrongly construed by everyone who tendered. -Councillor PEMBERTON said neither Messrs. Walker nor anybody else who happened to be the highest tenderers were being treated in a proper way. The MAYOR: I object to that. They are. Councillor PEMBERTON : We are only assuming. No- body knows what compensation you will get under the Act. Some people say you are going to withdraw it. You are going to get more money. The DEPUTY MAYOR : We don't know. The other way—we don't know whether we will get anything at all. Councillor PEMBERTON : We don't want the rate-</p>
        <p>payers to lose. The DEPUTY MAYOR: £2,530. Councillor. PEMBERTON : You know it was subject to the magistrates granting the licence when you adver- tised. My point is only for straight business transac- tion. I don't want to increase the facilities for drink- ing,.but at the same time I don't think the ratepayers of Warrington should pay for it. The ratepayars of Warrington axe paying enough. Surely the rates are heavy enough. This additional burden should not be put on them. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: It is in the interests of the ratepayers of Warrington that I .vant this referred back. There is no immediate hurry about it. Councillor PEMBERTON: But you don't show US how. Are Messrs. Walker going ,to have their tender accepted or not? The DEPUTY MAYOR: I cannot tell you. The MAYOR stated that on the terms that they had placed it, it could not be accepted by the Corporation. If they wanted to enter into a conspiracy for certain purposes—it practically was that—then they could go on with it. He would not be a party to do that. Councillor PEMBERTON: That may be, but we have sold a licence in the same way before. The MAYOR: You have not. We never bought the Green Dragon licence. The conditions werelf they got that licence transferred we gave them £l,OOO for the property. The licence never belonged to the Corpora- tion. COUtiCillOr PEMBEREON said he didn't refer to that. The DEPUTY MAYOR: That is the only one you can refer to. Councillor PEMBERTON thought as a matter of business that the Council should accept Messrs. Walker's tender. The minute ought net to be withdrawn unless better reasons were given. Councillor THORNTON said he opposed the acceptance of the tender at the committee meeting, and he had intended moving or seconding another resolu- tion that night. However, he thought the proposition of the chairman of the committee a reasonable one. He should like to ask the Town Clerk whether if they sold this. for £2,000 or £3,000 it would come upon the rates.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK It would not affedt the rates at all. It would have to go to capital account. Councillor THows-ros said that as the amount would cover a period of fifty or sixty years he did not think the amount was one to cavil at. As to the interests of the ratepayers—they would scarcely feel it. If it went on the rates it would only affect them to the extent of 2Ad. in the 4 for one year. Councillor EVANS said some time ago they had a rather heated discussion about the transfer of a licence. There were people who thought the Council had no right to leave the full power to the magistrates as regarded the transfer of a licence. Whilst the Council had part of the bargain they had a right to say some- thing upon the question of the transfer. If the licence was to be transferred to a new neighbourhood where strong objection could be taken to it,the Council ought to be ableito say whether that transfer should be part of the bargain or not. He understood that if the resolu- tion was withdrawn, they would be practically starting afresh. He supposed something had come to the know- ledge of the Finance Committee that it would not be in the interests of the _ratepayers to make common pro-</p>
        <p>perty at present. If that were so,he was quite agreeable to the withdrawing of the resolution. But he wanted to have the opportunity of discussing where the licence was to be transferred to. The MAYOR observed that the Act under which this</p>
        <p>transfer would take place, the Public Improvement Act, meant that a licence should only be transferred from one house to another in the event of a fire or a public improvement, and should serve the very same district in which it was. And to stretch it any further than that was to risk it being of any value to the Corpora- tion. If they had not got it transferred they did not get their money, and if they started risking they lost their licence perhaps. Councillor LORD said he had been in the dark about the question but he agreed.to the withdrawal. He had heard some very ugly rumours but he did not want to repeat them. Alderman Surrox did not see any objection to the minute being referred back, but he thought it was a great mistake to ask for tenders before the matter was fully considered. It was not a pleasant matter—(the Deputy Mayor: "Hear, hear,")—to have to withdraw it, and he didn't think it was treating Messrs. Walker and Son as they ought to be treated. He objected to any remarks being made at present about giving up this licence. They had no right or authority from the rate- payers to sacrifice the value of this licence, whether it was worth £2,500 or whatever it might be. (Hear, hear.) He objected to statements of that sort until the question had been fully considered. When this matter comes before us again, he concluded, let us understand the pros and cons, and, if it is not offered for sale or the sale is not to be carried out, what the reason is and all about it. The minutes were adopted with the exception of the portion relating to the George Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>STORMY COUNCIL SCENE.</head>
        <p>STORMY COUNCIL SCENE.</p>
        <p>THE GEORGE HOTEL LICENCE. Councillor Maginn and the Mayor. HIS WORSHIP THREATENS TO RESIGN.</p>
        <p>Tuesday's Town Council meeting had not been in progress many minutes before a stormy scene arose. The Mayor (Alderman Bolton) was in the chair and the business under consideration was the recommenda- tion of the Finance and Estate Committee relating to the George Hotel. Tenders having been invited for the purchase of the licence of the George Hotel, condi- tionally upon the magistrates consenting, to its transfer to other premises, the following tenders were received :—Mr. Thomas Halton, £050; Mr. Benjamin Kenyon, £1,000; Mr. William Bolton, £1,500; Messrs. P. Walker and Sons, Limited, £2,550. It was resolved that the tender of Messrs. Walkei', subject to the conditions named in their offer, at £2,550, be accepted, and that on the licence being transferred the premises of the George Hotel be demolished, the site being required for the rebuilding of premises acquired for the widening of Bridge- street.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR proposed the adoption of the minutes with the exception of the resolution re Messrs. Walker's tender, which he moved should be deferred for further consideration, along with the matter of the licence of the George Hotel. It would be in the interests of the ratepayers were the subject not dis- cussed that night—(the Mayor: "Hear, .hear ") because things might happen when the new Licensing Bill came to pass that would make them feel sorry should they express their views that night. Councillor DODDS seconded. He agreed with the chairman of the committee, but his reasons were not reasons that would affect anything that might happen in regard to the disposal of this licence. His reasons were these : That, as a Council elected by the rate- payers, they ought to do nothing to encourage the magistrates to increase the drinking facilities in the town. He felt they had far too many public-houses in Warrington, and it would be a great mistake to take any action which would encourage those irres- ponsible magistrates—or he would leave out the word "irresponsible" and say-the magistrates—to -add to the licences, or even re-place any licence which the Council had the disposal of. It was clear to every- body that, in proportion to the facilities for drinking was the amount of drunkenness and crime attendant on drunkenness. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor MAGI SIC rose to object to the approval of the minutes. For some reason unknown to him a resolution was put on the agenda that they accept £2,550 from Messrs. Walker for the transfer of the licence. He was a bit surprised to find his friend Councillor Dodds who had been such a great stickler for the saving of the rates for the last three years, rising to say that they should lose that large sum. The NIAYOR : How can you lose it, when we only ask for it to be referred back for consideration? There is no loss; in fact, I think you will save it. Councillor MA.GINN : With all due respect 1 submit you have no right to speak or vote on this question. The MAroll sit down then. I will not be insulted by you. Who has been drilling you to come here to-night to insult me? Councillor MAGINN lam not insulting you. are out of place in speaking of this matter at all. The Mivoa Have you been having a consultation with our Town Clerk? .</p>
        <p>You</p>
        <p>Councillor 111AGINN: That is none of your business. The MAYOR: It is my business. Councillor MAGINN : lam a free man.</p>
        <p>The : I shall ask you as members of the Council, whether lam to be allowed to direct the meeting or not. Councillor STorr : Have we any right to discuss minutes that are to be withdrawn. Councillor MAGINIC : You have no right. .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR (heatedly) : He says I h—ave no right to be in the chair. I leave the chair and there's my resignation. The Mayor was about to retire, when he was greeted with cries of " No." The MAYOR (to Mr. Mag7nn ) : Have you come here to insult me to-night,? Councillor MAGISN : Will you allow me to speak? The MAYou : I will allow you to speak but not to insult me. •</p>
        <p>Councillor IVIAGINN : We have heard of the closure business a great deal. The MAYOR: Are you gging to insult me. Councillor MAGINN : I disclaim any desire to insult you. The MAYOR: You have said I have no right to speak or vote on this question. Show me what interest I have financially in this matter. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor MA.GINN : Perhaps I should withdraw that (Hear, hear). Perhaps I should withdraw. The MAYOR : No, I don't think you are honest and generous enough for that. (Cries of" Yes, he is.") Councillor IVIAGINN : Well, I will withdraw. Remember, not through fear of you, Mr. Mayor. The MAYOR : Oh, I don't want any fear from you. I want'you to act as a gentleman, to carry on the business of the meeting as a gentleman and not to insult the chair. Councillor MAGINN : Have I any right to talk on this business? The Mivroa (to the members) : Is it your wish that I leave this chair, (Loud cries of " N0,n0.") Then Mr. Maginn, I will take your withdrawal and allow you to proceed. Councillor MAGINN said they came then to the posi- tion of Peter Walker and Son. He believed the Council asked for the tenders for the sale of a licence. They had a list of the firms that tendered.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR: Yes, but there was no pro- viso. They have only given this £2,550 provided they got the licence moved elsewhere. Councillor MAGINN : They are well prepared to give it. May I beallowed to talk? The MAYOR asked whether Councillor Maginn had not heard the proposal of the Deputy Mayor that the matter should be referred back for further consideration. Councillor MAGINN : In the first instance I say it is an insult to Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, and for some unknown reason to me those people have never been treated fairly in this town. The MAYOR: Who has treated them unfairly ? Whom do ,ou mean by several people? Do you mean the Council have not treated them fairly? Councillor MAGINN : Well, I don't say you have not. The MAYOR: Well, what is the use of throwing these slurs at me. Councillor MA.6 INN : Will you let me continue my speech? The MAYOR: I will if you will not let me have these rocriminations. I understand a little bit behind the</p>
        <p>scenes. Councillor MAGINN: Well, drop that. Continuing, Councillor Maginn said Peter Walker and Son had never been treated very fairly. Some time ago they made an offer for the Feathers Hotel—a good one too. It, was refused by the Council. Afterwards they built a house at a cost of something like £2,300. Messrs. Walker offered £3,000 for the transfer. After the Council had built the hotel they only got £3,800. They mode a good bargain. They refused Messrs: Walker the licence for the sake of reducing drink. They lapsed the Eagle and. Child. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Maginn said Coun- cillor Dodds said "hear, hear," but had the lapsing of that licence, which seemed to be worth £2.500, reduced drink by a single glass in Bridge-street? No. They had made a present of £2,500 to the neighbouring publicans. Then they came to the Green Dragon. Did they lapse the Green Dragon? No. They transferred it to a house in Lovely-lane worth £6OO. The following night' it was worth £O,OOO through getting the licence for it. Now they were told that in the interests of the ratepay- ers it would be well to adjourn this for future conside ra- tion in the hope that they might get more out of the new Act. But the new Act was not in force yet, and he preferred to have one bird in the hand to two in the I bush. If they carried these minutes with the proposal for the withdrawal of this resolution he should take the liberty of proposing an amendment, and he would reserve his right of speaking on it. Councillor PIMBERTON observed that he sympathized with some of the remarks of Councillor Magina in as much as he thought that if there were good reasons for the withdrawal of that minute they ought to be given to the Council. The tenders had been publicly adver- tised for and people had tendered in an honourable and bona-fide way he expected. ft was thoroughly under- ; stood what conditions the were tendering on. They: knew that it was at the option of the licensing ; justices— The MAYOR : Do you want me to answer this ques- tion or to wait for the Chairman of the Finance Corn- mittee. He told you that there were reasons for this being remitted, and the Corporation will not suffer. I That I am certain of. Councillor PEMBERTON: The committee have practi- cally entered into a contract with the highessatyte:ldeesrsersr. The . eMA von: No, they have not. Councillor PEITIIERTON : Nobody can Walker are not in a position to carry out their part of , the contract. If the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee can show me that something has occurred since</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>NEW WARRINGTON INDUSTRY !NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>A NEW WARRINGTON INDUSTRY !NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks, J.P. has been instrumental in bring- inn. to Warrington a new industry. Some time ago the Newall Engineering Company Limited, of London were burnt out of their premises and Mr. Monks took the opprtunity of recommending the removal of the works to Warrington. The suggestion has been acted upon,and the company are now established in commo- dious premises atAthe don's Quay. The firm's produc- tions include measuring machines, limit gauges,micra- meters, and surface plates--specialities requiring skilled workmanship, and remarkable exactitude. The industry is practically a new one in this country but the firm's manufactures are already in daily use in Government establishments and engineering. works. The basis of all modern methods of machining is an exact system of measurements,and in order to secure this there has grown up a demand for measuring machines and limit gauges, which de- fine with absolute certainty the margin above or below the absolutely correct size. The &amp;wall Engin- eering] Company is endeavouring to meet this demand and with a large measure of success. A reporter of the " Examiner " visited the firm's premises yesterday and found a large roam filled with machinery in charge of employees who were engaged in producing the firm's specialities. A measur- ing machine was shown capable of measuring' the twenty-fifth thousandth part of an inch. A single hair can be measured by this machine with the greatest exactitude, and it may be of interest to the reader to lean that a human hair is generally of the uniform width of .0025 of an inch. Mr. J. E. Storey, the manager of the works, is a member of the Engineering Standardisation Committee which is collecting data with the object of establishing the standarisation of gauges, &amp;c., for the engineering trade. A considerable amount of trouble and experi- mental work has been necessary to bring the firm's productions up to the present state of perfection, but the undertaking:appears to have a bright future before it, and as its !success is no-iv bound up with War- rington, it will have many local well-wishers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>Czarina's Accouchement.</p>
        <p>Birth of a Son.</p>
        <p>St. Petersburg, Friday.—The Czarina gave birth to an infant son to-day.—The little son will be baptised Alexis.</p>
        <p>The news of the birth of a son to the Czar will be hailed with delight throughout the length of the Russian Empire, particularly at this dark hour of the country's misfortunes will the event be invested with a significance which will mean much to the great masses of peasantry who regard the Emperor as "Little Father.' The Czarina was Princess AUK of Hesse-Darmstad, daughter of our own PrincessAlice,and since her marriage in 1894 she has presented her husband with four daughters—the Princess Olga (born in 1895), Tatiana (1897), Marie (1899), and4Anfu3tasia (19t1). Had the new arrival been a princess there was a fear that the event might have led to a crisis in the life of the Czar. The birth of a Prince destroys for the time being the right of succession of the Grand Duke Michael, the Czar's brother, who is 25 years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TO-DAY'S PARLIAMENT. -- House of Commons. The speaker took the chair at five minutes past twelve p.m.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S PARLIAMENT. -- House of Commons. The speaker took the chair at five minutes past twelve p.m.</p>
        <p>THE INDIAN BUDGET.</p>
        <p>On the motion to go on the Indian Budget, Mr. Brodrick said although surpluses had been shown for several years it must net be forgotten that the revenues of India were subject to great fluctuation. The revised figures for 1903-4 showed an excess of £1,732,000 over the Budget estimate. During the past year the steady improvement in the Indian financial position had been maintained, and there were encour- aging indications of increased prosperity among the civil population. Sir Henry yowler said the estimates this year showed two million in excess of the expenditure of last year. He could never understand why the Thibet expedition was undertaken, or what gain would follow its success. He opposed any establishment of a British resident at Lhasa.</p>
        <p>Lord George Hamilton advised Mr. Brodrick to economise military expenditure by reducing portions of the native army which were not up to modern standards of efficiency. As to the Thibetans, the best way of dealing with an insolent and stiff-necked clique of monks, intoxicated with a sense of their own sanctity, was to adopt a stiff and uncompromising position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>OPEN AIR AIR CONCERT IN THE PARK.</head>
        <p>OPEN AIR AIR CONCERT IN THE PARK.</p>
        <p>The Proposed War Memorial.</p>
        <p>The novelty of an open-air concert attracted a large' concourse to Bank Park on Wednesday evening, and the programme was gone through under tlie most favourable weather conditions. The object of the concert—a very worthy one—was to swell the fund which the Working- men's Committee is raising in aid of the proposed memorial to the gallant South La,ucashires who fell in the South African campaign. Thanks are due tA3 the members of the Warrington Male Voice Choir for offering their services, and providing a thoroughly enjoyable evening's entertainment. A number of well known choruses were rendered with precision and great expression, the concluding "Soldiers' Chorus" from "Faust" being particularly creditable. Mr. S. Hassall welded the baton with ability and is to be congratulated upon the efficiency attained by the choir. The voices of the Highfield party blended beautifully in the quartettes "Allan Water " and "The Dear Little Shamrock," and the duet" Flow Gently Deva "by Messrs. G. Gough and Hassan was an acceptable item. Mr. E. Pownall gave stirring interpretations of "The Old Brigade "and The Deathless Army "and Mr. J. Corrin acquitted himself well in "Mona." The duties o f accompanist were discharged by Mr. E. Bickers and Mr. F. Atkin. ton. The Mayor, who was accompanied by the Mayoress, presided, and others present included Alder- man Smethurst and Mrs. Smethurst, Alderman Bennett Mr. D. Davies (chairman of the committee), Mr. C. J Newton (hon. secretary), and Mr. H. Hope (hon treasurer.) Several short speeches were delivered and I the choir were cordially thanked for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
