      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington gave a disappointing display on Saturday, and Bootle avenged their defeat earlier in the season by gaining a decisive victory. The conditions under which the match was played were anything but cheerful. A cold wind swept across the ground and occasional showers of rain caused the players to hie themselves to the pavilion for brief intervals. The match was of more than usual interest to followers of the Warrington club, inasmuch as Mason, the Bootle professional, has signed on for Warrington and will succeed Lynes next season. He was unfortunate in his display with the bat,for Lynes bowled him off his pads with an extra fast delivery, but he had the satisfaction later in the afternoon of secur- ing Lynes' wicket and assisting materially in the triumph of Bootle. Warrington were without the services of E. A. Connor (the captain) and J. Übsdell, and their absence no doubt contributed to the second defeat of Warrington this season.</p>
        <p>The Bootle captain won the toss and elected to go to the wickets first. A good start was made, 40 runs being put together by the initial batsmen—H. Williams and J. Sprakeling—before a separation was made. Lynes and Thewlis shared the bowling, but as the latter did not secure a wicket, he gave way to W. H. Podmore at 52. The visitors had now lost two wickets, both having been secured by Lynes. The change in tbe bowling had the desired effect, for Podruore sent T. S. Johnson back to the pavilion, and shortly afterwards he also secured Sprakeling's wicket. From this point to the end of the Bootle innings there was little of interest, the only incident of note being a fine catch by Richardson. The Bootle innings closed for 99.</p>
        <p>Warrington commenced their task with J. W. Bibby and P. B. Hawtin. Bibby batted confidently but he was taken in the slips before he had scored a single. Podmore came next and commenced promisingly. He was, however, smartly caught by Johnson and two wickets were down for 13 runs. Lynes had added seven when he was given out leg before wicket—a decision about which he had some misgivings. Hawtin and Cation made a useful stand, but at 32 Hawtin was caught in the slips by Mason. Warrington's chances of winning were now at zero. A bad mistake cost Wright his wicket, when it was most wanted, and J. Callon followed shortly after- wards, his score being 12. which was the highest on the Warrington side. The remaining batsmen gave little trouble,and the display of the Warrington men with the bat was the weakest which has been seen this season. They were all dismissed for 57. Mason and Smith bowled unchanged throughout the innings and had every reason to be well satisfied with their performance.</p>
        <p>Although half the wickets were down for 35, the second eleven managed to make a draw at Bootle. The home tam compiled 191 and Warrington had knocked up 106 for eight wickets when time was called. To E. Pierpoint and W. Bennett belongs the credit for saving the situation, for they went in at a critical time and made 29 and 25 not out respectively. H. C. Schofield and W. SpeHee, a veteran cricketer, each contributed 35 for Bootle.</p>
        <p>Percy Mason, who will next year succeed J. Lynes as the Warrington profe.ssional,is a Nottingham man, and prior to taking up his position at Bootle he assisted Notts on many occasions. Whilst located in his native shire he played for Notts Amateurs, and many are the capable performances he accomplished for his old club. This season he has already had a most successful run, for besides taking 93 wickets at a cost of under ten runs per wicket, he has made successive scores of 114 and 75 against Rock Ferry and Liverpool respectively.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills fell easy victims to Moore and Daresbury who secured the victory by just a century. G. Wright (54) batted in good style for the winners, W. Evans's 11 being the highest score for the Wire Mills.</p>
        <p>Walker's Brewery 2nd were easily accounted for by the "Guardian" Office who had sound batsmen in G. Burrell (35) and J. Moss (25). J. Tichard was responsible for 23 for the Brewery. Burrell captured five wickets for 20 runs and performed the hat trick. '</p>
        <p>Grappenhall journeyed to Widnes and returned triumphant over the White Rose eleven by an eight majority. There was no particularly high scoring on either side. The second eleven had hard luck at home, the Widnesians Winning by seven.</p>
        <p>There was a well contested game between Latchford Baptists and Lovvton, the Bytists claiming the victory by 10 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Cycling Club held their President's Day last Saturday and at the kind invitation of Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds, twenty-three members journeyed to Penketh House. A start was made from the Chapel, and the route taken to Penketh was via Burtonwood and Bold. The weather was not very favourable, a great gale blowing, and at intervals showers made it necessary to shelter. Penketh was reached soon after 5 o'clock. and the cyclists were received by Mr. and Mrs. Dodds. The party adjourned to the house where a splendid tea was provided. Afterwards the younger visitors took advantage of the lawn tennis ground, while the elder ones contented themselves with a ramble round the gardens and the orchard. At 9 o'clock the party again assembled at the house, and a vote of thanks for the kind way in which Mr. and Mrs. Dodds had entertained the club members was proposed by Mr. S. Blythe and seconded by Mr. R. Graham. Mr. Dodds replied, and said he was pleased to see so many members of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Cycling Club. It gave him great pleasure to entertain them.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cycling Club entertained their esteemed President (Mr. John Naylor) at quaint old Chester on Saturday. The second " President's Day" of the season proved a gratifying success. a party of 37 making the journey. Most of them travelled by rail,the high wind making cycling a somewhat heavy task. A sail up the lovely Dee to Eccleston Ferry was thoroughly enjoyed, and at this picturesque spot an excellent tea was provided by Mr. Partington. After- wards, Mr. D. Davies, on behalf of the members, cordi- ally welcomed the President, and assured Mr. Naylor of the great esteem in which he was held by the War- rington Cycling Club.—Mr. D. Hodge (captain) supple- mented the remarks of Mr. Davies by a few well-chosen words.—Mr. Naylor thanked the members for their kindness, and spoke of his deep interest in the welfare of the club. He (Mr. Naylor) was sorry not to see the hon. secretary (Mr. E. Pickavance, jun.) present, as he had not missed a President's Day before for fifteen years, but owing to circumstances which were unavoid- able he was absent that day. The return journey was then commenced, and Warrington was reached about ten o'clock without mishap.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday sixteen members had a spin to Lymm and indulged in bowls on the green attached to the Plough Inn.</p>
        <p>To-day's run is to the pictnresqbe and richly-wooded Oniton Park, and on QTuesday Cronton will be the destination.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING-UP TIME. To-night 8.45 Sunday 8'43</p>
        <p>Monday 8.41 Tuesday 8.39 Wednesday</p>
        <p>Thursday Friday</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery Club regained their position at the top of the League table on Saturday. They were victorious in two matches and have now a total of 18 points to their credit. The prospects of the team for the cham- pionship are particularly bright, although Bull's Head are giving a very good account of themselves. Sankey Chapel House were defeated somewhat easily by the Cemetery Club, 59 being the margin. There were no exceptionally close contests. and Cemetery won nine of the twelve games.</p>
        <p>Brooklands proved more formidable opponents, but were vanquished by 19 points. The match was notable chiefly for the defeat of J. Berry, of the Cemetery Club, who has been going strong for the individual merit championship. T.Rigby was his opponent,and triumphed by 15-11. There was a keenly fought game between W. Savage and H. Williams, the first named triumphing by a solitary point.</p>
        <p>Penketh Park tried conclusions with Horse and Jockey, and claimed the victory by a. 13 point majority after an interesting match. The closest trials of skill were between T. Hackett and J. Shave ; T. Bretherton and W. Goulden, the first-named players gaining the victory by 15-13.</p>
        <p>The following is the' league table up to date : p. w. L. For Agst. Pts. Cemetery Bull's Head ... 8 ... 5 -.2040 ...1751 ... 16 Horse and Jockey l2 ... 6 ... 6 -.1790 ...1609 ... 12 Chapel House Brooklands Fearnhead Penketh Liberal Club</p>
        <p>To-daV's league matches are : Fearnhead V. Brooklands.</p>
        <p>Cemet,ery V. Horse and Jockey. Chapel House v. Penketh Park.</p>
        <p>The entries for the works handicap have not been nearly so numerous as last year. and naturally enthusiasm does not run as high. Thirty-four games were played on Saturday. To-day the first round will be concluded and the second stage entered upon. Two o'clock is the time for the opening. Owing to business engagements Mr. F. W. Scarisbrick has been compelled to resign the</p>
        <p>........___..-</p>
