      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON WITHDRAW THE TENDER.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON WITHDRAW THE TENDER.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of a letter sent by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited to the Town Clerk on Wednesday : The Brewery, Warrington. 10th August, 1001. Dear sir,—ln reference to our tender for the George Hotel licence, and the proceedings of the Town Council on Tuesday evening, we have decided under the circumstances to place the withdrawal of our tender in the hands of the Council. Yours faithfully, PETER WALKER &amp; SON', LTD. (Signed) J. REED WALKEIt, Managing Director. J. Lyon Whittle, Esq., Town Clerk, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ENGINE DRIVER'S SHOCKING DEATH Remarkable Case.</head>
        <p>ENGINE DRIVER'S SHOCKING DEATH Remarkable Case.</p>
        <p>Last evening Mr. S. Brighouse, Coroner, held an inquest at the Police Station touching the death of John Henry Smith, engine driver, Upper Bangor, who met with a shocking death on the London and North- Western Railway on Thursday. At the outset the Coroner asked if the Company were represented by a solicitor and an official answered in the negative. The death Lad been reported to the authorities at Lime- street.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: They ought to send a solicitor to represent them. I have told them over and over again about it. A solicitor is not only useful, but it shows respect to the memory of the deceased. The Lancashire and Yorkshire Company are always represented by a solicitor. I don't understand the L. and N.-W. Com- pany. You kill a man on your line and you don't send anybody to represent you. The facts of the case as stated by the police were that on Wednesday night deceased left Bangor with a train loaded with empties for Haydock collieries. He " booked off "at Warrington on Thursday morning and he was supposed .to go on•duty again at six in the even- ing when he would have returned to Bangor. About five minutes to six Albert Edward Creswell, foreman at Dallam locomotive sheds,wns proceeding along the line at Dallam and saw deceased iu a reclining position in the four-foot way of the down line. Although Creswell shouted to him the deceased took no notice and was cut to pieces by the London to Carlisle express. Randall Gregory, the fireman on the engine of which deceased had charge, said that on reaching Warrington he and deceased went to their lodgings in Folly-lane. At eleven o'clock deceased left the house saying he was going to the market and witness never saw him alive again. Deceased enjoyed the best of health and witness had never seen him the worse for drink. Albert Edward Creswell, foreman at the Dallam-lane sheds, 12, Morris-street, spoke to seeing the deceased sitting in the four-foot almost erect. Witness was between sixty and seventy yards away from him, and he called to deceased who, however, took no notice. A few minutes afterwards the Scotch express appeared, and witness waved his arms to attract the attention of the driver. The train was travelling at the rate of fifty miles an hour, and could not stop. Witness could not possibly have got to the deceased, who was run over. John Black, the driver of a St. Helens train, said that just before the Scotch express came along, he saw the deceased lying in !the four-foot. He was evidently struggling to get out of the way, and witness thought he had been knocked down by a passing train or had fallen in a fit. Had he stopped his train, witness would not have been of any service as the express was approach- ing. He sounded his whistle and waved his cap to attract the attention of the driver of the express. He succeeded in doing so, and the driver shut off steam but could not stop the train in time to prevent the fatality. A verdict of "Accidental death' was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-08-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Trades and Labour Council.</head>
        <p>Trades and Labour Council.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held in 13old-street Wesleyan School on Tuesday evening, Mr. J. Podmore, jun., presiding.—After some discussion it was decided that the Council should meet in future at 7.30 instead of at 8 o'clock.--Tt was announced that two societies had contributed sums of .g. 1.0 and £5 to the glassworkers strike fund. Happily the dispute has been settled.—Councillor Stott informed the Coun- cil that at the annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Trades and Labour Council he was elected vice-president. This is the first occasion on which a member of the Warrington Trades Council has been elected to an office of the Federa- tion.—lt was descided to ask. Mr. Horrocks to represent the Council at 9, conference on housing reform to be held in connection with the Trade Union Congress at Leeds.—A. letter was read from the Con- structive Temperance Reform Association asking for the Council to arrange for a conference to be held in Warringtou to discuss the questions of the public control of the liquor traffic and constructive temper- ance reform.—Mr. Smith did not think the Council had any right to interfere with teetotal questions. What tIA,y had to deal with were trade union matters.—The Secretary said many Trades Councils were taking up the question which affected the working classes to a very great extent. It was decided that the letter should lie on the table.</p>
      </div>
