        <p>secretaryship, and Mr. W. H. Smalley is undertaking the' duties for the rest of the season. Mr. Scarisbrick- has, been an energetic and capable official, and all who have the interests of the association at heart will regret that he is na longer able to take an active part in its working. A very successful event was the second annual bowl- ing handicap in connection with the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfellows which took place at the Brook-- lands Hotel on Saturday. The weather was fairly satis- factory, and the green in first class condition. A capital entry had been secured, and the games commenced promptly at 2 o'clock. The first round was eagerly witnessed, and the progress made was as follows : First round: James Wrench v. H. Hey, 13-10; E. Bond, v. J. Wilkins, 13-11; G. Whitham v. IV. King, 13-12 ;-- F. Corless v. A. Yates, 13-12. Second round: J. T. Boardman v. R. Holland, 13.11; J. Warburton v. R Cook, 13-11. Third round: W. Jones v. J. ',Elle. - 13-3 ; G. F. Freeman v. W. Kerfoot, 13-7; R. Smith v. J. T. Boardman,l3-4. Fourth raund : F. Wisedale v J. Holland, 13-12; NV. Jones v. R. Smith, 13-2. Fifth round : G. F. Freeman v. W. Cottam, 13-6; NV. Jones v. F. Wisedale, 13-7. Final : G. F. Freeman v. W Jones, 13-6. The conclusion of the games was very exciting, and Bro. Freeman was congratulated on his victory, taking the handsome cup and medal presented by Mr. A. H. Crosfleld. The complete list of winners is as follows :-1, G.. F. Freeman: 2, W. Jones ;3, F. Wisedale ; 4, %V. Cottam ; 5, J. Holland; 6, J. Warburton; 7, J. Taylor • 8, R Smith; 9, C. Glover; 10, G. Whitham ; 11, T. Middle- ton ; 12, A. S. Shaw ; 13, J. Corless ' • 14, NV. Kerfoot ; 15, J. T. Boardman; 16, F. Miller; 17, John Wrench ; 18, R. Cook; 19, A. Cooper; 20, B. Talbot. The donors of the prizes were Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Lodge prize, Messrs. Earle, T. Wadsworth, T. Reddish,. W. Chalmers, R. Sumner, H. Maddock, R. H. Halford, J. Taylor Frank Webster, H. Mather, J. Wrench,. T. P. 'BradsPaw, J. Stanway, H. Singleton, J. Banks, C.. Mounfleld, W. Cottam, P. Brimelow, J. Buckley and J. Avions. After tea, which was tastefully supplied by host and hostess Earle, speeches were made by the Grand Master, Deputy-Grand Master and the corresponding secretary, Brothers T. P. Bradshaw (chairman) and G. F. Freeman (secretary). The best thanks were accorded to the com- mittee,:including Mr. E. Whitham. The annual handicap in connection with the LiberaL Club was played on Saturday,Mr.Henry, Williams carry- ing off the first prize. There were fifty competitors. The following is the complete list of prize winners :- 1, Henry Williams; 2, W. Lee; 3. George Baker ; 4, A. Greenhalgh : 5. F. Rathbone ; 6, E. A. Sanders; 7, C. Nevins; 8, J. Davies • 9, T. Critchley; 10,R. Whitfield; 11, J. Houghton; 12;J. Rudd. Mr. A. H. Croslield has, given some handsome prizes which will be presented at a smoker at the close of the season. It has now been definitely decided that the Warring- ton and District Leagre shall play the onaiing match on the municipal bowling green at Victo, Park. The following letter on the subject was addressed to the- Mayor by Mr. F. W. Scarisbrick, as secretary of the Works Association :—Dear Sir, In view of the opening of the municipal green at Latchford, the Works Com- mittee feel that they have a just claim in asking to take part in the opening ceremony. They do not wish to open the green themselves, but would like to play a match with the Warrington, League, the proceeds to go to some charitable object. They believe it would be a great attraction, as it would mean the cream of the Warrington bowlers meeting for one afternoon. It has been rumoured that the League are going to open the green with a main. If promised, let them have one by all means, but we think the opening ceremony should take place first, and should not be given to any one organization. That our claim is a good one is shown by the following facts :—The Works Association have thirty-one teams representing twenty-six works. They started last year. The League have eight teams, and only started this year. The Works Committee are giving the proceeds of the next two matches (Final and Winners v. Rest) to the Infirmary , and Nurses' Institute. The committee are doing all in their power to work amicably with the league. Hoping you will look with favour on this petition, I remain, yours very faithfully, F. W. SCARISBRIbK. --- Mr. Scarisbrick has received the following reply, dated August 4th :—Dear Sir, I am instructed by the. Mayor to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 12th ultimo and to inform you that after careful con- sideration it has been decided to adhere to the arrange- ment already made, whereby the Warrington League will play the opening match on the occasion of the public bowling green in Victoria Park being formally opened in September next. Yours truly, J. LYON WHITTLE. - --- Last night the Warrington League Committee decided to open the green by playing a match with a team from the Works Association. The teams are to be sixteen' a-side. and the Mayor and Deputy Mayor will be asked to captain them. The League accepted the Cricket Club's terms to play a main on the Arpley Green.' Angling. At Warrington there is a lot of activity in angling circles and some important events to be disposed of - shortly. The Shakespaare Angling Society, have had a very successful match, in the Worsley Canal Fishery at Agden Bridge. At the close it was found that a fair lot of fish had been caught, many going to seven and eight ounces. All the twenty-two rods caught fish, the total weight being over 15111 s. The official list of prize winners is as follows :—Messrs. NV. Ellinson, 21b5.210z.; J. Astill, 11b. 41oz. ; E. Lawton, 11b. 10oz. 14dr.; W. W. Mellitt, 11b. 51oz. ; F. Moores, 11b. 41oz. ; J. Lawton, 11b. lox. 9dr. ; S. R. Lodge, 11b. loz. ldr. . • J. Preston, 12-oz.; J. Riley, INoz. ; J. Buckley, 10oz. 9dr., and W. Whitfield, azloz. Mr. Astill won the gold medal. In the death of Mr. NV. T. Reddish. the Warrington Anglers' Association have suffered a distinct loss. Al- though only recently-appointed assistant secretary, he had shown decided ability for the position. Swimming. The first annual gala in connection with the- Post Office Swimming Club was held in the Corporation Baths, Legh - street, on Thursday afternoon, with the following results: lengths handicap : Ist prize, dinner cruet, H. Inca ; 2nd prize, brief bag, R. Main; 3rd prize, clock, A. Jones. 2 Lengths Handicapl (learners) : Ist prize, electro• plated tea pot, T. Ellison; 2nd prize, preserve dish, Jesse Gray ; 3rd prize, silver medal, F. Atherton. Long Plunge: Ist prize, breakfast cruet, W. P. Gardiner ; 2nd prize, gold centre medal, A. Jones. Neat Dive ;* Ist prize, alarm clock, T. Parkes; 2nd prize, gold centre medal, Jesse Gray. Telegraph Messengers. 2 Breadths Handicap : let prize, silver medal, Sisson ; 2nd prize,- silver medal, Dunn; who won by ane second after swimming a dead heat with Horrocks. Great interest was taken in the gala the large attendance of members applauding every race. The handicapping and judging were ;n the hands of Mr. A. Barlow who acquitted him- ' self admirably and gave every satisfaction. Football. Local enthusiasts will be pleased to hear that the' Warrington Committee have secured the services of Frank Shugars, a well known forward from Penycraig, South Wales. The new player has a capital record, and is looked upon as one of the most promising men in his district. He is twenty-four years of age, scales 14st.- 61b., and is sft. 10in, in height. The forward rank should certainly be strengthened by his inclusion. Several other forwards are being negotiated with,and one additional "capture," at any rate, is regarded almost as a certainty. The team will have their first practice this evening. On Monday week a test match for members of junior organizations will be held, ifl order to see if there are any players likely to be Of assistance to the Warrington teams. That popular half-back Thomas Hockenhall entered' he bonds of matrimony on Thursday, and he Will have the best wifhes of the members of the club and all sue- tporters of the game in Warrington. Fire In Knutsford-road. About midnight on Sunday, Mr: Rosebottom, shoe- maker, 129, Knutsford-road, who had retired to bed, detected a smell of fire and on going downstairs he • found that his stock and fixtures were alight. Ile. immediately raised an alarm, and his family had to escape through the bedroom window. Information was at once sent to the Warrington Fire Station, and Superintendent Turner, with a good number of men," turned out with the engine " Major." They found, however, that their services were not- required, flames haying been extinguished by neighbours. The fire originated under the shop window, but the cause is not known. The damage is estimated at £925, The stock was insured, but not the fixtures. Deaths. M WILLIAS.—JiIIy 31st, at West street, Joseph Williaing aged 54 years. the LAWTON.—August Ist, at Keudrick street, Sarah Jolle' Lawton, aged 41 years. RlGNEY.—AtigriSt 2nd, at Lythgoes lane, Joseph RigneYr ued 54 years. Mmurox.—Auzust 6th, at Knutsford road, Elizabeth Melton, aged 51 years.</p>
